bdf16445 (1 1793066095337 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "8pniyrtk.8p" src "Every verb can run standalone: open the store, answer, exit. For a session that sends many verbs, serve holds the store open and answers over a unix socket. The client finds the socket by the same rule the daemon used to create it, so nothing needs to be told where it is. The daemon's answer is the CLI's answer, byte for byte; both call the same dispatcher, and no verb or answer shape exists in one and not the other, except eval, scope and collect: the daemon does not run them -- sent over the socket, they are answered unknown-verb -- and the CLI runs them in its own program (eval in core.sc as its own child). A plain command never starts a daemon: when none is listening, the CLI starts `core.sc --frame` beside itself as a child, writes it the request frame a daemon would have been sent, and reads the child's answer as it reads a daemon's (the same 32 MiB ceiling, and no answer deadline, so the request waits for the store's lock); nothing is left running after it. A daemon is started by `serve`, or by the MCP shell for the session it serves. Answered in a child, a request meets the in-process route's differences: `describe` adds no template clause, the store's lock is waited for rather than refused `store-busy` after five seconds, the store is read as it is rather than as last published, `--rev` is refused `no-published-state` and `subscribe` `needs-daemon`. `THEOURGIA_LOCAL=1` skips the socket and answers in process even when a daemon is there, for debugging. The daemon answers reads from the state it last folded, and can be one commit behind an outside change: a read that notices a record appended by another process, or a segment a git pull brought in, is still answered from that state and asks for a fresh fold, and the reads after the fold see the change. `query` and `context` are the exception: they make the publication current first. A write is never answered from that state: it is decided on the store as it stands under the lock. While a daemon serves a store, write through it.\n\nThe daemon keeps one query session across `query` and `context` requests: a request over the same publication reuses what earlier requests derived, the session is advanced when the publication moves forward, and a fresh one replaces it otherwise. Every answer is the one a fresh session would give, with one exception: a query whose budget a fresh session would exhaust may be answered, since the kept session has done some of the work already. `serve --session-size <entries>` bounds the kept session (0 keeps none). A store whose directory disappears is drained, and the daemon exits with `(exiting (reason store-gone))`.\n\nThe socket path is a symbolic link to the daemon's private socket, `<socket>.d/<pid>`, in a directory of the daemon's own whose `.endpoint` marker reserves it for that endpoint; a client connects through the link as it always has. A start reclaims what an earlier daemon of the same endpoint left; it is refused `serve-path-occupied` when the socket path or `<socket>.d` is something it does not own, `serve-path-reserved` when its socket's directory is inside another endpoint's marked directory, and `socket-path-too-long` (exit 2, before anything is created) when the path leaves no room for the private name. README's `serve` section gives the namespace's rules, the `<socket>.d.reserve` file that serialises a reservation, and the windows it accepts.\n\n`serve --idle-exit <seconds>` makes a daemon leave by itself after that long with no connection open and no request running; an open subscription is an open connection, and 0 or no option means never. When the window runs out the daemon unlinks its public link first, so from then on a client finds no daemon and sends nothing; for the grace, `--idle-grace-ms` (default 200, at most 5000), it still takes the connections the kernel had already completed, then exits 0 with `(idle-exit (after <seconds>) (refused-accepts <n>) (callback-raised <n>))` if none came. A late connection cancels the exit: the daemon binds and publishes again, logs `(idle-resumed (socket <path>))`, and answers it once the resume is done. A resume that cannot happen drains the daemon: the late requests are answered `draining` and it exits 75 with `(idle-exit (reason resume-failed <cause>))`. Every drain waits for the listener to hand over each connection it accepted, so a connection accepted just before a drain is answered `draining`, or served when its request was already running, never closed with nothing."))
36647285 (2 1793066097260 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "8pniyrtk.3o" src "instance.sexp binds a store to the machine and the directory it was made in, so the first write from a clone is refused rather than accepted into a second copy of the same writer's log. A clone that carries another copy's instance.sexp is refused for the mismatch, as above; a clone without one -- what the .gitignore init writes gives -- is refused `(error refused no-instance (remedy adopt))`. adopt mints a new writer id and leaves the history where it is: the clone keeps every block it was given, and its own records go under the new id from then on. A writer directory copied in from another checkout is read, never extended, while a live writer of this instance is there: a writer is this instance's when its owner.sexp names the `(machine nonce)` this copy's instance.sexp records, so a store holding both its own writer and another checkout's writes as its own, whichever sorts first, and adopt answers `not-needed`. A writer whose owner.sexp cannot be read is not passed over: its write is refused `owner-unreadable`. A request whose writer comes to be passed over while it runs -- this instance's writer copied in, or instance.sexp rewritten, during it -- is refused `(writer-superseded (writer <w>) (by <o>) (remedy reopen))`, and the next request writes as `<o>`. `check` says whose writers the store holds, in four lines that leave its verdict alone: `(writer-owner-foreign <w>)` for a writer whose owner names another instance, `(writer-owner-unverified <w>)` for one whose owner carries no instance field or does not parse, `(instance-absent (remedy adopt))` for a store with owners and no instance.sexp, and `(instance-unavailable (path <p>) (reason <r>))` when instance.sexp could not be read; a writer an adopt retired is not listed. A remount is not a move: the device number is the kernel's for the volume and a reboot can change it, so the device is compared last, and when it is the only field that differs a write takes it for a remount -- instance.sexp rewritten with the live device under the store lock, the change kept in a history of the last eight remounts -- and goes on, with no writer derived or retired; adopt on such a store does the same and answers `(ok (reason remount) (device <old> <new>))`. A copy that keeps the original's inode number on another volume -- a block-level clone of a whole volume, a restore that preserves inode numbers -- is therefore taken for a remount too; to keep such a copy as a store of its own, copy it with a tool that makes new files (`cp -R`), whose inode then differs, and adopt that copy. `check` reports the identity as `(instance (machine <m>) (device <d>) (inode <i>) (nonce <n>) [(remounts ...)])`, and a writer whose highest segment is empty after its last record's segment as `(empty-segment <n>)`, a legal state; neither changes its verdict. Reading never needed any of this -- outline, read, search and eval answer from a fresh clone straight away. Every answer above came from a clone of this site; the outline is abridged and the titles passed to insert are left out, and nothing else is changed."))
ca37f7b1 (3 1793066099183 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "d9l36a5r.40" src "# Changelog\n\n## 1.3.0 — unreleased\n\n*335 commits.* Manteia, the rule language, completed (kinds, stored derived relations, aggregation and stratified negation, incremental advance); structural drafts; a review channel that carries a frozen candidate; the daemon answering from its publication with one session kept across requests, started by the MCP shell and leaving when idle; a store identity that survives a remount; the reducer's pending queue and gates made linear; and the store's git bridge (import-git, publish-landing, the edit tools).\n\n### Manteia, the rule language\n\n- Column kinds for the rule language, checked statically; kind containment normalises both sides; goals dispatched to one handler per kind in three environments.\n- Stored derived relations, declared by a verb (`derive`) and checked where they are used; a resent rule is its replay; recursive relations kept whole; availability by one search over the reach, the shortest through-chain reported; a relation's through-chain built once a table.\n- `not`, `count`, `sum`, `min` and `max` over a goal; stored relations stratified; a form's table named by its occurrence and group; emptiness read goal by goal.\n- `session-advance`: a session advanced to a later state, its positive strata maintained by delete-and-rederive, the auxiliary session's work charged to the advance; each callee collected once.\n- An `and` nested in an `and` is its goals in place; `supply` from any tool with its version.\n- The README's language chapter, construct by construct, its example answers measured.\n- A text-mode Scheme file's library and imports are read from its header through its file holder (`in-library`, `imports` and the new `file-imports`, all external), by a new bounded reader library, `(theourgia reader)`; a `holder` fact relates a code block to its file holder, and `def-for`'s no-library clause holds only within one holder, so a script's own definitions serve its own pieces and nothing else. `uses-name` for a text block of a language with a reader binds what its complete forms bind, `(syntactic (reading forms))`. `context` folds a name's definitions past the first two into a `folded` clause, takes `--names <n>` (repeatable, reported in `asked`), scopes a file holder or any non-code target to the code blocks under it, and takes `--budget min+<t>`; the session trace counts, per call, definitions-index builds, fact builds by relation, preferred candidates by step, and whether the session was reused or why it was dropped.\n\n### Daemon\n\n- `query` and `context` are published reads of the store process: the publication made current first, no replay while the store has not changed; a query after an outside write sees it (the `theourgia` hooks rely on it).\n- One query session kept by the daemon across requests: reused on the same publication, advanced when the publication moves forward, replaced otherwise; `serve --session-size <entries>` (0 disables); `(name-use syntactic)` reported per answer; the one exception to byte-identity with the local route: a query the local route refuses for its budget may be answered by the daemon.\n- A store whose directory is removed is drained and the daemon exits `store-gone`; an absent store creates nothing.\n- A plain command starts no daemon. With nobody listening, the command line starts `core.sc --frame` as a child, writes it the request frame a daemon would have been sent and reads its answer as a daemon's (the same 32 MiB ceiling, no answer deadline, so the request waits for the store's lock), and nothing is left running; a daemon is started by `serve`, or by the MCP shell for the session it serves. A request answered in a child meets the in-process route's differences: `describe` adds no template clause, the lock is waited for rather than refused `store-busy`, the store is read as it is rather than as last published, `--rev` is refused `no-published-state` and `subscribe` `needs-daemon`. A daemon's connection and the child read, check and dispatch a frame through one library, `(theourgia frame)`. A store without its lock file answers `absent` on a plain command; `serve-start-failed` is only the MCP shell's start relaying `serve`'s answer.\n- `search` answers over the state the dispatcher hands it, as every read verb does, instead of opening and reducing the store on each call, so a resident daemon no longer folds the whole store again for every search. Over a connection it answers the publication as it stands, as `read` does.\n- `serve --idle-exit <seconds>` and `--idle-grace-ms <milliseconds>` (default 200, at most 5000, a larger grace refused `too-large`): a daemon leaves by itself after that long with no connection open and no request running. It unlinks its public link first, takes for the grace the connections the kernel had already completed, and exits 0 with `(idle-exit (after <seconds>) (refused-accepts <n>) (callback-raised <n>))`; a late connection cancels the exit (`idle-resumed`), and a resume that cannot happen drains, exiting 75 with `(idle-exit (reason resume-failed <cause>))`. A daemon started with the options names them in its `serving` line.\n- The MCP shell is the opener: it starts its daemon with `--idle-exit` from `THEOURGIA_IDLE_EXIT` (1800 seconds when unset, 0 never; any other value refused `bad-environment` before a start) and `--idle-grace-ms 200`, gives each request one deadline and at most two attempts (a `serve-busy` start retried after 100 ms, a daemon gone before the request's own connect started once more), and then answers `connect-failed` with `(carried-out no)`; a written request is never sent again.\n- The socket path is a symbolic link to the daemon's private socket, `<socket>.d/<pid>`, in a directory of its own whose `.endpoint` marker reserves it for the endpoint. A start reclaims what an earlier daemon of the same endpoint left; it is refused `serve-path-reserved` inside another endpoint's marked directory, `socket-dir-missing` where it would make such a directory, and `socket-path-too-long`, exit 2, when the path leaves no room for the private name. A bind or publish that fails after the store is ready exits `listener-down`.\n- Every drain waits for its listener to hand over the connections it accepted, so a connection accepted just before a drain is answered `draining` or served, never closed with nothing.\n\n### Reducer and admission\n\n- Gated arrivals: an arrival's work no longer grows with the number of standing gates (the square of the standing gates per arrival before, the whole fold the cube).\n- The pending queue indexed by what each record waits for: arrivals wake only their waiters; a seq or dep outside the log's envelope refused at `reduce-apply!`; two copies of one event leave the heap in arrival order.\n- An arrival's work independent of the standing gates (the gates as a table in admission; the notes exact); the declaration tables indexed by name and replayed in linear time; the blocks and the pasts found by id; the block structure maintained, not recomputed; the outline's order total.\n- Admission judges only what an arrival can change; a single record naming a plan is judged again when its plan's binding changes.\n\n### Structural drafts\n\n- A draft can create a file or delete one: `working-new` and `working-delete` beside the block drafts, each with its own version and cut; `commit --working-new <provisional>=<version>` and `--working-delete <holder>=<version>`; the commit makes the holder and its pieces by the construction import-git shares, in one batch with the block drafts, and answers `(created ...)` and `(pieces ...)`, also on a replay. edit-sync turns an added file into a new-file draft and a removed one into a delete draft, refusing paths outside the store route's domain (dot-named, not text, a mode other than 100644, a case collision in the resulting tree) and any live structural draft the export does not account for (`draft-changed`, remedy: export again). A new file is shown by the export only (not by outline, context or eval --working).\n\n### The review channel\n\n- `scope --freeze <dir>` reviews a frozen candidate (the copies hold the drafts' bytes, a `before` section the committed text) and writes no main letter; `--packet` adds the questions; `--roots all` copies the whole tree; `--home <h>` gives the scoped store its own home so the reviewer writes its verdict there. review-results answers `pending` until a verdict exists and refuses an incomplete one by name; sources must include a candidate copy. `collect-into --letter new --map <LANDED.sexp> --to-writer <W>` brings the verdict into the main store after the landing, resolving provisional ids and pieces, and turns the reviewer's proposals into drafts for writer W. Cuts are compared as sets. The mail handler is now contrib/mail-hook.sh (codex-mail.sh still runs it), and contrib/claude-hooks.json runs it for Claude Code with `THEOURGIA_ACTOR=claude`; it exits 1 with a line when its query fails, since a failed query is not an empty mailbox.\n\n### Store tools and the git bridge\n\n- `import-git`: a git range absorbed into the store as committed blocks and a tag; one comparison-domain judgement for publication and import.\n- `publish-landing`: the files a landing changed published from the store as one git commit; `landing-commit` tags the start of a landing; `edit-export` and `edit-sync` turn an agent's file edits into a writer's drafts; dump and restore a writer's drafts; the freeze as one script checking the view its tree came from.\n- `changed-files`: the files whose exported bytes or presence differ between two cuts; `locate`: a file's byte or line range, or one diff hunk, to the blocks holding it; `position`: the local writer's last sequence; a named tag's answer carries the cut it bound.\n- Exports at a past cut (`export-code`, `export-md --cut`), judged against the daemon's publication as a read at a cut is.\n- A draft whose base fell behind says so: `drafts --writer W` names each after its items, `(behind <block> (based-on <v>) (committed <v>))`. `landing-freeze` brings every draft onto the applied cut before it dumps (a draft whose block did not change is moved, one whose block changed is merged by a line-based three-way merge, or the freeze stops with `drafts-behind-conflict` naming every conflicting block), so a record committed after the drafts, a review letter included, no longer leaves every draft to be rebased by hand; `landing-freeze --check <dir>` validates a frozen directory and answers current, refreeze or the conflict, so a landing script can stop before its start tag.\n- `landing-freeze` moves a structural draft behind an unrelated commit onto the applied cut: a new file whose path no live file holds there, or a delete whose file's baseline there is still the draft's `based-on`, is rewritten in place at the applied cut with the same bytes, path and language and a version recomputed for that cut, under one hold of the writer's draft lock and only while it is still the version the freeze judged (a draft written again since is refused `structural-changed`, not overwritten). Nothing is removed first: a refusal or a failure before the rename leaves the entry where it was, a failed directory flush after it leaves the whole new entry at the applied cut, and either way the freeze is refused `structural-move-refused`. `REBASED.sexp` records the move as `(moved <key> (from-cut <cut>) (to-cut <cut>))`. A new file whose path a live file holds at the applied cut, or a delete whose file is gone or has changed there, still stops the freeze with `drafts-behind-conflict`.\n\n### Reading and rendering\n\n- A text-mode code block's src and doc given as text; a src held as bytes renders and is measured as its text; a draft beginning with a UTF-8 byte-order mark commits as its bytes; `whereis` nearest finds a name in another case.\n- `search` over a long query, and the relevant step of `context --for`, cost what the hits cost: a scan drops a block at the first token none of its fields takes and compares characters where they stand, without a copy per position, and the relevant step asks with each token of the text once. On a development store of 17,000 blocks, the context of a 30 KB block went from 420 s to under 4 s, with the same hits, scores and snippets for every query.\n- `context --working` assembles the material over the request writer's working view, the committed state at the drafts' cut with that writer's drafts laid over it, in a fresh query session, never the daemon's kept one. The answer carries `(working \"<writer>\")` after `for`, counted in the budget; a refusal of the view (`writer-required`, `invalid-working-writer`, `working-unavailable`, `working-draft-unreadable`) is the answer, and an id the view does not hold is answered as `read` answers it over that view. Drafts are not changes, so validity and the other hard queries answer the committed rows; the premises of drafted blocks are the overlay's and are not handed to a commit.\n\n### Store identity\n\n- A remount is not a move: the device is compared last, and a store whose directory changed only its device (a reboot can do that) is written as the same instance, `instance.sexp` rewritten with the live device under the store lock and the change kept in a history of the last eight remounts; `adopt` on such a store answers `(ok (reason remount) (device <old> <new>))` without deriving or retiring a writer. A copy elsewhere has another inode and is refused for it as before. The limit: a block-level clone of a whole volume keeps its inode numbers and is taken for a remount; `cp -R` makes new files, and adopting that copy gives a store of its own.\n- `check` reports the store's identity as `(instance (machine <m>) (device <d>) (inode <i>) (nonce <n>) [(remounts ...)])`, and a writer whose highest segment is empty after its last record's segment as `(empty-segment <n>)`, the state an interrupted rotation leaves; neither changes the verdict.\n\n### Log, ffi, platform\n\n- An append keeps its own writer's prefix; the tail check lists segments without a stat per entry; the kept prefix seeds past an older empty segment.\n- `path-stat` and `fd-stat`; a child receives this process's environment on every platform, FreeBSD included; reading the environment fails as a spawn failure.\n- A test-only timing seam on the write path (the wrappers a library of their own, decided at expansion).\n\n### Transport and CLI\n\n- `--help` and `-h` answer the verb's usage and touch nothing; the verb libraries entered on first use; a registered verb handed the dispatcher's helpers; a transport option on the stream's route refused by name; a trace line written whole.\n- Programs compiled as program objects at optimize-level 2.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- `split-suggest` reads Scheme character literals, so `#\\(` opens nothing and a file with one is no longer a single block; a Scheme library's body is cut at its definitions.\n- A `define-values` definition names its formals, so `whereis` finds it as a definition.\n- A marked export whose last body row would be a marker line but for a trailing carriage return, followed by a pad, now escapes that row; such a file decoded as `invalid-padding` before.\n- `context` finds the blocks relevant to a committed text held as bytes.\n- `adopt` on a store with no local writer is refused `no-local-writer`, before any step; it answered `internal`.\n- `write` given only half a baseline names the missing half: `--based-on` alone is refused `cut-missing`, `--working-cut` alone `based-on-missing`, a working parent's likewise.\n- An error raised out of a verb is answered with the dispatch's clause on every route; the command line ended on an uncaught exception.\n- `diff` checks that each replay covers its cut, and is refused `cut-moved` when one does not.\n- An endpoint selector whose field clause held `_` matched nothing, so every link under its relation was refused `relation-endpoint`. `_` now holds for any value of a field the block carries (a contested field holds none), and a new declaration that writes a variable there is refused `selector-value-not-literal`. An earlier declaration with `_` now holds for any value; nothing stored is reinterpreted.\n- The MCP shell refused `tools/list` whenever it carried params, answering \"Pagination is not available\", so a client that sends params with no cursor (Codex does) could not start; it now serves the whole list and refuses only a cursor with a value. `contrib/doorbell.sh` sends the Enter as its own keystroke after the line, which a combined send left unsubmitted in some terminals.\n- A batch of a few hundred inserts under a rule naming `field+` was refused `rule-unevaluable` (`query-budget`, `field+`) before a selector was asked: a write's facts (`kind+`, `field+`, `edge+`, `edge-kind+`) were read by scanning the whole relation and charged its full size on every dispatch. They are now read by their bound arguments and charged the rows matched each time the goal is dispatched; an over-budget join is still refused.\n- A writer directory copied in from another checkout that sorted first was taken as the local writer, so this copy's writes went into another instance's log. The local writer is now chosen by ownership: a live writer whose `owner.sexp` names the `(machine nonce)` of this copy's `instance.sexp` is preferred by every write, `position`, a commit's cursor and `adopt`, and name order decides only when none does; a request whose writer comes to be passed over while it runs is refused `writer-superseded` (remedy `reopen`). The verbs that change drafts refuse a `--writer` owned by another instance (`foreign-writer`, remedy `draft-writer`), and `check` reports `writer-owner-foreign`, `writer-owner-unverified`, `instance-absent` and `instance-unavailable` without changing its verdict.\n- A write refused for the store's identity left an empty next segment behind, and the `adopt` that followed named a prefix in no segment, which `check` then read as damage for ever. A write is now admitted before it cuts a torn tail or rotates, and `adopt`'s prefix names the segment of the last applied record.\n\n### Tests\n\n- The test runner gives fixtures a home of their own: `HOME` is the run's scratch home and `THEOURGIA_HOME` is unset, so no fixture writes the user's machine registry; the test home links the `.rustup` and `.cargo` directories of the runner's own home when they exist, so toolchains that resolve from `HOME` still run.\n- The fixture launcher reads the anchor's process group back instead of trusting an errno: a failed `setpgid` with the group set goes on with a note, another group stops the launch as `ANCHOR GROUP NOT SET`, and only `ESRCH` is `ANCHOR DIED`. Its test-only `--fault-setpgid` gains the `anchor-skips` and `anchor-late` modes.\n- The interleave row of `unreadable-acknowledge-routes` waits for the search's answer on the monotonic clock instead of a count of shell sleeps, which a slow runner stretched past the daemon's hold.\n- Two `context-verb` rows pin a working answer's used tokens against what it rendered, and that its hard queries are asked at the drafts' cut.\n- The change-stream guard masks the device and inode in `check`'s instance clause, which differ between any two store directories, and nothing else.\n\n### Known at this release\n\n- The median start-up, 668 ms over ten runs, is above its 637 ms budget; recorded, with a profiling item open.\n\n## 1.2.0 — 2026-10-11\n\n*156 commits.* Mail and review between sessions of any vendor through a store, rules and relation effects declared as data and judged before every write, a first import cut at an editor's symbols, stale verifications named by `context`, and a run of fixes found by the first two measurements on the real tree.\n\n### New verbs\n\n- `scope <dir> --cut <cut> --roots <id> --for <actor>` makes a store for one reader holding exactly the blocks under the roots as they were at the cut, with a letter copy (`to`, `status`, `cut`, `roots`, `scope`, `baseline`) and one letter in this store; `review-results` reads the reader's verdict and findings back as one datum; `collect-into <letter> --results <datum>` and `collect <dir>` bring them under the letter here, each finding's `about` edge on the original block. A message is a block of kind `doc` with `to` and `status`; reading one's mail is a query. `contrib/` holds the Codex hooks, the doorbell and the reader's AGENTS.md. `THEOURGIA_SOCKET` names the main store's daemon for `scope` and `collect`.\n- `relation <name> --as <kind> [--from <selector>] [--to <selector>]` declares what a relation name does in this store: one of the six relations with an effect, or `nothing`; the lifecycle reads the declarations, and a reason names the relation.\n- `rule <name> --on <kind> [--where <goal>] --must|--must-not <goal>` declares a rule of this store in the query language; every committed write is judged against the rules before it is written, by a rehearsal of the write on a clone of the state, and refused with the rule's name. `--builtin citation-coverage` enables the built-in rule. `template apply` installs a template's rules with its roots; the `project` template declares `cover`.\n- `import-code --symbols <symbols-file>` splits the FIRST import of a file with no marker lines at the symbols an editor collected, into code blocks under its file block; a file that carries marker lines follows its markers.\n\n### New options and clauses\n\n- `context` names a stale verification: for a block in scope whose `verifies` edge's target has moved past it, the notes carry `(stale-verification <m> verifies <c>)` and the receipt holds the target, read through the new query relation `verified-moved`; the human output prints the receipt on one line, as `--wire` writes it.\n- `commit` answers `behind-records`, how far each writer that moved since the writer's baseline is, in records.\n- `check` answers `(undecodable-text ((<id> <field> ...) ...))` naming the blocks whose stored text cannot be decoded; a text block beginning with a UTF-8 byte-order mark is text, and `search` and `grep` read it.\n- `eval` refuses an output limit by naming the option, cuts what a limit carries to the limit, and takes `--under` a library id; `eval --working` refuses a draft on a datum block by name.\n- `set <id> mode datum` from the command line stores the symbol, as `batch` does.\n- A datum block takes no `src` by any intent's spelling or order; a batch is read as a set for the datum rule.\n\n### Changed behaviour you may notice\n\n- A delete's targets include the live far ends of the deleted block's edges, so a rule over a review that cites a result judges the deletion of that result.\n- A field set to the symbol `omit` reads as itself; it was read as absent.\n- The history's order is delivery order, and the comments and the two readers of it say so and agree on every route.\n- A plan another writer made is read as its members are declared: five readers unwrap an `expect` wrapper through one procedure; a revoked plan's wrapped member is restored from its text.\n- The daemon's own repair of a torn tail replaces what it knows of the segment, and records the snapshot only when it is at least the cut's length; what it knew of the segment is kept only when the segment shrank or its new snapshot cannot be read.\n- The transport refusal of the MCP shell names its option, and the tools say they are bound to a store.\n- `def` refuses a name the library already defines.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- `supply` refuses a datum library's file by name instead of answering stale.\n- `state-revoked` compares versions as the strings they are.\n- A write declaration too short for its verb is asked its shape first, instead of being read as a different request.\n- An insert refused for both datum and `src` carries no block clause; `eval` states its busy pool before usage.\n- `check`'s reserved-relation records are sorted by event, the same on every route.\n\n### Test-only hooks\n\n- `THEOURGIA_PROBE=off` starts a test daemon without its timer probe; read only by an injection build, any other value refused at load.\n\n### Documentation\n\n- The README gains \"Mail and review between sessions\" (the mailbox, `scope`, `review-results`, `collect-into`, `collect`, a review round, Codex as a reader, what this does not protect against, where this runs), the `relation` and `rule` sections, the rules' place in `template`, and the environment variable table's new rows.\n\n## VS Code extension 1.1.1 — 2026-10-09\n\n*The extension's own version, `theourgia.theourgos`, published on the Marketplace for macOS on arm64 and x86_64 and Linux on x86_64 and arm64.* Import with Symbols, on a core pinned at theourgia be42914.\n\n### Added\n\n- Import with Symbols: a folder or a source file is imported into the store with the core's `import-code --symbols`, and the first import of each file is split at the editor's top-level symbols into a file block and its code blocks, with no review copy first. The report in the output channel places each new block at its lines, and gives a file the core refused to split with the line and column of the byte it names. A file with unsaved edits stops the command before anything is sent.\n\n### The core it runs\n\n- The core is pinned at theourgia be42914, ten commits after 1.1.0, which adds `import-code --symbols`.\n- The core checks every cut of a first import split at symbols: a cut must fall at the top level of the file, outside its protected prefix (a shebang, a coding line, a byte-order mark), so a symbol that starts inside a body, a string, a block comment or an open bracket refuses that file instead of cutting it. The report names the refusal: `symbols-not-top-level`, `symbols-unscanned`, `symbols-in-prefix`, `symbols-unchecked` or `symbols-no-file`.\n- The extension's unit suite passed against that core on darwin-arm64, darwin-x64, linux-x64 and linux-arm64 (CI run 37816045917).\n\n## VS Code extension 1.1.0 — 2026-10-06\n\n*The extension's own version, `theourgia.theourgos`, on the Marketplace for macOS on arm64 and x86_64 and Linux on x86_64 and arm64.* The core pinned at theourgia 1.1.0.\n\n### Changed\n\n- The extension is Theourgos: package `theourgos` under the publisher `theourgia`, id `theourgia.theourgos`, packages named `theourgos-<target>.vsix`. Its commands and settings keep their `theourgia.` names. A 1.0.0 installed by hand from a `.vsix` built before this change has the id `theourgia.theourgia`, which VS Code treats as another extension: let its pending saves go through, or discard them, then uninstall it before installing 1.1.0, since both register the same commands.\n- The core is pinned at theourgia 1.1.0 (3a4ac93).\n- The setting `theourgia.writer` is removed: each window has written its drafts under a name of its own since before 1.0.0, so it decided nothing. A `settings.json` that still sets it shows an unknown setting, and nothing changes.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- The search view answered \"the store did not answer the search\" for every query that found anything, since 1.0.0. A search now lists its hits.\n- A datum-mode block opened as an empty buffer, and an edit saved through it was answered as saved and never reached the code. It now opens read-only, as the datum export writes its library's file, and a file opened under 1.0.0 on a datum block is refused on save instead of appearing to save.\n- A save the core refuses because its block is a datum block, or because a log segment was replaced under the store's daemon, now says which, and for the second says to restart the store's daemon and save again.\n- Search and the hover's mentions ask the core with `--wire`. When every hit is in a superseded or refuted block, the search view says the words are found only in blocks that are not in force, rather than that nothing matches.\n- The package for macOS on x86_64 passes its unit suite against the core it is pinned to, and 1.1.0 is on the Marketplace for all four targets; 1.0.0 was packaged for Apple Silicon only.\n\n## 1.1.0 — 2026-10-05\n\n*26 commits.* Queries and context for agents, read receipts and write premises, class and validity, a change stream, runners for five more languages, and a measured row for macOS on x86_64.\n\n### New verbs\n\n- `query <goal>` answers every binding of a goal's variables over the committed state at one cut. Its facts are twenty relations the store already answers, combined by nineteen rules kept as data; `query --relations` lists both. A query reads no log, runs no code and writes nothing; one whose new work passes a million tuples is refused, never answered in part.\n- `context --for <id> --budget <tokens>` answers what to read before working on a block, within a budget counted as four bytes a token of the answer as the wire prints it, its cut left out, with a receipt that a commit can be given back as its premises.\n- `subscribe changes <rev> [<token>]` keeps a connection to the daemon and prints one line for each publication: blocks added and removed, fields, positions and parents changed, conflicts entered and resolved, edges added and removed. A commit made outside the daemon reaches subscribers within about a second. The MCP shell does not offer it, and without a daemon it answers `needs-daemon`.\n- `commitments` lists the decisions still owed: a decision is open until a live block implements it (a task only once it is done) or its status is done or dropped. `--drifted` names implementations whose content has changed since the link, unless a later link or edit of the decision has seen the change; status, batch, keywords, class and slug do not count.\n- `init --template` or `--template-file`, `template apply` and `template export` make a store from a template of document roots and relations, or give one to a store; the built-in templates are `project` and `memory`. `tasks` lists blocks of kind task.\n- `names <id>` lists the names a code block uses and the libraries a library block imports; `uses <name>` lists the live code blocks that use a name.\n\n### New options and clauses\n\n- `read <id> --cut <cut>` reads a block at a causal cut, written out or as a tag; every `log` entry gives the event's `cut` and `past`.\n- `read --rev` adds the daemon publication the answer was read from, `(rev <n> (daemon \"<token>\"))`, so a subscriber can apply frames by revision.\n- Read receipts: `(cut ...)`, the state a read was answered from, and `(versions ...)`, the version of each block it shows or lists, at the end of an answer. `read`, `refs`, `commitments`, `tasks`, `names` and `uses` carry both; `search`, `grep` and `whereis` add the versions; `outline` and `reach` carry the cut.\n- `--premises <datum>` on every committed write -- insert, set, move, del, link, unlink, tag, batch, commit, import-code, import-md, def and template apply -- with a list of block versions and query digests, or a `receipt` as `context` prints it. When they no longer hold, nothing is written and the write is refused by name.\n- A block's `class`: observation, inference, ruling, verification or external. The relations supersedes, refutes, depends-on, implements, verifies and conflicts-with decide whether a block is valid, superseded, refuted or needs review. A plain `read` of a block that is not valid adds `(validity ...)`; `search`, `grep` and `whereis` leave out superseded and refuted blocks, and `--all-validity` keeps them.\n- `eval --lang` has default runners for `typescript` (node), `go` (go run), `rust` and `c` (compiled, then run) and `java` (single-file launch). `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_TYPESCRIPT`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_GO`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_RUST`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_C` and `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_JAVA` each replace one with a whole runner. Runners still need `THEOURGIA_RUNNERS=on`.\n\n### Platforms\n\n- macOS on x86_64 has its own measured row, read under Rosetta, and is no longer refused; there `stat` and `lstat` bind their `$INODE64` symbols.\n\n### Changed behaviour you may notice\n\n- When validity leaves out every hit, the human output of `search`, `grep` and `whereis` now prints the `(excluded ...)` line instead of nothing. A script that took empty output to mean no match should read `--wire`.\n- `write` on a block whose mode is datum is refused `draft-on-datum-unsupported`, and `commit` refuses a draft that would put text on one. Both used to answer ok, and the edit never took effect. Change a datum block with `def`; `check` reports a datum block that already carries text.\n- A daemon refuses every later write to a writer's log once the segment it was appending to is replaced by a shorter one -- a git checkout of another branch under a running daemon -- until it is restarted. Restart the daemon after switching the store's branch.\n- The answers of `read`, `refs`, `commitments`, `tasks`, `names`, `uses`, `search`, `grep`, `whereis`, `outline` and `reach` end with receipt clauses. The human output is unchanged except for plain `read` and `reach`, which print whole; a script that compares whole `--wire` answers will see the new clauses at the end.\n- Superseded and refuted blocks are left out of `search`, `grep` and `whereis` by default in a store that links those relations. Ask with `--all-validity` to see them.\n- Starting from source takes about 1% longer: the code the premises check adds is compiled at every start (1.1%, the median of three runs of 40 interleaved pairs); the commitments registry added 0.5% before it.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- The daemon sampled a publication's snapshot after its fold had released the store's lock, so a commit by another process in between could go unseen until something else changed.\n- A retry that completes an interrupted plan -- a commit, an import, a def -- wrote its missing members over what other requests had written to the same blocks since, and answered ok. It now checks first and answers `stale-baseline` when a block has moved. An interrupted import that created a file can now be completed; it used to answer `malformed-intent`.\n- `import-md` replaced bytes that were not UTF-8 with U+FFFD and imported the file changed. A file that is not valid UTF-8, or holds a NUL byte, is now skipped and listed under `(skipped ...)`, as `import-code` does.\n- `read --cut` could answer ok from a smaller cut when a record was quarantined between the cut check and the replay; it now refuses `cut-moved`.\n- A code block whose derived fields raised when shown was answered `(error internal ...)` with no block named; the answer now names it.\n- A value written once that merely had a conflict's shape was read as a conflict by `search`, `grep`, references, `commitments` and `tasks`; a field is contested now only when two or more of its candidates survive.\n- A record definition with a malformed field spec could raise when the store read a block's names; it now binds only its type's names. The name walk's shape rules follow what Chez accepts.\n\n### Documentation\n\n- README corrected against the code, with a new opening line.\n- The agent-memory guide, `docs/claude-code-memory.md`, checked against the code.\n\n## 1.0.0 — 2026-09-30\n\n*225 commits.* The first release: a block-graph store where agents and people write together, with a command line, a daemon, an MCP shell and a VS Code extension.\n\n### The store\n\n- A store is a directory of plain files. Each writer appends to its own segments, and the store can live in a git repository; `init` writes its `.gitignore`.\n- 38 verbs, each with a usage line and a one-sentence description that `describe` answers: init, insert, set, move, del, link, unlink, def, outline, read, refs, reach, search, grep, whereis, log, tag, diff, describe, conflicts, write, restore, commit, drafts, diagnostics, discard, batch, import-md, export-md, import-code, export-code, split-suggest, supply, adopt, check, snapshot, publish and eval.\n- Writing goes through drafts: `write` keeps a draft in the writer's own space, and `commit` installs it; a draft whose baseline the block has moved on from is refused `stale-baseline`.\n- The block-editing verbs, `batch` and `tag <name>` accept a request identity, so a client that missed the answer can send the request again and be told what happened.\n- Code and prose sit in one graph: `import-md` and `import-code` read a directory into blocks (Scheme as data with `--datum`), and `export-md` and `export-code` write them back out.\n- `eval` evaluates one expression against the store in a child process with no filesystem and no network; another language runs with `--lang` where the operator has set `THEOURGIA_RUNNERS=on`.\n- `supply` keeps what an editor computed (signatures, calls, diagnostics) in `<store>/derived/`, outside the log.\n- `publish` receives another store's history one segment at a time.\n\n### The daemon\n\n- `theourgia serve` holds a store open and answers over a unix socket; the command line starts one when none is running, and finds its socket by the same rule the daemon used to create it.\n- It answers through the same dispatcher as the command line. `THEOURGIA_LOCAL=1` answers in process instead.\n\n### The MCP shell\n\n- `theourgia-mcp --store <path>` speaks MCP `2025-11-25` over stdio: one tool per verb routed to the daemon, and `eval`, run as the shell's own child.\n- A core refusal comes back as a successful tool result.\n- Each session gets a writer of its own unless `THEOURGIA_WRITER` or `--writer` fixes one.\n\n### The command line\n\n- `theourgia` is the command; `theourgiad`, the daemon program, and `theourgia-mcp`, the MCP shell, sit beside it.\n- `build.ss` compiles the tree and igropyr into a directory of objects, which start about twelve times faster than source.\n- Every verb takes `--store`, `--actor`, `--socket`, `--wire`, `--req` and `--cursor`.\n\n### Platforms and requirements\n\n- Chez Scheme 10.1.0, 10.3.0, 10.4.0 or 10.4.1, libuv and igropyr. On another Chez version the verbs that print a datum refuse with `unsupported-printer-version`.\n- Platforms with a measured row: macOS on arm64, and Linux on x86_64 and on aarch64 with glibc. On any other platform every program exits 75 with `platform-unmeasured`.\n\n### Published to\n\n- Homebrew: the tap `guenchi/theourgia`, whose formula builds this release with Homebrew's Chez Scheme.\n- npm: `theourgia` 1.0.0.\n- The VS Code extension, at its own version 1.0.0, packaged for macOS on Apple Silicon.\n\n### Known not to work\n\n- macOS on Intel, or under Rosetta: the core has no measured row there and exits 75 with `platform-unmeasured`.\n- FreeBSD 15 on amd64 has a measured row, but the daemon does not start there yet.\n- The machine registry never retires an entry: entries for deleted stores stay in it, marked active.\n- The export verbs take no `--cut`: a historical cut with drafts over it has no export."))
5cb47138 (4 1793066101134 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "8pniyrtk.8r" src "`theourgia-mcp --store <path> [--socket <path>] [--actor <name>] [--writer <name>]` speaks MCP `2025-11-25` over stdio. Create the store with `theourgia init` first, then register the shell once:\n\n```\nclaude mcp add theourgia -- theourgia-mcp --store /path/to/store --actor <name>\n```\n\nOr in a client's JSON configuration:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"theourgia\": {\n      \"command\": \"theourgia-mcp\",\n      \"args\": [\"--store\", \"/path/to/store\", \"--actor\", \"<name>\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nOne tool per verb the shell can carry out -- every verb routed to the daemon, and `eval` -- named `theourgia_<verb>`, each taking `{\"argv\": [...]}` with the command line's own arguments as an array of strings. The shell asks the store's daemon for the catalogue on every call and never caches it, so a verb added to a newer build appears without restarting the server. `theourgia_insert` and `theourgia_write` carry the core's writing protocol in their descriptions.\n\nThe core's answer arrives as S-expression text, byte for byte. A core refusal is a successful tool result, not a JSON-RPC error: `(error unknown-id ...)` is the store's answer to the question asked, delivered with `isError: false`. Only the shell's own failures use the JSON-RPC error channel: a frame it could not parse, a frame past the size limit, or a connection that was made and then lost. A lost connection says the request may have been carried out, because it may have been; a request that reached nobody says it was not. A store condition the shell meets before the verb runs -- a daemon that would not start, a refused or unreadable catalogue -- is a tool result with `isError: true` and the refusal in `_meta.refusal`: read the text, not the flag.\n\n`eval` is offered as `theourgia_eval`. The shell runs `core.sc eval` as its own child, not through the daemon: the store's server never runs user code. The call's optional `stdin` string becomes the child's standard input; without one it reads `/dev/null`. Transport options (`--store`, `--wire`, `--socket`, `--actor`) are refused with `transport-option-in-rpc`; the writer defaults to the session's unless `--writer` is given; a language other than Scheme runs only where the shell was started with `THEOURGIA_RUNNERS=on`; a lost evaluation is a protocol error naming the reason. `init` is not offered, because the store must exist before the MCP server starts.\n\nThe catalogue comes from whichever daemon is serving the store, and a client may read `tools/list` only once per session. After a core upgrade, stop the store's daemon first — the shell's next request starts one from the new core, where a plain command starts none — then restart the agent's session. A session restarted while the old daemon still runs is given the old catalogue and sees none of the new verbs.\n\nThe shell is the opener: with nobody listening it starts the store's daemon, and the daemon it starts leaves when idle. It passes `--idle-exit` with the seconds in `THEOURGIA_IDLE_EXIT` (1800 when unset, `0` for never) and `--idle-grace-ms 200`; a value that is not a non-negative integer is that call's answer, `(error bad-environment (reason not-a-count) (variable \"THEOURGIA_IDLE_EXIT\") (value \"<v>\"))`, and no daemon is started. Two shells on one store share its daemon with no counting, and the one that finds it gone starts it again on its next request. A request has one deadline and at most two attempts: a start that meets a daemon leaving or resuming (`serve-busy`) is tried again after 100 ms, a connect first each time, and a request whose daemon left between its start and its own connect is tried once more; after that the answer is `connect-failed` with `(carried-out no)`. A request that was written is never sent again."))
9f816285 (5 1793066996195 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "d9l36a5r.58" src "`query <goal>` answers every binding of a goal's variables over the committed state at one cut. Its facts are 26 relations the store already answers -- a block's kind, class, validity, version, title and fields, edges, parents and text references, libraries, a code block's file holder and a text file's header imports, definitions, name use, the lifecycle's reasons and states, and search scores -- with 4 tests, and 21 relations defined by the library's 43 rules combine them; `query --relations` lists them, then the store's declared and stored relations. The language is Manteia (below). A query reads no log, runs no code and writes nothing, and one whose new work passes its budget is refused, never answered in part. An answer carries a digest of its rows, which a write can give back as a premise. Through the daemon, `query` and `context` are answered from the daemon's publication, made current first when the disk has moved past it, so a query after a write made outside the daemon sees that write.\n\nA Scheme file stored in text mode takes its library and imports from its header, read by the store's own bounded reader from its pieces joined in order: a code block in no library block is `in-library` its file's library, and a script's pieces see the libraries it imports and no others. A definition in no library serves only the pieces of its own file, the code blocks with the same `holder`. Name use reads such a block as forms where its text reads, `(name-use syntactic (reading forms))`, and falls back to its tokens, `(lexing whole-text)` with a reason, where it does not.\n\n`context --for <id> --budget <tokens>` answers what to read before working on a block, within a budget counted as four bytes a token of the answer as the wire prints it, its cut left out: the blocks it must show, with their roles and validity, the decisions still unsettled for the block, implementations in review and contradictions in its scope, then definitions, evidence, dependents and ancestors as the budget allows. Its receipt holds a premise for every block it had to show and the digests of the five queries that chose them, so a commit given that receipt is refused if any of that has changed; what it adds as the budget allows is not in the receipt. A verification whose target has moved past it is named in the notes as `(stale-verification <m> verifies <c>)`, and its target is held by the receipt. The human output prints the receipt on one line, exactly as `--wire` writes it, ready for `commit --premises`.\n\n`--working` answers over the request writer's working view instead, the one `read --working` reads: the committed state at the drafts' cut with that writer's drafts laid over it, in a fresh query session, never the one the daemon keeps. The answer carries `(working \"<writer>\")` after `for`, counted in the budget. A refusal of the view (`writer-required`, `invalid-working-writer`, `working-unavailable`, `working-draft-unreadable`) is the answer, and an id the view does not hold is answered as `read` answers it over that view. A working view changes block text and the names it uses, not validity: drafts are not changes, so the five queries answer the committed rows. Its premises for drafted blocks are the overlay's versions and are not handed to a commit; a committed answer's are. Without `--working` context writes nothing; with it, it reads the writer's drafts under the writer's draft lock, and for a writer with no draft area it creates nothing.\n\nWhen a name the block uses has more than two definitions left, context shows the first two and counts the rest in `(folded ...)`. `--names <name>`, repeated, enters the definitions of each given name in the block's scope before anything else that is optional, and `(asked ...)` says which are defined only in a block that must be shown or nowhere; for a file block, or any block that is not code, the scope is that of every code block under it. `--budget min+<t>` asks for the smallest budget the request needs plus t tokens, and the budget clause shows the number it came to."))
3555fa5d (6 1793066998095 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "d9l36a5r.40" src "# Changelog\n\n## 1.3.0 — unreleased\n\n*336 commits.* Manteia, the rule language, completed (kinds, stored derived relations, aggregation and stratified negation, incremental advance); structural drafts; a review channel that carries a frozen candidate; the daemon answering from its publication with one session kept across requests, started by the MCP shell and leaving when idle; a store identity that survives a remount; the reducer's pending queue and gates made linear; and the store's git bridge (import-git, publish-landing, the edit tools).\n\n### Manteia, the rule language\n\n- Column kinds for the rule language, checked statically; kind containment normalises both sides; goals dispatched to one handler per kind in three environments.\n- Stored derived relations, declared by a verb (`derive`) and checked where they are used; a resent rule is its replay; recursive relations kept whole; availability by one search over the reach, the shortest through-chain reported; a relation's through-chain built once a table.\n- `not`, `count`, `sum`, `min` and `max` over a goal; stored relations stratified; a form's table named by its occurrence and group; emptiness read goal by goal.\n- `session-advance`: a session advanced to a later state, its positive strata maintained by delete-and-rederive, the auxiliary session's work charged to the advance; each callee collected once.\n- An `and` nested in an `and` is its goals in place; `supply` from any tool with its version.\n- The README's language chapter, construct by construct, its example answers measured.\n- A text-mode Scheme file's library and imports are read from its header through its file holder (`in-library`, `imports` and the new `file-imports`, all external), by a new bounded reader library, `(theourgia reader)`; a `holder` fact relates a code block to its file holder, and `def-for`'s no-library clause holds only within one holder, so a script's own definitions serve its own pieces and nothing else. `uses-name` for a text block of a language with a reader binds what its complete forms bind, `(syntactic (reading forms))`. `context` folds a name's definitions past the first two into a `folded` clause, takes `--names <n>` (repeatable, reported in `asked`), scopes a file holder or any non-code target to the code blocks under it, and takes `--budget min+<t>`; the session trace counts, per call, definitions-index builds, fact builds by relation, preferred candidates by step, and whether the session was reused or why it was dropped.\n\n### Daemon\n\n- `query` and `context` are published reads of the store process: the publication made current first, no replay while the store has not changed; a query after an outside write sees it (the `theourgia` hooks rely on it).\n- One query session kept by the daemon across requests: reused on the same publication, advanced when the publication moves forward, replaced otherwise; `serve --session-size <entries>` (0 disables); `(name-use syntactic)` reported per answer; the one exception to byte-identity with the local route: a query the local route refuses for its budget may be answered by the daemon.\n- A store whose directory is removed is drained and the daemon exits `store-gone`; an absent store creates nothing.\n- A plain command starts no daemon. With nobody listening, the command line starts `core.sc --frame` as a child, writes it the request frame a daemon would have been sent and reads its answer as a daemon's (the same 32 MiB ceiling, no answer deadline, so the request waits for the store's lock), and nothing is left running; a daemon is started by `serve`, or by the MCP shell for the session it serves. A request answered in a child meets the in-process route's differences: `describe` adds no template clause, the lock is waited for rather than refused `store-busy`, the store is read as it is rather than as last published, `--rev` is refused `no-published-state` and `subscribe` `needs-daemon`. A daemon's connection and the child read, check and dispatch a frame through one library, `(theourgia frame)`. A store without its lock file answers `absent` on a plain command; `serve-start-failed` is only the MCP shell's start relaying `serve`'s answer.\n- `search` answers over the state the dispatcher hands it, as every read verb does, instead of opening and reducing the store on each call, so a resident daemon no longer folds the whole store again for every search. Over a connection it answers the publication as it stands, as `read` does.\n- `serve --idle-exit <seconds>` and `--idle-grace-ms <milliseconds>` (default 200, at most 5000, a larger grace refused `too-large`): a daemon leaves by itself after that long with no connection open and no request running. It unlinks its public link first, takes for the grace the connections the kernel had already completed, and exits 0 with `(idle-exit (after <seconds>) (refused-accepts <n>) (callback-raised <n>))`; a late connection cancels the exit (`idle-resumed`), and a resume that cannot happen drains, exiting 75 with `(idle-exit (reason resume-failed <cause>))`. A daemon started with the options names them in its `serving` line.\n- The MCP shell is the opener: it starts its daemon with `--idle-exit` from `THEOURGIA_IDLE_EXIT` (1800 seconds when unset, 0 never; any other value refused `bad-environment` before a start) and `--idle-grace-ms 200`, gives each request one deadline and at most two attempts (a `serve-busy` start retried after 100 ms, a daemon gone before the request's own connect started once more), and then answers `connect-failed` with `(carried-out no)`; a written request is never sent again.\n- The socket path is a symbolic link to the daemon's private socket, `<socket>.d/<pid>`, in a directory of its own whose `.endpoint` marker reserves it for the endpoint. A start reclaims what an earlier daemon of the same endpoint left; it is refused `serve-path-reserved` inside another endpoint's marked directory, `socket-dir-missing` where it would make such a directory, and `socket-path-too-long`, exit 2, when the path leaves no room for the private name. A bind or publish that fails after the store is ready exits `listener-down`.\n- Every drain waits for its listener to hand over the connections it accepted, so a connection accepted just before a drain is answered `draining` or served, never closed with nothing.\n\n### Reducer and admission\n\n- Gated arrivals: an arrival's work no longer grows with the number of standing gates (the square of the standing gates per arrival before, the whole fold the cube).\n- The pending queue indexed by what each record waits for: arrivals wake only their waiters; a seq or dep outside the log's envelope refused at `reduce-apply!`; two copies of one event leave the heap in arrival order.\n- An arrival's work independent of the standing gates (the gates as a table in admission; the notes exact); the declaration tables indexed by name and replayed in linear time; the blocks and the pasts found by id; the block structure maintained, not recomputed; the outline's order total.\n- Admission judges only what an arrival can change; a single record naming a plan is judged again when its plan's binding changes.\n\n### Structural drafts\n\n- A draft can create a file or delete one: `working-new` and `working-delete` beside the block drafts, each with its own version and cut; `commit --working-new <provisional>=<version>` and `--working-delete <holder>=<version>`; the commit makes the holder and its pieces by the construction import-git shares, in one batch with the block drafts, and answers `(created ...)` and `(pieces ...)`, also on a replay. edit-sync turns an added file into a new-file draft and a removed one into a delete draft, refusing paths outside the store route's domain (dot-named, not text, a mode other than 100644, a case collision in the resulting tree) and any live structural draft the export does not account for (`draft-changed`, remedy: export again). A new file is shown by the export only (not by outline, context or eval --working).\n\n### The review channel\n\n- `scope --freeze <dir>` reviews a frozen candidate (the copies hold the drafts' bytes, a `before` section the committed text) and writes no main letter; `--packet` adds the questions; `--roots all` copies the whole tree; `--home <h>` gives the scoped store its own home so the reviewer writes its verdict there. review-results answers `pending` until a verdict exists and refuses an incomplete one by name; sources must include a candidate copy. `collect-into --letter new --map <LANDED.sexp> --to-writer <W>` brings the verdict into the main store after the landing, resolving provisional ids and pieces, and turns the reviewer's proposals into drafts for writer W. Cuts are compared as sets. The mail handler is now contrib/mail-hook.sh (codex-mail.sh still runs it), and contrib/claude-hooks.json runs it for Claude Code with `THEOURGIA_ACTOR=claude`; it exits 1 with a line when its query fails, since a failed query is not an empty mailbox.\n\n### Store tools and the git bridge\n\n- `import-git`: a git range absorbed into the store as committed blocks and a tag; one comparison-domain judgement for publication and import.\n- `publish-landing`: the files a landing changed published from the store as one git commit; `landing-commit` tags the start of a landing; `edit-export` and `edit-sync` turn an agent's file edits into a writer's drafts; dump and restore a writer's drafts; the freeze as one script checking the view its tree came from.\n- `changed-files`: the files whose exported bytes or presence differ between two cuts; `locate`: a file's byte or line range, or one diff hunk, to the blocks holding it; `position`: the local writer's last sequence; a named tag's answer carries the cut it bound.\n- Exports at a past cut (`export-code`, `export-md --cut`), judged against the daemon's publication as a read at a cut is.\n- A draft whose base fell behind says so: `drafts --writer W` names each after its items, `(behind <block> (based-on <v>) (committed <v>))`. `landing-freeze` brings every draft onto the applied cut before it dumps (a draft whose block did not change is moved, one whose block changed is merged by a line-based three-way merge, or the freeze stops with `drafts-behind-conflict` naming every conflicting block), so a record committed after the drafts, a review letter included, no longer leaves every draft to be rebased by hand; `landing-freeze --check <dir>` validates a frozen directory and answers current, refreeze or the conflict, so a landing script can stop before its start tag.\n- `landing-freeze` moves a structural draft behind an unrelated commit onto the applied cut: a new file whose path no live file holds there, or a delete whose file's baseline there is still the draft's `based-on`, is rewritten in place at the applied cut with the same bytes, path and language and a version recomputed for that cut, under one hold of the writer's draft lock and only while it is still the version the freeze judged (a draft written again since is refused `structural-changed`, not overwritten). Nothing is removed first: a refusal or a failure before the rename leaves the entry where it was, a failed directory flush after it leaves the whole new entry at the applied cut, and either way the freeze is refused `structural-move-refused`. `REBASED.sexp` records the move as `(moved <key> (from-cut <cut>) (to-cut <cut>))`. A new file whose path a live file holds at the applied cut, or a delete whose file is gone or has changed there, still stops the freeze with `drafts-behind-conflict`.\n\n### Reading and rendering\n\n- A text-mode code block's src and doc given as text; a src held as bytes renders and is measured as its text; a draft beginning with a UTF-8 byte-order mark commits as its bytes; `whereis` nearest finds a name in another case.\n- `search` over a long query, and the relevant step of `context --for`, cost what the hits cost: a scan drops a block at the first token none of its fields takes and compares characters where they stand, without a copy per position, and the relevant step asks with each token of the text once. On a development store of 17,000 blocks, the context of a 30 KB block went from 420 s to under 4 s, with the same hits, scores and snippets for every query.\n- `context --working` assembles the material over the request writer's working view, the committed state at the drafts' cut with that writer's drafts laid over it, in a fresh query session, never the daemon's kept one. The answer carries `(working \"<writer>\")` after `for`, counted in the budget; a refusal of the view (`writer-required`, `invalid-working-writer`, `working-unavailable`, `working-draft-unreadable`) is the answer, and an id the view does not hold is answered as `read` answers it over that view. Drafts are not changes, so validity and the other hard queries answer the committed rows; the premises of drafted blocks are the overlay's and are not handed to a commit.\n\n### Store identity\n\n- A remount is not a move: the device is compared last, and a store whose directory changed only its device (a reboot can do that) is written as the same instance, `instance.sexp` rewritten with the live device under the store lock and the change kept in a history of the last eight remounts; `adopt` on such a store answers `(ok (reason remount) (device <old> <new>))` without deriving or retiring a writer. A copy elsewhere has another inode and is refused for it as before. The limit: a block-level clone of a whole volume keeps its inode numbers and is taken for a remount; `cp -R` makes new files, and adopting that copy gives a store of its own.\n- `check` reports the store's identity as `(instance (machine <m>) (device <d>) (inode <i>) (nonce <n>) [(remounts ...)])`, and a writer whose highest segment is empty after its last record's segment as `(empty-segment <n>)`, the state an interrupted rotation leaves; neither changes the verdict.\n\n### Log, ffi, platform\n\n- An append keeps its own writer's prefix; the tail check lists segments without a stat per entry; the kept prefix seeds past an older empty segment.\n- `path-stat` and `fd-stat`; a child receives this process's environment on every platform, FreeBSD included; reading the environment fails as a spawn failure.\n- A test-only timing seam on the write path (the wrappers a library of their own, decided at expansion).\n\n### Transport and CLI\n\n- `--help` and `-h` answer the verb's usage and touch nothing; the verb libraries entered on first use; a registered verb handed the dispatcher's helpers; a transport option on the stream's route refused by name; a trace line written whole.\n- Programs compiled as program objects at optimize-level 2.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- `split-suggest` reads Scheme character literals, so `#\\(` opens nothing and a file with one is no longer a single block; a Scheme library's body is cut at its definitions.\n- A `define-values` definition names its formals, so `whereis` finds it as a definition.\n- A marked export whose last body row would be a marker line but for a trailing carriage return, followed by a pad, now escapes that row; such a file decoded as `invalid-padding` before.\n- `context` finds the blocks relevant to a committed text held as bytes.\n- `adopt` on a store with no local writer is refused `no-local-writer`, before any step; it answered `internal`.\n- `write` given only half a baseline names the missing half: `--based-on` alone is refused `cut-missing`, `--working-cut` alone `based-on-missing`, a working parent's likewise.\n- An error raised out of a verb is answered with the dispatch's clause on every route; the command line ended on an uncaught exception.\n- `diff` checks that each replay covers its cut, and is refused `cut-moved` when one does not.\n- An endpoint selector whose field clause held `_` matched nothing, so every link under its relation was refused `relation-endpoint`. `_` now holds for any value of a field the block carries (a contested field holds none), and a new declaration that writes a variable there is refused `selector-value-not-literal`. An earlier declaration with `_` now holds for any value; nothing stored is reinterpreted.\n- The MCP shell refused `tools/list` whenever it carried params, answering \"Pagination is not available\", so a client that sends params with no cursor (Codex does) could not start; it now serves the whole list and refuses only a cursor with a value. `contrib/doorbell.sh` sends the Enter as its own keystroke after the line, which a combined send left unsubmitted in some terminals.\n- A batch of a few hundred inserts under a rule naming `field+` was refused `rule-unevaluable` (`query-budget`, `field+`) before a selector was asked: a write's facts (`kind+`, `field+`, `edge+`, `edge-kind+`) were read by scanning the whole relation and charged its full size on every dispatch. They are now read by their bound arguments and charged the rows matched each time the goal is dispatched; an over-budget join is still refused.\n- A writer directory copied in from another checkout that sorted first was taken as the local writer, so this copy's writes went into another instance's log. The local writer is now chosen by ownership: a live writer whose `owner.sexp` names the `(machine nonce)` of this copy's `instance.sexp` is preferred by every write, `position`, a commit's cursor and `adopt`, and name order decides only when none does; a request whose writer comes to be passed over while it runs is refused `writer-superseded` (remedy `reopen`). The verbs that change drafts refuse a `--writer` owned by another instance (`foreign-writer`, remedy `draft-writer`), and `check` reports `writer-owner-foreign`, `writer-owner-unverified`, `instance-absent` and `instance-unavailable` without changing its verdict.\n- A write refused for the store's identity left an empty next segment behind, and the `adopt` that followed named a prefix in no segment, which `check` then read as damage for ever. A write is now admitted before it cuts a torn tail or rotates, and `adopt`'s prefix names the segment of the last applied record.\n- A working view asked for a writer with no drafts, through `context`, `eval`, an export or the drafts listing, no longer creates the writer's area and lock file: the view looks first, without the lock, for the writer's `working/` or `structural/` directory, and with neither answers the committed state and creates nothing (`read --working` never created it).\n\n### Tests\n\n- The test runner gives fixtures a home of their own: `HOME` is the run's scratch home and `THEOURGIA_HOME` is unset, so no fixture writes the user's machine registry; the test home links the `.rustup` and `.cargo` directories of the runner's own home when they exist, so toolchains that resolve from `HOME` still run.\n- The fixture launcher reads the anchor's process group back instead of trusting an errno: a failed `setpgid` with the group set goes on with a note, another group stops the launch as `ANCHOR GROUP NOT SET`, and only `ESRCH` is `ANCHOR DIED`. Its test-only `--fault-setpgid` gains the `anchor-skips` and `anchor-late` modes.\n- The interleave row of `unreadable-acknowledge-routes` waits for the search's answer on the monotonic clock instead of a count of shell sleeps, which a slow runner stretched past the daemon's hold.\n- Two `context-verb` rows pin a working answer's used tokens against what it rendered, and that its hard queries are asked at the drafts' cut.\n- The change-stream guard masks the device and inode in `check`'s instance clause, which differ between any two store directories, and nothing else.\n\n### Known at this release\n\n- The median start-up, 668 ms over ten runs, is above its 637 ms budget; recorded, with a profiling item open.\n\n## 1.2.0 — 2026-10-11\n\n*156 commits.* Mail and review between sessions of any vendor through a store, rules and relation effects declared as data and judged before every write, a first import cut at an editor's symbols, stale verifications named by `context`, and a run of fixes found by the first two measurements on the real tree.\n\n### New verbs\n\n- `scope <dir> --cut <cut> --roots <id> --for <actor>` makes a store for one reader holding exactly the blocks under the roots as they were at the cut, with a letter copy (`to`, `status`, `cut`, `roots`, `scope`, `baseline`) and one letter in this store; `review-results` reads the reader's verdict and findings back as one datum; `collect-into <letter> --results <datum>` and `collect <dir>` bring them under the letter here, each finding's `about` edge on the original block. A message is a block of kind `doc` with `to` and `status`; reading one's mail is a query. `contrib/` holds the Codex hooks, the doorbell and the reader's AGENTS.md. `THEOURGIA_SOCKET` names the main store's daemon for `scope` and `collect`.\n- `relation <name> --as <kind> [--from <selector>] [--to <selector>]` declares what a relation name does in this store: one of the six relations with an effect, or `nothing`; the lifecycle reads the declarations, and a reason names the relation.\n- `rule <name> --on <kind> [--where <goal>] --must|--must-not <goal>` declares a rule of this store in the query language; every committed write is judged against the rules before it is written, by a rehearsal of the write on a clone of the state, and refused with the rule's name. `--builtin citation-coverage` enables the built-in rule. `template apply` installs a template's rules with its roots; the `project` template declares `cover`.\n- `import-code --symbols <symbols-file>` splits the FIRST import of a file with no marker lines at the symbols an editor collected, into code blocks under its file block; a file that carries marker lines follows its markers.\n\n### New options and clauses\n\n- `context` names a stale verification: for a block in scope whose `verifies` edge's target has moved past it, the notes carry `(stale-verification <m> verifies <c>)` and the receipt holds the target, read through the new query relation `verified-moved`; the human output prints the receipt on one line, as `--wire` writes it.\n- `commit` answers `behind-records`, how far each writer that moved since the writer's baseline is, in records.\n- `check` answers `(undecodable-text ((<id> <field> ...) ...))` naming the blocks whose stored text cannot be decoded; a text block beginning with a UTF-8 byte-order mark is text, and `search` and `grep` read it.\n- `eval` refuses an output limit by naming the option, cuts what a limit carries to the limit, and takes `--under` a library id; `eval --working` refuses a draft on a datum block by name.\n- `set <id> mode datum` from the command line stores the symbol, as `batch` does.\n- A datum block takes no `src` by any intent's spelling or order; a batch is read as a set for the datum rule.\n\n### Changed behaviour you may notice\n\n- A delete's targets include the live far ends of the deleted block's edges, so a rule over a review that cites a result judges the deletion of that result.\n- A field set to the symbol `omit` reads as itself; it was read as absent.\n- The history's order is delivery order, and the comments and the two readers of it say so and agree on every route.\n- A plan another writer made is read as its members are declared: five readers unwrap an `expect` wrapper through one procedure; a revoked plan's wrapped member is restored from its text.\n- The daemon's own repair of a torn tail replaces what it knows of the segment, and records the snapshot only when it is at least the cut's length; what it knew of the segment is kept only when the segment shrank or its new snapshot cannot be read.\n- The transport refusal of the MCP shell names its option, and the tools say they are bound to a store.\n- `def` refuses a name the library already defines.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- `supply` refuses a datum library's file by name instead of answering stale.\n- `state-revoked` compares versions as the strings they are.\n- A write declaration too short for its verb is asked its shape first, instead of being read as a different request.\n- An insert refused for both datum and `src` carries no block clause; `eval` states its busy pool before usage.\n- `check`'s reserved-relation records are sorted by event, the same on every route.\n\n### Test-only hooks\n\n- `THEOURGIA_PROBE=off` starts a test daemon without its timer probe; read only by an injection build, any other value refused at load.\n\n### Documentation\n\n- The README gains \"Mail and review between sessions\" (the mailbox, `scope`, `review-results`, `collect-into`, `collect`, a review round, Codex as a reader, what this does not protect against, where this runs), the `relation` and `rule` sections, the rules' place in `template`, and the environment variable table's new rows.\n\n## VS Code extension 1.1.1 — 2026-10-09\n\n*The extension's own version, `theourgia.theourgos`, published on the Marketplace for macOS on arm64 and x86_64 and Linux on x86_64 and arm64.* Import with Symbols, on a core pinned at theourgia be42914.\n\n### Added\n\n- Import with Symbols: a folder or a source file is imported into the store with the core's `import-code --symbols`, and the first import of each file is split at the editor's top-level symbols into a file block and its code blocks, with no review copy first. The report in the output channel places each new block at its lines, and gives a file the core refused to split with the line and column of the byte it names. A file with unsaved edits stops the command before anything is sent.\n\n### The core it runs\n\n- The core is pinned at theourgia be42914, ten commits after 1.1.0, which adds `import-code --symbols`.\n- The core checks every cut of a first import split at symbols: a cut must fall at the top level of the file, outside its protected prefix (a shebang, a coding line, a byte-order mark), so a symbol that starts inside a body, a string, a block comment or an open bracket refuses that file instead of cutting it. The report names the refusal: `symbols-not-top-level`, `symbols-unscanned`, `symbols-in-prefix`, `symbols-unchecked` or `symbols-no-file`.\n- The extension's unit suite passed against that core on darwin-arm64, darwin-x64, linux-x64 and linux-arm64 (CI run 37816045917).\n\n## VS Code extension 1.1.0 — 2026-10-06\n\n*The extension's own version, `theourgia.theourgos`, on the Marketplace for macOS on arm64 and x86_64 and Linux on x86_64 and arm64.* The core pinned at theourgia 1.1.0.\n\n### Changed\n\n- The extension is Theourgos: package `theourgos` under the publisher `theourgia`, id `theourgia.theourgos`, packages named `theourgos-<target>.vsix`. Its commands and settings keep their `theourgia.` names. A 1.0.0 installed by hand from a `.vsix` built before this change has the id `theourgia.theourgia`, which VS Code treats as another extension: let its pending saves go through, or discard them, then uninstall it before installing 1.1.0, since both register the same commands.\n- The core is pinned at theourgia 1.1.0 (3a4ac93).\n- The setting `theourgia.writer` is removed: each window has written its drafts under a name of its own since before 1.0.0, so it decided nothing. A `settings.json` that still sets it shows an unknown setting, and nothing changes.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- The search view answered \"the store did not answer the search\" for every query that found anything, since 1.0.0. A search now lists its hits.\n- A datum-mode block opened as an empty buffer, and an edit saved through it was answered as saved and never reached the code. It now opens read-only, as the datum export writes its library's file, and a file opened under 1.0.0 on a datum block is refused on save instead of appearing to save.\n- A save the core refuses because its block is a datum block, or because a log segment was replaced under the store's daemon, now says which, and for the second says to restart the store's daemon and save again.\n- Search and the hover's mentions ask the core with `--wire`. When every hit is in a superseded or refuted block, the search view says the words are found only in blocks that are not in force, rather than that nothing matches.\n- The package for macOS on x86_64 passes its unit suite against the core it is pinned to, and 1.1.0 is on the Marketplace for all four targets; 1.0.0 was packaged for Apple Silicon only.\n\n## 1.1.0 — 2026-10-05\n\n*26 commits.* Queries and context for agents, read receipts and write premises, class and validity, a change stream, runners for five more languages, and a measured row for macOS on x86_64.\n\n### New verbs\n\n- `query <goal>` answers every binding of a goal's variables over the committed state at one cut. Its facts are twenty relations the store already answers, combined by nineteen rules kept as data; `query --relations` lists both. A query reads no log, runs no code and writes nothing; one whose new work passes a million tuples is refused, never answered in part.\n- `context --for <id> --budget <tokens>` answers what to read before working on a block, within a budget counted as four bytes a token of the answer as the wire prints it, its cut left out, with a receipt that a commit can be given back as its premises.\n- `subscribe changes <rev> [<token>]` keeps a connection to the daemon and prints one line for each publication: blocks added and removed, fields, positions and parents changed, conflicts entered and resolved, edges added and removed. A commit made outside the daemon reaches subscribers within about a second. The MCP shell does not offer it, and without a daemon it answers `needs-daemon`.\n- `commitments` lists the decisions still owed: a decision is open until a live block implements it (a task only once it is done) or its status is done or dropped. `--drifted` names implementations whose content has changed since the link, unless a later link or edit of the decision has seen the change; status, batch, keywords, class and slug do not count.\n- `init --template` or `--template-file`, `template apply` and `template export` make a store from a template of document roots and relations, or give one to a store; the built-in templates are `project` and `memory`. `tasks` lists blocks of kind task.\n- `names <id>` lists the names a code block uses and the libraries a library block imports; `uses <name>` lists the live code blocks that use a name.\n\n### New options and clauses\n\n- `read <id> --cut <cut>` reads a block at a causal cut, written out or as a tag; every `log` entry gives the event's `cut` and `past`.\n- `read --rev` adds the daemon publication the answer was read from, `(rev <n> (daemon \"<token>\"))`, so a subscriber can apply frames by revision.\n- Read receipts: `(cut ...)`, the state a read was answered from, and `(versions ...)`, the version of each block it shows or lists, at the end of an answer. `read`, `refs`, `commitments`, `tasks`, `names` and `uses` carry both; `search`, `grep` and `whereis` add the versions; `outline` and `reach` carry the cut.\n- `--premises <datum>` on every committed write -- insert, set, move, del, link, unlink, tag, batch, commit, import-code, import-md, def and template apply -- with a list of block versions and query digests, or a `receipt` as `context` prints it. When they no longer hold, nothing is written and the write is refused by name.\n- A block's `class`: observation, inference, ruling, verification or external. The relations supersedes, refutes, depends-on, implements, verifies and conflicts-with decide whether a block is valid, superseded, refuted or needs review. A plain `read` of a block that is not valid adds `(validity ...)`; `search`, `grep` and `whereis` leave out superseded and refuted blocks, and `--all-validity` keeps them.\n- `eval --lang` has default runners for `typescript` (node), `go` (go run), `rust` and `c` (compiled, then run) and `java` (single-file launch). `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_TYPESCRIPT`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_GO`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_RUST`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_C` and `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_JAVA` each replace one with a whole runner. Runners still need `THEOURGIA_RUNNERS=on`.\n\n### Platforms\n\n- macOS on x86_64 has its own measured row, read under Rosetta, and is no longer refused; there `stat` and `lstat` bind their `$INODE64` symbols.\n\n### Changed behaviour you may notice\n\n- When validity leaves out every hit, the human output of `search`, `grep` and `whereis` now prints the `(excluded ...)` line instead of nothing. A script that took empty output to mean no match should read `--wire`.\n- `write` on a block whose mode is datum is refused `draft-on-datum-unsupported`, and `commit` refuses a draft that would put text on one. Both used to answer ok, and the edit never took effect. Change a datum block with `def`; `check` reports a datum block that already carries text.\n- A daemon refuses every later write to a writer's log once the segment it was appending to is replaced by a shorter one -- a git checkout of another branch under a running daemon -- until it is restarted. Restart the daemon after switching the store's branch.\n- The answers of `read`, `refs`, `commitments`, `tasks`, `names`, `uses`, `search`, `grep`, `whereis`, `outline` and `reach` end with receipt clauses. The human output is unchanged except for plain `read` and `reach`, which print whole; a script that compares whole `--wire` answers will see the new clauses at the end.\n- Superseded and refuted blocks are left out of `search`, `grep` and `whereis` by default in a store that links those relations. Ask with `--all-validity` to see them.\n- Starting from source takes about 1% longer: the code the premises check adds is compiled at every start (1.1%, the median of three runs of 40 interleaved pairs); the commitments registry added 0.5% before it.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- The daemon sampled a publication's snapshot after its fold had released the store's lock, so a commit by another process in between could go unseen until something else changed.\n- A retry that completes an interrupted plan -- a commit, an import, a def -- wrote its missing members over what other requests had written to the same blocks since, and answered ok. It now checks first and answers `stale-baseline` when a block has moved. An interrupted import that created a file can now be completed; it used to answer `malformed-intent`.\n- `import-md` replaced bytes that were not UTF-8 with U+FFFD and imported the file changed. A file that is not valid UTF-8, or holds a NUL byte, is now skipped and listed under `(skipped ...)`, as `import-code` does.\n- `read --cut` could answer ok from a smaller cut when a record was quarantined between the cut check and the replay; it now refuses `cut-moved`.\n- A code block whose derived fields raised when shown was answered `(error internal ...)` with no block named; the answer now names it.\n- A value written once that merely had a conflict's shape was read as a conflict by `search`, `grep`, references, `commitments` and `tasks`; a field is contested now only when two or more of its candidates survive.\n- A record definition with a malformed field spec could raise when the store read a block's names; it now binds only its type's names. The name walk's shape rules follow what Chez accepts.\n\n### Documentation\n\n- README corrected against the code, with a new opening line.\n- The agent-memory guide, `docs/claude-code-memory.md`, checked against the code.\n\n## 1.0.0 — 2026-09-30\n\n*225 commits.* The first release: a block-graph store where agents and people write together, with a command line, a daemon, an MCP shell and a VS Code extension.\n\n### The store\n\n- A store is a directory of plain files. Each writer appends to its own segments, and the store can live in a git repository; `init` writes its `.gitignore`.\n- 38 verbs, each with a usage line and a one-sentence description that `describe` answers: init, insert, set, move, del, link, unlink, def, outline, read, refs, reach, search, grep, whereis, log, tag, diff, describe, conflicts, write, restore, commit, drafts, diagnostics, discard, batch, import-md, export-md, import-code, export-code, split-suggest, supply, adopt, check, snapshot, publish and eval.\n- Writing goes through drafts: `write` keeps a draft in the writer's own space, and `commit` installs it; a draft whose baseline the block has moved on from is refused `stale-baseline`.\n- The block-editing verbs, `batch` and `tag <name>` accept a request identity, so a client that missed the answer can send the request again and be told what happened.\n- Code and prose sit in one graph: `import-md` and `import-code` read a directory into blocks (Scheme as data with `--datum`), and `export-md` and `export-code` write them back out.\n- `eval` evaluates one expression against the store in a child process with no filesystem and no network; another language runs with `--lang` where the operator has set `THEOURGIA_RUNNERS=on`.\n- `supply` keeps what an editor computed (signatures, calls, diagnostics) in `<store>/derived/`, outside the log.\n- `publish` receives another store's history one segment at a time.\n\n### The daemon\n\n- `theourgia serve` holds a store open and answers over a unix socket; the command line starts one when none is running, and finds its socket by the same rule the daemon used to create it.\n- It answers through the same dispatcher as the command line. `THEOURGIA_LOCAL=1` answers in process instead.\n\n### The MCP shell\n\n- `theourgia-mcp --store <path>` speaks MCP `2025-11-25` over stdio: one tool per verb routed to the daemon, and `eval`, run as the shell's own child.\n- A core refusal comes back as a successful tool result.\n- Each session gets a writer of its own unless `THEOURGIA_WRITER` or `--writer` fixes one.\n\n### The command line\n\n- `theourgia` is the command; `theourgiad`, the daemon program, and `theourgia-mcp`, the MCP shell, sit beside it.\n- `build.ss` compiles the tree and igropyr into a directory of objects, which start about twelve times faster than source.\n- Every verb takes `--store`, `--actor`, `--socket`, `--wire`, `--req` and `--cursor`.\n\n### Platforms and requirements\n\n- Chez Scheme 10.1.0, 10.3.0, 10.4.0 or 10.4.1, libuv and igropyr. On another Chez version the verbs that print a datum refuse with `unsupported-printer-version`.\n- Platforms with a measured row: macOS on arm64, and Linux on x86_64 and on aarch64 with glibc. On any other platform every program exits 75 with `platform-unmeasured`.\n\n### Published to\n\n- Homebrew: the tap `guenchi/theourgia`, whose formula builds this release with Homebrew's Chez Scheme.\n- npm: `theourgia` 1.0.0.\n- The VS Code extension, at its own version 1.0.0, packaged for macOS on Apple Silicon.\n\n### Known not to work\n\n- macOS on Intel, or under Rosetta: the core has no measured row there and exits 75 with `platform-unmeasured`.\n- FreeBSD 15 on amd64 has a measured row, but the daemon does not start there yet.\n- The machine registry never retires an entry: entries for deleted stores stay in it, marked active.\n- The export verbs take no `--cut`: a historical cut with drafts over it has no export."))
86ba9025 (7 1793164944541 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "d9l36a5r.40" src "# Changelog\n\n## 1.3.0 — 2026-10-28\n\n*336 commits.* Manteia, the rule language, completed (kinds, stored derived relations, aggregation and stratified negation, incremental advance); structural drafts; a review channel that carries a frozen candidate; the daemon answering from its publication with one session kept across requests, started by the MCP shell and leaving when idle; a store identity that survives a remount; the reducer's pending queue and gates made linear; and the store's git bridge (import-git, publish-landing, the edit tools).\n\n### Manteia, the rule language\n\n- Column kinds for the rule language, checked statically; kind containment normalises both sides; goals dispatched to one handler per kind in three environments.\n- Stored derived relations, declared by a verb (`derive`) and checked where they are used; a resent rule is its replay; recursive relations kept whole; availability by one search over the reach, the shortest through-chain reported; a relation's through-chain built once a table.\n- `not`, `count`, `sum`, `min` and `max` over a goal; stored relations stratified; a form's table named by its occurrence and group; emptiness read goal by goal.\n- `session-advance`: a session advanced to a later state, its positive strata maintained by delete-and-rederive, the auxiliary session's work charged to the advance; each callee collected once.\n- An `and` nested in an `and` is its goals in place; `supply` from any tool with its version.\n- The README's language chapter, construct by construct, its example answers measured.\n- A text-mode Scheme file's library and imports are read from its header through its file holder (`in-library`, `imports` and the new `file-imports`, all external), by a new bounded reader library, `(theourgia reader)`; a `holder` fact relates a code block to its file holder, and `def-for`'s no-library clause holds only within one holder, so a script's own definitions serve its own pieces and nothing else. `uses-name` for a text block of a language with a reader binds what its complete forms bind, `(syntactic (reading forms))`. `context` folds a name's definitions past the first two into a `folded` clause, takes `--names <n>` (repeatable, reported in `asked`), scopes a file holder or any non-code target to the code blocks under it, and takes `--budget min+<t>`; the session trace counts, per call, definitions-index builds, fact builds by relation, preferred candidates by step, and whether the session was reused or why it was dropped.\n\n### Daemon\n\n- `query` and `context` are published reads of the store process: the publication made current first, no replay while the store has not changed; a query after an outside write sees it (the `theourgia` hooks rely on it).\n- One query session kept by the daemon across requests: reused on the same publication, advanced when the publication moves forward, replaced otherwise; `serve --session-size <entries>` (0 disables); `(name-use syntactic)` reported per answer; the one exception to byte-identity with the local route: a query the local route refuses for its budget may be answered by the daemon.\n- A store whose directory is removed is drained and the daemon exits `store-gone`; an absent store creates nothing.\n- A plain command starts no daemon. With nobody listening, the command line starts `core.sc --frame` as a child, writes it the request frame a daemon would have been sent and reads its answer as a daemon's (the same 32 MiB ceiling, no answer deadline, so the request waits for the store's lock), and nothing is left running; a daemon is started by `serve`, or by the MCP shell for the session it serves. A request answered in a child meets the in-process route's differences: `describe` adds no template clause, the lock is waited for rather than refused `store-busy`, the store is read as it is rather than as last published, `--rev` is refused `no-published-state` and `subscribe` `needs-daemon`. A daemon's connection and the child read, check and dispatch a frame through one library, `(theourgia frame)`. A store without its lock file answers `absent` on a plain command; `serve-start-failed` is only the MCP shell's start relaying `serve`'s answer.\n- `search` answers over the state the dispatcher hands it, as every read verb does, instead of opening and reducing the store on each call, so a resident daemon no longer folds the whole store again for every search. Over a connection it answers the publication as it stands, as `read` does.\n- `serve --idle-exit <seconds>` and `--idle-grace-ms <milliseconds>` (default 200, at most 5000, a larger grace refused `too-large`): a daemon leaves by itself after that long with no connection open and no request running. It unlinks its public link first, takes for the grace the connections the kernel had already completed, and exits 0 with `(idle-exit (after <seconds>) (refused-accepts <n>) (callback-raised <n>))`; a late connection cancels the exit (`idle-resumed`), and a resume that cannot happen drains, exiting 75 with `(idle-exit (reason resume-failed <cause>))`. A daemon started with the options names them in its `serving` line.\n- The MCP shell is the opener: it starts its daemon with `--idle-exit` from `THEOURGIA_IDLE_EXIT` (1800 seconds when unset, 0 never; any other value refused `bad-environment` before a start) and `--idle-grace-ms 200`, gives each request one deadline and at most two attempts (a `serve-busy` start retried after 100 ms, a daemon gone before the request's own connect started once more), and then answers `connect-failed` with `(carried-out no)`; a written request is never sent again.\n- The socket path is a symbolic link to the daemon's private socket, `<socket>.d/<pid>`, in a directory of its own whose `.endpoint` marker reserves it for the endpoint. A start reclaims what an earlier daemon of the same endpoint left; it is refused `serve-path-reserved` inside another endpoint's marked directory, `socket-dir-missing` where it would make such a directory, and `socket-path-too-long`, exit 2, when the path leaves no room for the private name. A bind or publish that fails after the store is ready exits `listener-down`.\n- Every drain waits for its listener to hand over the connections it accepted, so a connection accepted just before a drain is answered `draining` or served, never closed with nothing.\n\n### Reducer and admission\n\n- Gated arrivals: an arrival's work no longer grows with the number of standing gates (the square of the standing gates per arrival before, the whole fold the cube).\n- The pending queue indexed by what each record waits for: arrivals wake only their waiters; a seq or dep outside the log's envelope refused at `reduce-apply!`; two copies of one event leave the heap in arrival order.\n- An arrival's work independent of the standing gates (the gates as a table in admission; the notes exact); the declaration tables indexed by name and replayed in linear time; the blocks and the pasts found by id; the block structure maintained, not recomputed; the outline's order total.\n- Admission judges only what an arrival can change; a single record naming a plan is judged again when its plan's binding changes.\n\n### Structural drafts\n\n- A draft can create a file or delete one: `working-new` and `working-delete` beside the block drafts, each with its own version and cut; `commit --working-new <provisional>=<version>` and `--working-delete <holder>=<version>`; the commit makes the holder and its pieces by the construction import-git shares, in one batch with the block drafts, and answers `(created ...)` and `(pieces ...)`, also on a replay. edit-sync turns an added file into a new-file draft and a removed one into a delete draft, refusing paths outside the store route's domain (dot-named, not text, a mode other than 100644, a case collision in the resulting tree) and any live structural draft the export does not account for (`draft-changed`, remedy: export again). A new file is shown by the export only (not by outline, context or eval --working).\n\n### The review channel\n\n- `scope --freeze <dir>` reviews a frozen candidate (the copies hold the drafts' bytes, a `before` section the committed text) and writes no main letter; `--packet` adds the questions; `--roots all` copies the whole tree; `--home <h>` gives the scoped store its own home so the reviewer writes its verdict there. review-results answers `pending` until a verdict exists and refuses an incomplete one by name; sources must include a candidate copy. `collect-into --letter new --map <LANDED.sexp> --to-writer <W>` brings the verdict into the main store after the landing, resolving provisional ids and pieces, and turns the reviewer's proposals into drafts for writer W. Cuts are compared as sets. The mail handler is now contrib/mail-hook.sh (codex-mail.sh still runs it), and contrib/claude-hooks.json runs it for Claude Code with `THEOURGIA_ACTOR=claude`; it exits 1 with a line when its query fails, since a failed query is not an empty mailbox.\n\n### Store tools and the git bridge\n\n- `import-git`: a git range absorbed into the store as committed blocks and a tag; one comparison-domain judgement for publication and import.\n- `publish-landing`: the files a landing changed published from the store as one git commit; `landing-commit` tags the start of a landing; `edit-export` and `edit-sync` turn an agent's file edits into a writer's drafts; dump and restore a writer's drafts; the freeze as one script checking the view its tree came from.\n- `changed-files`: the files whose exported bytes or presence differ between two cuts; `locate`: a file's byte or line range, or one diff hunk, to the blocks holding it; `position`: the local writer's last sequence; a named tag's answer carries the cut it bound.\n- Exports at a past cut (`export-code`, `export-md --cut`), judged against the daemon's publication as a read at a cut is.\n- A draft whose base fell behind says so: `drafts --writer W` names each after its items, `(behind <block> (based-on <v>) (committed <v>))`. `landing-freeze` brings every draft onto the applied cut before it dumps (a draft whose block did not change is moved, one whose block changed is merged by a line-based three-way merge, or the freeze stops with `drafts-behind-conflict` naming every conflicting block), so a record committed after the drafts, a review letter included, no longer leaves every draft to be rebased by hand; `landing-freeze --check <dir>` validates a frozen directory and answers current, refreeze or the conflict, so a landing script can stop before its start tag.\n- `landing-freeze` moves a structural draft behind an unrelated commit onto the applied cut: a new file whose path no live file holds there, or a delete whose file's baseline there is still the draft's `based-on`, is rewritten in place at the applied cut with the same bytes, path and language and a version recomputed for that cut, under one hold of the writer's draft lock and only while it is still the version the freeze judged (a draft written again since is refused `structural-changed`, not overwritten). Nothing is removed first: a refusal or a failure before the rename leaves the entry where it was, a failed directory flush after it leaves the whole new entry at the applied cut, and either way the freeze is refused `structural-move-refused`. `REBASED.sexp` records the move as `(moved <key> (from-cut <cut>) (to-cut <cut>))`. A new file whose path a live file holds at the applied cut, or a delete whose file is gone or has changed there, still stops the freeze with `drafts-behind-conflict`.\n\n### Reading and rendering\n\n- A text-mode code block's src and doc given as text; a src held as bytes renders and is measured as its text; a draft beginning with a UTF-8 byte-order mark commits as its bytes; `whereis` nearest finds a name in another case.\n- `search` over a long query, and the relevant step of `context --for`, cost what the hits cost: a scan drops a block at the first token none of its fields takes and compares characters where they stand, without a copy per position, and the relevant step asks with each token of the text once. On a development store of 17,000 blocks, the context of a 30 KB block went from 420 s to under 4 s, with the same hits, scores and snippets for every query.\n- `context --working` assembles the material over the request writer's working view, the committed state at the drafts' cut with that writer's drafts laid over it, in a fresh query session, never the daemon's kept one. The answer carries `(working \"<writer>\")` after `for`, counted in the budget; a refusal of the view (`writer-required`, `invalid-working-writer`, `working-unavailable`, `working-draft-unreadable`) is the answer, and an id the view does not hold is answered as `read` answers it over that view. Drafts are not changes, so validity and the other hard queries answer the committed rows; the premises of drafted blocks are the overlay's and are not handed to a commit.\n\n### Store identity\n\n- A remount is not a move: the device is compared last, and a store whose directory changed only its device (a reboot can do that) is written as the same instance, `instance.sexp` rewritten with the live device under the store lock and the change kept in a history of the last eight remounts; `adopt` on such a store answers `(ok (reason remount) (device <old> <new>))` without deriving or retiring a writer. A copy elsewhere has another inode and is refused for it as before. The limit: a block-level clone of a whole volume keeps its inode numbers and is taken for a remount; `cp -R` makes new files, and adopting that copy gives a store of its own.\n- `check` reports the store's identity as `(instance (machine <m>) (device <d>) (inode <i>) (nonce <n>) [(remounts ...)])`, and a writer whose highest segment is empty after its last record's segment as `(empty-segment <n>)`, the state an interrupted rotation leaves; neither changes the verdict.\n\n### Log, ffi, platform\n\n- An append keeps its own writer's prefix; the tail check lists segments without a stat per entry; the kept prefix seeds past an older empty segment.\n- `path-stat` and `fd-stat`; a child receives this process's environment on every platform, FreeBSD included; reading the environment fails as a spawn failure.\n- A test-only timing seam on the write path (the wrappers a library of their own, decided at expansion).\n\n### Transport and CLI\n\n- `--help` and `-h` answer the verb's usage and touch nothing; the verb libraries entered on first use; a registered verb handed the dispatcher's helpers; a transport option on the stream's route refused by name; a trace line written whole.\n- Programs compiled as program objects at optimize-level 2.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- `split-suggest` reads Scheme character literals, so `#\\(` opens nothing and a file with one is no longer a single block; a Scheme library's body is cut at its definitions.\n- A `define-values` definition names its formals, so `whereis` finds it as a definition.\n- A marked export whose last body row would be a marker line but for a trailing carriage return, followed by a pad, now escapes that row; such a file decoded as `invalid-padding` before.\n- `context` finds the blocks relevant to a committed text held as bytes.\n- `adopt` on a store with no local writer is refused `no-local-writer`, before any step; it answered `internal`.\n- `write` given only half a baseline names the missing half: `--based-on` alone is refused `cut-missing`, `--working-cut` alone `based-on-missing`, a working parent's likewise.\n- An error raised out of a verb is answered with the dispatch's clause on every route; the command line ended on an uncaught exception.\n- `diff` checks that each replay covers its cut, and is refused `cut-moved` when one does not.\n- An endpoint selector whose field clause held `_` matched nothing, so every link under its relation was refused `relation-endpoint`. `_` now holds for any value of a field the block carries (a contested field holds none), and a new declaration that writes a variable there is refused `selector-value-not-literal`. An earlier declaration with `_` now holds for any value; nothing stored is reinterpreted.\n- The MCP shell refused `tools/list` whenever it carried params, answering \"Pagination is not available\", so a client that sends params with no cursor (Codex does) could not start; it now serves the whole list and refuses only a cursor with a value. `contrib/doorbell.sh` sends the Enter as its own keystroke after the line, which a combined send left unsubmitted in some terminals.\n- A batch of a few hundred inserts under a rule naming `field+` was refused `rule-unevaluable` (`query-budget`, `field+`) before a selector was asked: a write's facts (`kind+`, `field+`, `edge+`, `edge-kind+`) were read by scanning the whole relation and charged its full size on every dispatch. They are now read by their bound arguments and charged the rows matched each time the goal is dispatched; an over-budget join is still refused.\n- A writer directory copied in from another checkout that sorted first was taken as the local writer, so this copy's writes went into another instance's log. The local writer is now chosen by ownership: a live writer whose `owner.sexp` names the `(machine nonce)` of this copy's `instance.sexp` is preferred by every write, `position`, a commit's cursor and `adopt`, and name order decides only when none does; a request whose writer comes to be passed over while it runs is refused `writer-superseded` (remedy `reopen`). The verbs that change drafts refuse a `--writer` owned by another instance (`foreign-writer`, remedy `draft-writer`), and `check` reports `writer-owner-foreign`, `writer-owner-unverified`, `instance-absent` and `instance-unavailable` without changing its verdict.\n- A write refused for the store's identity left an empty next segment behind, and the `adopt` that followed named a prefix in no segment, which `check` then read as damage for ever. A write is now admitted before it cuts a torn tail or rotates, and `adopt`'s prefix names the segment of the last applied record.\n- A working view asked for a writer with no drafts, through `context`, `eval`, an export or the drafts listing, no longer creates the writer's area and lock file: the view looks first, without the lock, for the writer's `working/` or `structural/` directory, and with neither answers the committed state and creates nothing (`read --working` never created it).\n\n### Tests\n\n- The test runner gives fixtures a home of their own: `HOME` is the run's scratch home and `THEOURGIA_HOME` is unset, so no fixture writes the user's machine registry; the test home links the `.rustup` and `.cargo` directories of the runner's own home when they exist, so toolchains that resolve from `HOME` still run.\n- The fixture launcher reads the anchor's process group back instead of trusting an errno: a failed `setpgid` with the group set goes on with a note, another group stops the launch as `ANCHOR GROUP NOT SET`, and only `ESRCH` is `ANCHOR DIED`. Its test-only `--fault-setpgid` gains the `anchor-skips` and `anchor-late` modes.\n- The interleave row of `unreadable-acknowledge-routes` waits for the search's answer on the monotonic clock instead of a count of shell sleeps, which a slow runner stretched past the daemon's hold.\n- Two `context-verb` rows pin a working answer's used tokens against what it rendered, and that its hard queries are asked at the drafts' cut.\n- The change-stream guard masks the device and inode in `check`'s instance clause, which differ between any two store directories, and nothing else.\n\n### Known at this release\n\n- The median start-up, 668 ms over ten runs, is above its 637 ms budget; recorded, with a profiling item open.\n\n## 1.2.0 — 2026-10-11\n\n*156 commits.* Mail and review between sessions of any vendor through a store, rules and relation effects declared as data and judged before every write, a first import cut at an editor's symbols, stale verifications named by `context`, and a run of fixes found by the first two measurements on the real tree.\n\n### New verbs\n\n- `scope <dir> --cut <cut> --roots <id> --for <actor>` makes a store for one reader holding exactly the blocks under the roots as they were at the cut, with a letter copy (`to`, `status`, `cut`, `roots`, `scope`, `baseline`) and one letter in this store; `review-results` reads the reader's verdict and findings back as one datum; `collect-into <letter> --results <datum>` and `collect <dir>` bring them under the letter here, each finding's `about` edge on the original block. A message is a block of kind `doc` with `to` and `status`; reading one's mail is a query. `contrib/` holds the Codex hooks, the doorbell and the reader's AGENTS.md. `THEOURGIA_SOCKET` names the main store's daemon for `scope` and `collect`.\n- `relation <name> --as <kind> [--from <selector>] [--to <selector>]` declares what a relation name does in this store: one of the six relations with an effect, or `nothing`; the lifecycle reads the declarations, and a reason names the relation.\n- `rule <name> --on <kind> [--where <goal>] --must|--must-not <goal>` declares a rule of this store in the query language; every committed write is judged against the rules before it is written, by a rehearsal of the write on a clone of the state, and refused with the rule's name. `--builtin citation-coverage` enables the built-in rule. `template apply` installs a template's rules with its roots; the `project` template declares `cover`.\n- `import-code --symbols <symbols-file>` splits the FIRST import of a file with no marker lines at the symbols an editor collected, into code blocks under its file block; a file that carries marker lines follows its markers.\n\n### New options and clauses\n\n- `context` names a stale verification: for a block in scope whose `verifies` edge's target has moved past it, the notes carry `(stale-verification <m> verifies <c>)` and the receipt holds the target, read through the new query relation `verified-moved`; the human output prints the receipt on one line, as `--wire` writes it.\n- `commit` answers `behind-records`, how far each writer that moved since the writer's baseline is, in records.\n- `check` answers `(undecodable-text ((<id> <field> ...) ...))` naming the blocks whose stored text cannot be decoded; a text block beginning with a UTF-8 byte-order mark is text, and `search` and `grep` read it.\n- `eval` refuses an output limit by naming the option, cuts what a limit carries to the limit, and takes `--under` a library id; `eval --working` refuses a draft on a datum block by name.\n- `set <id> mode datum` from the command line stores the symbol, as `batch` does.\n- A datum block takes no `src` by any intent's spelling or order; a batch is read as a set for the datum rule.\n\n### Changed behaviour you may notice\n\n- A delete's targets include the live far ends of the deleted block's edges, so a rule over a review that cites a result judges the deletion of that result.\n- A field set to the symbol `omit` reads as itself; it was read as absent.\n- The history's order is delivery order, and the comments and the two readers of it say so and agree on every route.\n- A plan another writer made is read as its members are declared: five readers unwrap an `expect` wrapper through one procedure; a revoked plan's wrapped member is restored from its text.\n- The daemon's own repair of a torn tail replaces what it knows of the segment, and records the snapshot only when it is at least the cut's length; what it knew of the segment is kept only when the segment shrank or its new snapshot cannot be read.\n- The transport refusal of the MCP shell names its option, and the tools say they are bound to a store.\n- `def` refuses a name the library already defines.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- `supply` refuses a datum library's file by name instead of answering stale.\n- `state-revoked` compares versions as the strings they are.\n- A write declaration too short for its verb is asked its shape first, instead of being read as a different request.\n- An insert refused for both datum and `src` carries no block clause; `eval` states its busy pool before usage.\n- `check`'s reserved-relation records are sorted by event, the same on every route.\n\n### Test-only hooks\n\n- `THEOURGIA_PROBE=off` starts a test daemon without its timer probe; read only by an injection build, any other value refused at load.\n\n### Documentation\n\n- The README gains \"Mail and review between sessions\" (the mailbox, `scope`, `review-results`, `collect-into`, `collect`, a review round, Codex as a reader, what this does not protect against, where this runs), the `relation` and `rule` sections, the rules' place in `template`, and the environment variable table's new rows.\n\n## VS Code extension 1.1.1 — 2026-10-09\n\n*The extension's own version, `theourgia.theourgos`, published on the Marketplace for macOS on arm64 and x86_64 and Linux on x86_64 and arm64.* Import with Symbols, on a core pinned at theourgia be42914.\n\n### Added\n\n- Import with Symbols: a folder or a source file is imported into the store with the core's `import-code --symbols`, and the first import of each file is split at the editor's top-level symbols into a file block and its code blocks, with no review copy first. The report in the output channel places each new block at its lines, and gives a file the core refused to split with the line and column of the byte it names. A file with unsaved edits stops the command before anything is sent.\n\n### The core it runs\n\n- The core is pinned at theourgia be42914, ten commits after 1.1.0, which adds `import-code --symbols`.\n- The core checks every cut of a first import split at symbols: a cut must fall at the top level of the file, outside its protected prefix (a shebang, a coding line, a byte-order mark), so a symbol that starts inside a body, a string, a block comment or an open bracket refuses that file instead of cutting it. The report names the refusal: `symbols-not-top-level`, `symbols-unscanned`, `symbols-in-prefix`, `symbols-unchecked` or `symbols-no-file`.\n- The extension's unit suite passed against that core on darwin-arm64, darwin-x64, linux-x64 and linux-arm64 (CI run 37816045917).\n\n## VS Code extension 1.1.0 — 2026-10-06\n\n*The extension's own version, `theourgia.theourgos`, on the Marketplace for macOS on arm64 and x86_64 and Linux on x86_64 and arm64.* The core pinned at theourgia 1.1.0.\n\n### Changed\n\n- The extension is Theourgos: package `theourgos` under the publisher `theourgia`, id `theourgia.theourgos`, packages named `theourgos-<target>.vsix`. Its commands and settings keep their `theourgia.` names. A 1.0.0 installed by hand from a `.vsix` built before this change has the id `theourgia.theourgia`, which VS Code treats as another extension: let its pending saves go through, or discard them, then uninstall it before installing 1.1.0, since both register the same commands.\n- The core is pinned at theourgia 1.1.0 (3a4ac93).\n- The setting `theourgia.writer` is removed: each window has written its drafts under a name of its own since before 1.0.0, so it decided nothing. A `settings.json` that still sets it shows an unknown setting, and nothing changes.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- The search view answered \"the store did not answer the search\" for every query that found anything, since 1.0.0. A search now lists its hits.\n- A datum-mode block opened as an empty buffer, and an edit saved through it was answered as saved and never reached the code. It now opens read-only, as the datum export writes its library's file, and a file opened under 1.0.0 on a datum block is refused on save instead of appearing to save.\n- A save the core refuses because its block is a datum block, or because a log segment was replaced under the store's daemon, now says which, and for the second says to restart the store's daemon and save again.\n- Search and the hover's mentions ask the core with `--wire`. When every hit is in a superseded or refuted block, the search view says the words are found only in blocks that are not in force, rather than that nothing matches.\n- The package for macOS on x86_64 passes its unit suite against the core it is pinned to, and 1.1.0 is on the Marketplace for all four targets; 1.0.0 was packaged for Apple Silicon only.\n\n## 1.1.0 — 2026-10-05\n\n*26 commits.* Queries and context for agents, read receipts and write premises, class and validity, a change stream, runners for five more languages, and a measured row for macOS on x86_64.\n\n### New verbs\n\n- `query <goal>` answers every binding of a goal's variables over the committed state at one cut. Its facts are twenty relations the store already answers, combined by nineteen rules kept as data; `query --relations` lists both. A query reads no log, runs no code and writes nothing; one whose new work passes a million tuples is refused, never answered in part.\n- `context --for <id> --budget <tokens>` answers what to read before working on a block, within a budget counted as four bytes a token of the answer as the wire prints it, its cut left out, with a receipt that a commit can be given back as its premises.\n- `subscribe changes <rev> [<token>]` keeps a connection to the daemon and prints one line for each publication: blocks added and removed, fields, positions and parents changed, conflicts entered and resolved, edges added and removed. A commit made outside the daemon reaches subscribers within about a second. The MCP shell does not offer it, and without a daemon it answers `needs-daemon`.\n- `commitments` lists the decisions still owed: a decision is open until a live block implements it (a task only once it is done) or its status is done or dropped. `--drifted` names implementations whose content has changed since the link, unless a later link or edit of the decision has seen the change; status, batch, keywords, class and slug do not count.\n- `init --template` or `--template-file`, `template apply` and `template export` make a store from a template of document roots and relations, or give one to a store; the built-in templates are `project` and `memory`. `tasks` lists blocks of kind task.\n- `names <id>` lists the names a code block uses and the libraries a library block imports; `uses <name>` lists the live code blocks that use a name.\n\n### New options and clauses\n\n- `read <id> --cut <cut>` reads a block at a causal cut, written out or as a tag; every `log` entry gives the event's `cut` and `past`.\n- `read --rev` adds the daemon publication the answer was read from, `(rev <n> (daemon \"<token>\"))`, so a subscriber can apply frames by revision.\n- Read receipts: `(cut ...)`, the state a read was answered from, and `(versions ...)`, the version of each block it shows or lists, at the end of an answer. `read`, `refs`, `commitments`, `tasks`, `names` and `uses` carry both; `search`, `grep` and `whereis` add the versions; `outline` and `reach` carry the cut.\n- `--premises <datum>` on every committed write -- insert, set, move, del, link, unlink, tag, batch, commit, import-code, import-md, def and template apply -- with a list of block versions and query digests, or a `receipt` as `context` prints it. When they no longer hold, nothing is written and the write is refused by name.\n- A block's `class`: observation, inference, ruling, verification or external. The relations supersedes, refutes, depends-on, implements, verifies and conflicts-with decide whether a block is valid, superseded, refuted or needs review. A plain `read` of a block that is not valid adds `(validity ...)`; `search`, `grep` and `whereis` leave out superseded and refuted blocks, and `--all-validity` keeps them.\n- `eval --lang` has default runners for `typescript` (node), `go` (go run), `rust` and `c` (compiled, then run) and `java` (single-file launch). `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_TYPESCRIPT`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_GO`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_RUST`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_C` and `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_JAVA` each replace one with a whole runner. Runners still need `THEOURGIA_RUNNERS=on`.\n\n### Platforms\n\n- macOS on x86_64 has its own measured row, read under Rosetta, and is no longer refused; there `stat` and `lstat` bind their `$INODE64` symbols.\n\n### Changed behaviour you may notice\n\n- When validity leaves out every hit, the human output of `search`, `grep` and `whereis` now prints the `(excluded ...)` line instead of nothing. A script that took empty output to mean no match should read `--wire`.\n- `write` on a block whose mode is datum is refused `draft-on-datum-unsupported`, and `commit` refuses a draft that would put text on one. Both used to answer ok, and the edit never took effect. Change a datum block with `def`; `check` reports a datum block that already carries text.\n- A daemon refuses every later write to a writer's log once the segment it was appending to is replaced by a shorter one -- a git checkout of another branch under a running daemon -- until it is restarted. Restart the daemon after switching the store's branch.\n- The answers of `read`, `refs`, `commitments`, `tasks`, `names`, `uses`, `search`, `grep`, `whereis`, `outline` and `reach` end with receipt clauses. The human output is unchanged except for plain `read` and `reach`, which print whole; a script that compares whole `--wire` answers will see the new clauses at the end.\n- Superseded and refuted blocks are left out of `search`, `grep` and `whereis` by default in a store that links those relations. Ask with `--all-validity` to see them.\n- Starting from source takes about 1% longer: the code the premises check adds is compiled at every start (1.1%, the median of three runs of 40 interleaved pairs); the commitments registry added 0.5% before it.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- The daemon sampled a publication's snapshot after its fold had released the store's lock, so a commit by another process in between could go unseen until something else changed.\n- A retry that completes an interrupted plan -- a commit, an import, a def -- wrote its missing members over what other requests had written to the same blocks since, and answered ok. It now checks first and answers `stale-baseline` when a block has moved. An interrupted import that created a file can now be completed; it used to answer `malformed-intent`.\n- `import-md` replaced bytes that were not UTF-8 with U+FFFD and imported the file changed. A file that is not valid UTF-8, or holds a NUL byte, is now skipped and listed under `(skipped ...)`, as `import-code` does.\n- `read --cut` could answer ok from a smaller cut when a record was quarantined between the cut check and the replay; it now refuses `cut-moved`.\n- A code block whose derived fields raised when shown was answered `(error internal ...)` with no block named; the answer now names it.\n- A value written once that merely had a conflict's shape was read as a conflict by `search`, `grep`, references, `commitments` and `tasks`; a field is contested now only when two or more of its candidates survive.\n- A record definition with a malformed field spec could raise when the store read a block's names; it now binds only its type's names. The name walk's shape rules follow what Chez accepts.\n\n### Documentation\n\n- README corrected against the code, with a new opening line.\n- The agent-memory guide, `docs/claude-code-memory.md`, checked against the code.\n\n## 1.0.0 — 2026-09-30\n\n*225 commits.* The first release: a block-graph store where agents and people write together, with a command line, a daemon, an MCP shell and a VS Code extension.\n\n### The store\n\n- A store is a directory of plain files. Each writer appends to its own segments, and the store can live in a git repository; `init` writes its `.gitignore`.\n- 38 verbs, each with a usage line and a one-sentence description that `describe` answers: init, insert, set, move, del, link, unlink, def, outline, read, refs, reach, search, grep, whereis, log, tag, diff, describe, conflicts, write, restore, commit, drafts, diagnostics, discard, batch, import-md, export-md, import-code, export-code, split-suggest, supply, adopt, check, snapshot, publish and eval.\n- Writing goes through drafts: `write` keeps a draft in the writer's own space, and `commit` installs it; a draft whose baseline the block has moved on from is refused `stale-baseline`.\n- The block-editing verbs, `batch` and `tag <name>` accept a request identity, so a client that missed the answer can send the request again and be told what happened.\n- Code and prose sit in one graph: `import-md` and `import-code` read a directory into blocks (Scheme as data with `--datum`), and `export-md` and `export-code` write them back out.\n- `eval` evaluates one expression against the store in a child process with no filesystem and no network; another language runs with `--lang` where the operator has set `THEOURGIA_RUNNERS=on`.\n- `supply` keeps what an editor computed (signatures, calls, diagnostics) in `<store>/derived/`, outside the log.\n- `publish` receives another store's history one segment at a time.\n\n### The daemon\n\n- `theourgia serve` holds a store open and answers over a unix socket; the command line starts one when none is running, and finds its socket by the same rule the daemon used to create it.\n- It answers through the same dispatcher as the command line. `THEOURGIA_LOCAL=1` answers in process instead.\n\n### The MCP shell\n\n- `theourgia-mcp --store <path>` speaks MCP `2025-11-25` over stdio: one tool per verb routed to the daemon, and `eval`, run as the shell's own child.\n- A core refusal comes back as a successful tool result.\n- Each session gets a writer of its own unless `THEOURGIA_WRITER` or `--writer` fixes one.\n\n### The command line\n\n- `theourgia` is the command; `theourgiad`, the daemon program, and `theourgia-mcp`, the MCP shell, sit beside it.\n- `build.ss` compiles the tree and igropyr into a directory of objects, which start about twelve times faster than source.\n- Every verb takes `--store`, `--actor`, `--socket`, `--wire`, `--req` and `--cursor`.\n\n### Platforms and requirements\n\n- Chez Scheme 10.1.0, 10.3.0, 10.4.0 or 10.4.1, libuv and igropyr. On another Chez version the verbs that print a datum refuse with `unsupported-printer-version`.\n- Platforms with a measured row: macOS on arm64, and Linux on x86_64 and on aarch64 with glibc. On any other platform every program exits 75 with `platform-unmeasured`.\n\n### Published to\n\n- Homebrew: the tap `guenchi/theourgia`, whose formula builds this release with Homebrew's Chez Scheme.\n- npm: `theourgia` 1.0.0.\n- The VS Code extension, at its own version 1.0.0, packaged for macOS on Apple Silicon.\n\n### Known not to work\n\n- macOS on Intel, or under Rosetta: the core has no measured row there and exits 75 with `platform-unmeasured`.\n- FreeBSD 15 on amd64 has a measured row, but the daemon does not start there yet.\n- The machine registry never retires an entry: entries for deleted stores stay in it, marked active.\n- The export verbs take no `--cut`: a historical cut with drafts over it has no export."))
d48111d6 (8 1793400157914 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "d9l36a5r.40" src "# Changelog\n\n## 1.3.1 — 2026-10-30\n\n*31 commits.* Splitting and import with symbols on real TypeScript and JavaScript: the ECMAScript scan no longer falls back to one block at a division, a regular expression, a template or a generic, a file the scan cannot check is imported whole instead of being left out, a symbol-cut block carries the symbol's name where the language derives none, and JSX-looking text in strings, comments and regular expressions no longer makes plain JavaScript fall back. Beside it: the daemon yields during blocking file I/O, `context` lists the users of a unit's names, the review channel keeps every text of a packet section, a failed commit says why, and the store tools refuse a few wrong landings by name.\n\n### Import with symbols\n\n- Splitting and `import --symbols`: the ECMAScript scan keeps every reading the text has not disproved (a `/` read both as a division and as a regular expression, template and interpolation frames, TypeScript's postfix `!` and type-argument `>`) and takes a line as top level only when every surviving reading does, so divisions, regular expressions, templates and generics no longer make a file fall back with `lexically-uncertain`; past 32 readings the file falls back as before.\n- A file the scan cannot check (no language entry, or text the scanner does not follow) is imported whole and listed under `symbols-imported-whole`; a file whose cut was checked and found wrong is still left out, under `symbols-refused`.\n- A symbol-cut block the language does not name is titled by the symbol the editor gave.\n- JSX in plain JavaScript is refused by position, not by text: a `<` where an operand is expected is an `opener`, and a `/` read as a division whose next token is `>` is a `self-close`; markup in strings, comments, templates and regular expressions passes.\n- In the table scan of the other languages, a comment no longer ends the statement it follows.\n\n### Daemon\n\n- Blocking file I/O yields to the scheduler; a probe defers while a publication is in progress.\n- A public name spelled like another endpoint's lock is refused.\n\n### Context and working views\n\n- `context` gains a `users` clause after `background`: the live code blocks outside the unit that use a name it defines, each listed whole or counted under the excluded counter `users`; the clause is reserved at the counters' digits, so which hard blocks are shown still depends on the hard material only.\n- A draft new file's provisional id is answered `(error provisional-id (id ..) (reason not-committed))` by every verb that resolves an id over a view; with `--working`, a refusal of the view still comes first.\n- A fold that fails at a named cut is answered in its own class (a busy lock as `store-busy`), not as an unusable cut; `context`'s description says that under `--working` a drafted block's premises are the working view's versions.\n\n### Store and store tools\n\n- A failed commit's answer carries a `(failed ...)` clause naming its cause, where it said only `commit-barrier-failed`.\n- `publish-landing` refuses a landing whose datum libraries changed and names a library with no path by its id; a freeze stays current when nothing that lands has changed; `landing-freeze --tree` compares the freeze with the tested edit directory.\n- `import-git` follows the domain for a range instead of refusing a binary.\n- `supply` and `init` keep an existing `.gitignore` and refuse one that is not a file; `supply --clear` writes nothing it need not; the edit tools find the edit directory's sidecar by one lexical fold.\n\n### Review channel\n\n- A packet section may carry several `(text ...)` entries: joined in packet order with one empty line; `(text)` and `(text #f)` carry none; a non-string text refuses the packet as `packet-malformed`.\n\n### Tests\n\n- The change-stream guard runs real daemons on a real seed and reads answers whole; the guard names the daemon program by a define the spawn reading sees; two timing rows wait for the event, not a clock; the scan-cost row reads medians over three pairs; the two-opener daemon rows read the held loser by pid; the two context base rows leave the users clause out of both sides; eval-local binds its runs before their probes; signal!'s identity guard has its own rows; the mcp-shell stub appends its log line before it answers.\n\n## 1.3.0 — 2026-10-28\n\n*336 commits.* Manteia, the rule language, completed (kinds, stored derived relations, aggregation and stratified negation, incremental advance); structural drafts; a review channel that carries a frozen candidate; the daemon answering from its publication with one session kept across requests, started by the MCP shell and leaving when idle; a store identity that survives a remount; the reducer's pending queue and gates made linear; and the store's git bridge (import-git, publish-landing, the edit tools).\n\n### Manteia, the rule language\n\n- Column kinds for the rule language, checked statically; kind containment normalises both sides; goals dispatched to one handler per kind in three environments.\n- Stored derived relations, declared by a verb (`derive`) and checked where they are used; a resent rule is its replay; recursive relations kept whole; availability by one search over the reach, the shortest through-chain reported; a relation's through-chain built once a table.\n- `not`, `count`, `sum`, `min` and `max` over a goal; stored relations stratified; a form's table named by its occurrence and group; emptiness read goal by goal.\n- `session-advance`: a session advanced to a later state, its positive strata maintained by delete-and-rederive, the auxiliary session's work charged to the advance; each callee collected once.\n- An `and` nested in an `and` is its goals in place; `supply` from any tool with its version.\n- The README's language chapter, construct by construct, its example answers measured.\n- A text-mode Scheme file's library and imports are read from its header through its file holder (`in-library`, `imports` and the new `file-imports`, all external), by a new bounded reader library, `(theourgia reader)`; a `holder` fact relates a code block to its file holder, and `def-for`'s no-library clause holds only within one holder, so a script's own definitions serve its own pieces and nothing else. `uses-name` for a text block of a language with a reader binds what its complete forms bind, `(syntactic (reading forms))`. `context` folds a name's definitions past the first two into a `folded` clause, takes `--names <n>` (repeatable, reported in `asked`), scopes a file holder or any non-code target to the code blocks under it, and takes `--budget min+<t>`; the session trace counts, per call, definitions-index builds, fact builds by relation, preferred candidates by step, and whether the session was reused or why it was dropped.\n\n### Daemon\n\n- `query` and `context` are published reads of the store process: the publication made current first, no replay while the store has not changed; a query after an outside write sees it (the `theourgia` hooks rely on it).\n- One query session kept by the daemon across requests: reused on the same publication, advanced when the publication moves forward, replaced otherwise; `serve --session-size <entries>` (0 disables); `(name-use syntactic)` reported per answer; the one exception to byte-identity with the local route: a query the local route refuses for its budget may be answered by the daemon.\n- A store whose directory is removed is drained and the daemon exits `store-gone`; an absent store creates nothing.\n- A plain command starts no daemon. With nobody listening, the command line starts `core.sc --frame` as a child, writes it the request frame a daemon would have been sent and reads its answer as a daemon's (the same 32 MiB ceiling, no answer deadline, so the request waits for the store's lock), and nothing is left running; a daemon is started by `serve`, or by the MCP shell for the session it serves. A request answered in a child meets the in-process route's differences: `describe` adds no template clause, the lock is waited for rather than refused `store-busy`, the store is read as it is rather than as last published, `--rev` is refused `no-published-state` and `subscribe` `needs-daemon`. A daemon's connection and the child read, check and dispatch a frame through one library, `(theourgia frame)`. A store without its lock file answers `absent` on a plain command; `serve-start-failed` is only the MCP shell's start relaying `serve`'s answer.\n- `search` answers over the state the dispatcher hands it, as every read verb does, instead of opening and reducing the store on each call, so a resident daemon no longer folds the whole store again for every search. Over a connection it answers the publication as it stands, as `read` does.\n- `serve --idle-exit <seconds>` and `--idle-grace-ms <milliseconds>` (default 200, at most 5000, a larger grace refused `too-large`): a daemon leaves by itself after that long with no connection open and no request running. It unlinks its public link first, takes for the grace the connections the kernel had already completed, and exits 0 with `(idle-exit (after <seconds>) (refused-accepts <n>) (callback-raised <n>))`; a late connection cancels the exit (`idle-resumed`), and a resume that cannot happen drains, exiting 75 with `(idle-exit (reason resume-failed <cause>))`. A daemon started with the options names them in its `serving` line.\n- The MCP shell is the opener: it starts its daemon with `--idle-exit` from `THEOURGIA_IDLE_EXIT` (1800 seconds when unset, 0 never; any other value refused `bad-environment` before a start) and `--idle-grace-ms 200`, gives each request one deadline and at most two attempts (a `serve-busy` start retried after 100 ms, a daemon gone before the request's own connect started once more), and then answers `connect-failed` with `(carried-out no)`; a written request is never sent again.\n- The socket path is a symbolic link to the daemon's private socket, `<socket>.d/<pid>`, in a directory of its own whose `.endpoint` marker reserves it for the endpoint. A start reclaims what an earlier daemon of the same endpoint left; it is refused `serve-path-reserved` inside another endpoint's marked directory, `socket-dir-missing` where it would make such a directory, and `socket-path-too-long`, exit 2, when the path leaves no room for the private name. A bind or publish that fails after the store is ready exits `listener-down`.\n- Every drain waits for its listener to hand over the connections it accepted, so a connection accepted just before a drain is answered `draining` or served, never closed with nothing.\n\n### Reducer and admission\n\n- Gated arrivals: an arrival's work no longer grows with the number of standing gates (the square of the standing gates per arrival before, the whole fold the cube).\n- The pending queue indexed by what each record waits for: arrivals wake only their waiters; a seq or dep outside the log's envelope refused at `reduce-apply!`; two copies of one event leave the heap in arrival order.\n- An arrival's work independent of the standing gates (the gates as a table in admission; the notes exact); the declaration tables indexed by name and replayed in linear time; the blocks and the pasts found by id; the block structure maintained, not recomputed; the outline's order total.\n- Admission judges only what an arrival can change; a single record naming a plan is judged again when its plan's binding changes.\n\n### Structural drafts\n\n- A draft can create a file or delete one: `working-new` and `working-delete` beside the block drafts, each with its own version and cut; `commit --working-new <provisional>=<version>` and `--working-delete <holder>=<version>`; the commit makes the holder and its pieces by the construction import-git shares, in one batch with the block drafts, and answers `(created ...)` and `(pieces ...)`, also on a replay. edit-sync turns an added file into a new-file draft and a removed one into a delete draft, refusing paths outside the store route's domain (dot-named, not text, a mode other than 100644, a case collision in the resulting tree) and any live structural draft the export does not account for (`draft-changed`, remedy: export again). A new file is shown by the export only (not by outline, context or eval --working).\n\n### The review channel\n\n- `scope --freeze <dir>` reviews a frozen candidate (the copies hold the drafts' bytes, a `before` section the committed text) and writes no main letter; `--packet` adds the questions; `--roots all` copies the whole tree; `--home <h>` gives the scoped store its own home so the reviewer writes its verdict there. review-results answers `pending` until a verdict exists and refuses an incomplete one by name; sources must include a candidate copy. `collect-into --letter new --map <LANDED.sexp> --to-writer <W>` brings the verdict into the main store after the landing, resolving provisional ids and pieces, and turns the reviewer's proposals into drafts for writer W. Cuts are compared as sets. The mail handler is now contrib/mail-hook.sh (codex-mail.sh still runs it), and contrib/claude-hooks.json runs it for Claude Code with `THEOURGIA_ACTOR=claude`; it exits 1 with a line when its query fails, since a failed query is not an empty mailbox.\n\n### Store tools and the git bridge\n\n- `import-git`: a git range absorbed into the store as committed blocks and a tag; one comparison-domain judgement for publication and import.\n- `publish-landing`: the files a landing changed published from the store as one git commit; `landing-commit` tags the start of a landing; `edit-export` and `edit-sync` turn an agent's file edits into a writer's drafts; dump and restore a writer's drafts; the freeze as one script checking the view its tree came from.\n- `changed-files`: the files whose exported bytes or presence differ between two cuts; `locate`: a file's byte or line range, or one diff hunk, to the blocks holding it; `position`: the local writer's last sequence; a named tag's answer carries the cut it bound.\n- Exports at a past cut (`export-code`, `export-md --cut`), judged against the daemon's publication as a read at a cut is.\n- A draft whose base fell behind says so: `drafts --writer W` names each after its items, `(behind <block> (based-on <v>) (committed <v>))`. `landing-freeze` brings every draft onto the applied cut before it dumps (a draft whose block did not change is moved, one whose block changed is merged by a line-based three-way merge, or the freeze stops with `drafts-behind-conflict` naming every conflicting block), so a record committed after the drafts, a review letter included, no longer leaves every draft to be rebased by hand; `landing-freeze --check <dir>` validates a frozen directory and answers current, refreeze or the conflict, so a landing script can stop before its start tag.\n- `landing-freeze` moves a structural draft behind an unrelated commit onto the applied cut: a new file whose path no live file holds there, or a delete whose file's baseline there is still the draft's `based-on`, is rewritten in place at the applied cut with the same bytes, path and language and a version recomputed for that cut, under one hold of the writer's draft lock and only while it is still the version the freeze judged (a draft written again since is refused `structural-changed`, not overwritten). Nothing is removed first: a refusal or a failure before the rename leaves the entry where it was, a failed directory flush after it leaves the whole new entry at the applied cut, and either way the freeze is refused `structural-move-refused`. `REBASED.sexp` records the move as `(moved <key> (from-cut <cut>) (to-cut <cut>))`. A new file whose path a live file holds at the applied cut, or a delete whose file is gone or has changed there, still stops the freeze with `drafts-behind-conflict`.\n\n### Reading and rendering\n\n- A text-mode code block's src and doc given as text; a src held as bytes renders and is measured as its text; a draft beginning with a UTF-8 byte-order mark commits as its bytes; `whereis` nearest finds a name in another case.\n- `search` over a long query, and the relevant step of `context --for`, cost what the hits cost: a scan drops a block at the first token none of its fields takes and compares characters where they stand, without a copy per position, and the relevant step asks with each token of the text once. On a development store of 17,000 blocks, the context of a 30 KB block went from 420 s to under 4 s, with the same hits, scores and snippets for every query.\n- `context --working` assembles the material over the request writer's working view, the committed state at the drafts' cut with that writer's drafts laid over it, in a fresh query session, never the daemon's kept one. The answer carries `(working \"<writer>\")` after `for`, counted in the budget; a refusal of the view (`writer-required`, `invalid-working-writer`, `working-unavailable`, `working-draft-unreadable`) is the answer, and an id the view does not hold is answered as `read` answers it over that view. Drafts are not changes, so validity and the other hard queries answer the committed rows; the premises of drafted blocks are the overlay's and are not handed to a commit.\n\n### Store identity\n\n- A remount is not a move: the device is compared last, and a store whose directory changed only its device (a reboot can do that) is written as the same instance, `instance.sexp` rewritten with the live device under the store lock and the change kept in a history of the last eight remounts; `adopt` on such a store answers `(ok (reason remount) (device <old> <new>))` without deriving or retiring a writer. A copy elsewhere has another inode and is refused for it as before. The limit: a block-level clone of a whole volume keeps its inode numbers and is taken for a remount; `cp -R` makes new files, and adopting that copy gives a store of its own.\n- `check` reports the store's identity as `(instance (machine <m>) (device <d>) (inode <i>) (nonce <n>) [(remounts ...)])`, and a writer whose highest segment is empty after its last record's segment as `(empty-segment <n>)`, the state an interrupted rotation leaves; neither changes the verdict.\n\n### Log, ffi, platform\n\n- An append keeps its own writer's prefix; the tail check lists segments without a stat per entry; the kept prefix seeds past an older empty segment.\n- `path-stat` and `fd-stat`; a child receives this process's environment on every platform, FreeBSD included; reading the environment fails as a spawn failure.\n- A test-only timing seam on the write path (the wrappers a library of their own, decided at expansion).\n\n### Transport and CLI\n\n- `--help` and `-h` answer the verb's usage and touch nothing; the verb libraries entered on first use; a registered verb handed the dispatcher's helpers; a transport option on the stream's route refused by name; a trace line written whole.\n- Programs compiled as program objects at optimize-level 2.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- `split-suggest` reads Scheme character literals, so `#\\(` opens nothing and a file with one is no longer a single block; a Scheme library's body is cut at its definitions.\n- A `define-values` definition names its formals, so `whereis` finds it as a definition.\n- A marked export whose last body row would be a marker line but for a trailing carriage return, followed by a pad, now escapes that row; such a file decoded as `invalid-padding` before.\n- `context` finds the blocks relevant to a committed text held as bytes.\n- `adopt` on a store with no local writer is refused `no-local-writer`, before any step; it answered `internal`.\n- `write` given only half a baseline names the missing half: `--based-on` alone is refused `cut-missing`, `--working-cut` alone `based-on-missing`, a working parent's likewise.\n- An error raised out of a verb is answered with the dispatch's clause on every route; the command line ended on an uncaught exception.\n- `diff` checks that each replay covers its cut, and is refused `cut-moved` when one does not.\n- An endpoint selector whose field clause held `_` matched nothing, so every link under its relation was refused `relation-endpoint`. `_` now holds for any value of a field the block carries (a contested field holds none), and a new declaration that writes a variable there is refused `selector-value-not-literal`. An earlier declaration with `_` now holds for any value; nothing stored is reinterpreted.\n- The MCP shell refused `tools/list` whenever it carried params, answering \"Pagination is not available\", so a client that sends params with no cursor (Codex does) could not start; it now serves the whole list and refuses only a cursor with a value. `contrib/doorbell.sh` sends the Enter as its own keystroke after the line, which a combined send left unsubmitted in some terminals.\n- A batch of a few hundred inserts under a rule naming `field+` was refused `rule-unevaluable` (`query-budget`, `field+`) before a selector was asked: a write's facts (`kind+`, `field+`, `edge+`, `edge-kind+`) were read by scanning the whole relation and charged its full size on every dispatch. They are now read by their bound arguments and charged the rows matched each time the goal is dispatched; an over-budget join is still refused.\n- A writer directory copied in from another checkout that sorted first was taken as the local writer, so this copy's writes went into another instance's log. The local writer is now chosen by ownership: a live writer whose `owner.sexp` names the `(machine nonce)` of this copy's `instance.sexp` is preferred by every write, `position`, a commit's cursor and `adopt`, and name order decides only when none does; a request whose writer comes to be passed over while it runs is refused `writer-superseded` (remedy `reopen`). The verbs that change drafts refuse a `--writer` owned by another instance (`foreign-writer`, remedy `draft-writer`), and `check` reports `writer-owner-foreign`, `writer-owner-unverified`, `instance-absent` and `instance-unavailable` without changing its verdict.\n- A write refused for the store's identity left an empty next segment behind, and the `adopt` that followed named a prefix in no segment, which `check` then read as damage for ever. A write is now admitted before it cuts a torn tail or rotates, and `adopt`'s prefix names the segment of the last applied record.\n- A working view asked for a writer with no drafts, through `context`, `eval`, an export or the drafts listing, no longer creates the writer's area and lock file: the view looks first, without the lock, for the writer's `working/` or `structural/` directory, and with neither answers the committed state and creates nothing (`read --working` never created it).\n\n### Tests\n\n- The test runner gives fixtures a home of their own: `HOME` is the run's scratch home and `THEOURGIA_HOME` is unset, so no fixture writes the user's machine registry; the test home links the `.rustup` and `.cargo` directories of the runner's own home when they exist, so toolchains that resolve from `HOME` still run.\n- The fixture launcher reads the anchor's process group back instead of trusting an errno: a failed `setpgid` with the group set goes on with a note, another group stops the launch as `ANCHOR GROUP NOT SET`, and only `ESRCH` is `ANCHOR DIED`. Its test-only `--fault-setpgid` gains the `anchor-skips` and `anchor-late` modes.\n- The interleave row of `unreadable-acknowledge-routes` waits for the search's answer on the monotonic clock instead of a count of shell sleeps, which a slow runner stretched past the daemon's hold.\n- Two `context-verb` rows pin a working answer's used tokens against what it rendered, and that its hard queries are asked at the drafts' cut.\n- The change-stream guard masks the device and inode in `check`'s instance clause, which differ between any two store directories, and nothing else.\n\n### Known at this release\n\n- The median start-up, 668 ms over ten runs, is above its 637 ms budget; recorded, with a profiling item open.\n\n## 1.2.0 — 2026-10-11\n\n*156 commits.* Mail and review between sessions of any vendor through a store, rules and relation effects declared as data and judged before every write, a first import cut at an editor's symbols, stale verifications named by `context`, and a run of fixes found by the first two measurements on the real tree.\n\n### New verbs\n\n- `scope <dir> --cut <cut> --roots <id> --for <actor>` makes a store for one reader holding exactly the blocks under the roots as they were at the cut, with a letter copy (`to`, `status`, `cut`, `roots`, `scope`, `baseline`) and one letter in this store; `review-results` reads the reader's verdict and findings back as one datum; `collect-into <letter> --results <datum>` and `collect <dir>` bring them under the letter here, each finding's `about` edge on the original block. A message is a block of kind `doc` with `to` and `status`; reading one's mail is a query. `contrib/` holds the Codex hooks, the doorbell and the reader's AGENTS.md. `THEOURGIA_SOCKET` names the main store's daemon for `scope` and `collect`.\n- `relation <name> --as <kind> [--from <selector>] [--to <selector>]` declares what a relation name does in this store: one of the six relations with an effect, or `nothing`; the lifecycle reads the declarations, and a reason names the relation.\n- `rule <name> --on <kind> [--where <goal>] --must|--must-not <goal>` declares a rule of this store in the query language; every committed write is judged against the rules before it is written, by a rehearsal of the write on a clone of the state, and refused with the rule's name. `--builtin citation-coverage` enables the built-in rule. `template apply` installs a template's rules with its roots; the `project` template declares `cover`.\n- `import-code --symbols <symbols-file>` splits the FIRST import of a file with no marker lines at the symbols an editor collected, into code blocks under its file block; a file that carries marker lines follows its markers.\n\n### New options and clauses\n\n- `context` names a stale verification: for a block in scope whose `verifies` edge's target has moved past it, the notes carry `(stale-verification <m> verifies <c>)` and the receipt holds the target, read through the new query relation `verified-moved`; the human output prints the receipt on one line, as `--wire` writes it.\n- `commit` answers `behind-records`, how far each writer that moved since the writer's baseline is, in records.\n- `check` answers `(undecodable-text ((<id> <field> ...) ...))` naming the blocks whose stored text cannot be decoded; a text block beginning with a UTF-8 byte-order mark is text, and `search` and `grep` read it.\n- `eval` refuses an output limit by naming the option, cuts what a limit carries to the limit, and takes `--under` a library id; `eval --working` refuses a draft on a datum block by name.\n- `set <id> mode datum` from the command line stores the symbol, as `batch` does.\n- A datum block takes no `src` by any intent's spelling or order; a batch is read as a set for the datum rule.\n\n### Changed behaviour you may notice\n\n- A delete's targets include the live far ends of the deleted block's edges, so a rule over a review that cites a result judges the deletion of that result.\n- A field set to the symbol `omit` reads as itself; it was read as absent.\n- The history's order is delivery order, and the comments and the two readers of it say so and agree on every route.\n- A plan another writer made is read as its members are declared: five readers unwrap an `expect` wrapper through one procedure; a revoked plan's wrapped member is restored from its text.\n- The daemon's own repair of a torn tail replaces what it knows of the segment, and records the snapshot only when it is at least the cut's length; what it knew of the segment is kept only when the segment shrank or its new snapshot cannot be read.\n- The transport refusal of the MCP shell names its option, and the tools say they are bound to a store.\n- `def` refuses a name the library already defines.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- `supply` refuses a datum library's file by name instead of answering stale.\n- `state-revoked` compares versions as the strings they are.\n- A write declaration too short for its verb is asked its shape first, instead of being read as a different request.\n- An insert refused for both datum and `src` carries no block clause; `eval` states its busy pool before usage.\n- `check`'s reserved-relation records are sorted by event, the same on every route.\n\n### Test-only hooks\n\n- `THEOURGIA_PROBE=off` starts a test daemon without its timer probe; read only by an injection build, any other value refused at load.\n\n### Documentation\n\n- The README gains \"Mail and review between sessions\" (the mailbox, `scope`, `review-results`, `collect-into`, `collect`, a review round, Codex as a reader, what this does not protect against, where this runs), the `relation` and `rule` sections, the rules' place in `template`, and the environment variable table's new rows.\n\n## VS Code extension 1.1.1 — 2026-10-09\n\n*The extension's own version, `theourgia.theourgos`, published on the Marketplace for macOS on arm64 and x86_64 and Linux on x86_64 and arm64.* Import with Symbols, on a core pinned at theourgia be42914.\n\n### Added\n\n- Import with Symbols: a folder or a source file is imported into the store with the core's `import-code --symbols`, and the first import of each file is split at the editor's top-level symbols into a file block and its code blocks, with no review copy first. The report in the output channel places each new block at its lines, and gives a file the core refused to split with the line and column of the byte it names. A file with unsaved edits stops the command before anything is sent.\n\n### The core it runs\n\n- The core is pinned at theourgia be42914, ten commits after 1.1.0, which adds `import-code --symbols`.\n- The core checks every cut of a first import split at symbols: a cut must fall at the top level of the file, outside its protected prefix (a shebang, a coding line, a byte-order mark), so a symbol that starts inside a body, a string, a block comment or an open bracket refuses that file instead of cutting it. The report names the refusal: `symbols-not-top-level`, `symbols-unscanned`, `symbols-in-prefix`, `symbols-unchecked` or `symbols-no-file`.\n- The extension's unit suite passed against that core on darwin-arm64, darwin-x64, linux-x64 and linux-arm64 (CI run 37816045917).\n\n## VS Code extension 1.1.0 — 2026-10-06\n\n*The extension's own version, `theourgia.theourgos`, on the Marketplace for macOS on arm64 and x86_64 and Linux on x86_64 and arm64.* The core pinned at theourgia 1.1.0.\n\n### Changed\n\n- The extension is Theourgos: package `theourgos` under the publisher `theourgia`, id `theourgia.theourgos`, packages named `theourgos-<target>.vsix`. Its commands and settings keep their `theourgia.` names. A 1.0.0 installed by hand from a `.vsix` built before this change has the id `theourgia.theourgia`, which VS Code treats as another extension: let its pending saves go through, or discard them, then uninstall it before installing 1.1.0, since both register the same commands.\n- The core is pinned at theourgia 1.1.0 (3a4ac93).\n- The setting `theourgia.writer` is removed: each window has written its drafts under a name of its own since before 1.0.0, so it decided nothing. A `settings.json` that still sets it shows an unknown setting, and nothing changes.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- The search view answered \"the store did not answer the search\" for every query that found anything, since 1.0.0. A search now lists its hits.\n- A datum-mode block opened as an empty buffer, and an edit saved through it was answered as saved and never reached the code. It now opens read-only, as the datum export writes its library's file, and a file opened under 1.0.0 on a datum block is refused on save instead of appearing to save.\n- A save the core refuses because its block is a datum block, or because a log segment was replaced under the store's daemon, now says which, and for the second says to restart the store's daemon and save again.\n- Search and the hover's mentions ask the core with `--wire`. When every hit is in a superseded or refuted block, the search view says the words are found only in blocks that are not in force, rather than that nothing matches.\n- The package for macOS on x86_64 passes its unit suite against the core it is pinned to, and 1.1.0 is on the Marketplace for all four targets; 1.0.0 was packaged for Apple Silicon only.\n\n## 1.1.0 — 2026-10-05\n\n*26 commits.* Queries and context for agents, read receipts and write premises, class and validity, a change stream, runners for five more languages, and a measured row for macOS on x86_64.\n\n### New verbs\n\n- `query <goal>` answers every binding of a goal's variables over the committed state at one cut. Its facts are twenty relations the store already answers, combined by nineteen rules kept as data; `query --relations` lists both. A query reads no log, runs no code and writes nothing; one whose new work passes a million tuples is refused, never answered in part.\n- `context --for <id> --budget <tokens>` answers what to read before working on a block, within a budget counted as four bytes a token of the answer as the wire prints it, its cut left out, with a receipt that a commit can be given back as its premises.\n- `subscribe changes <rev> [<token>]` keeps a connection to the daemon and prints one line for each publication: blocks added and removed, fields, positions and parents changed, conflicts entered and resolved, edges added and removed. A commit made outside the daemon reaches subscribers within about a second. The MCP shell does not offer it, and without a daemon it answers `needs-daemon`.\n- `commitments` lists the decisions still owed: a decision is open until a live block implements it (a task only once it is done) or its status is done or dropped. `--drifted` names implementations whose content has changed since the link, unless a later link or edit of the decision has seen the change; status, batch, keywords, class and slug do not count.\n- `init --template` or `--template-file`, `template apply` and `template export` make a store from a template of document roots and relations, or give one to a store; the built-in templates are `project` and `memory`. `tasks` lists blocks of kind task.\n- `names <id>` lists the names a code block uses and the libraries a library block imports; `uses <name>` lists the live code blocks that use a name.\n\n### New options and clauses\n\n- `read <id> --cut <cut>` reads a block at a causal cut, written out or as a tag; every `log` entry gives the event's `cut` and `past`.\n- `read --rev` adds the daemon publication the answer was read from, `(rev <n> (daemon \"<token>\"))`, so a subscriber can apply frames by revision.\n- Read receipts: `(cut ...)`, the state a read was answered from, and `(versions ...)`, the version of each block it shows or lists, at the end of an answer. `read`, `refs`, `commitments`, `tasks`, `names` and `uses` carry both; `search`, `grep` and `whereis` add the versions; `outline` and `reach` carry the cut.\n- `--premises <datum>` on every committed write -- insert, set, move, del, link, unlink, tag, batch, commit, import-code, import-md, def and template apply -- with a list of block versions and query digests, or a `receipt` as `context` prints it. When they no longer hold, nothing is written and the write is refused by name.\n- A block's `class`: observation, inference, ruling, verification or external. The relations supersedes, refutes, depends-on, implements, verifies and conflicts-with decide whether a block is valid, superseded, refuted or needs review. A plain `read` of a block that is not valid adds `(validity ...)`; `search`, `grep` and `whereis` leave out superseded and refuted blocks, and `--all-validity` keeps them.\n- `eval --lang` has default runners for `typescript` (node), `go` (go run), `rust` and `c` (compiled, then run) and `java` (single-file launch). `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_TYPESCRIPT`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_GO`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_RUST`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_C` and `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_JAVA` each replace one with a whole runner. Runners still need `THEOURGIA_RUNNERS=on`.\n\n### Platforms\n\n- macOS on x86_64 has its own measured row, read under Rosetta, and is no longer refused; there `stat` and `lstat` bind their `$INODE64` symbols.\n\n### Changed behaviour you may notice\n\n- When validity leaves out every hit, the human output of `search`, `grep` and `whereis` now prints the `(excluded ...)` line instead of nothing. A script that took empty output to mean no match should read `--wire`.\n- `write` on a block whose mode is datum is refused `draft-on-datum-unsupported`, and `commit` refuses a draft that would put text on one. Both used to answer ok, and the edit never took effect. Change a datum block with `def`; `check` reports a datum block that already carries text.\n- A daemon refuses every later write to a writer's log once the segment it was appending to is replaced by a shorter one -- a git checkout of another branch under a running daemon -- until it is restarted. Restart the daemon after switching the store's branch.\n- The answers of `read`, `refs`, `commitments`, `tasks`, `names`, `uses`, `search`, `grep`, `whereis`, `outline` and `reach` end with receipt clauses. The human output is unchanged except for plain `read` and `reach`, which print whole; a script that compares whole `--wire` answers will see the new clauses at the end.\n- Superseded and refuted blocks are left out of `search`, `grep` and `whereis` by default in a store that links those relations. Ask with `--all-validity` to see them.\n- Starting from source takes about 1% longer: the code the premises check adds is compiled at every start (1.1%, the median of three runs of 40 interleaved pairs); the commitments registry added 0.5% before it.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- The daemon sampled a publication's snapshot after its fold had released the store's lock, so a commit by another process in between could go unseen until something else changed.\n- A retry that completes an interrupted plan -- a commit, an import, a def -- wrote its missing members over what other requests had written to the same blocks since, and answered ok. It now checks first and answers `stale-baseline` when a block has moved. An interrupted import that created a file can now be completed; it used to answer `malformed-intent`.\n- `import-md` replaced bytes that were not UTF-8 with U+FFFD and imported the file changed. A file that is not valid UTF-8, or holds a NUL byte, is now skipped and listed under `(skipped ...)`, as `import-code` does.\n- `read --cut` could answer ok from a smaller cut when a record was quarantined between the cut check and the replay; it now refuses `cut-moved`.\n- A code block whose derived fields raised when shown was answered `(error internal ...)` with no block named; the answer now names it.\n- A value written once that merely had a conflict's shape was read as a conflict by `search`, `grep`, references, `commitments` and `tasks`; a field is contested now only when two or more of its candidates survive.\n- A record definition with a malformed field spec could raise when the store read a block's names; it now binds only its type's names. The name walk's shape rules follow what Chez accepts.\n\n### Documentation\n\n- README corrected against the code, with a new opening line.\n- The agent-memory guide, `docs/claude-code-memory.md`, checked against the code.\n\n## 1.0.0 — 2026-09-30\n\n*225 commits.* The first release: a block-graph store where agents and people write together, with a command line, a daemon, an MCP shell and a VS Code extension.\n\n### The store\n\n- A store is a directory of plain files. Each writer appends to its own segments, and the store can live in a git repository; `init` writes its `.gitignore`.\n- 38 verbs, each with a usage line and a one-sentence description that `describe` answers: init, insert, set, move, del, link, unlink, def, outline, read, refs, reach, search, grep, whereis, log, tag, diff, describe, conflicts, write, restore, commit, drafts, diagnostics, discard, batch, import-md, export-md, import-code, export-code, split-suggest, supply, adopt, check, snapshot, publish and eval.\n- Writing goes through drafts: `write` keeps a draft in the writer's own space, and `commit` installs it; a draft whose baseline the block has moved on from is refused `stale-baseline`.\n- The block-editing verbs, `batch` and `tag <name>` accept a request identity, so a client that missed the answer can send the request again and be told what happened.\n- Code and prose sit in one graph: `import-md` and `import-code` read a directory into blocks (Scheme as data with `--datum`), and `export-md` and `export-code` write them back out.\n- `eval` evaluates one expression against the store in a child process with no filesystem and no network; another language runs with `--lang` where the operator has set `THEOURGIA_RUNNERS=on`.\n- `supply` keeps what an editor computed (signatures, calls, diagnostics) in `<store>/derived/`, outside the log.\n- `publish` receives another store's history one segment at a time.\n\n### The daemon\n\n- `theourgia serve` holds a store open and answers over a unix socket; the command line starts one when none is running, and finds its socket by the same rule the daemon used to create it.\n- It answers through the same dispatcher as the command line. `THEOURGIA_LOCAL=1` answers in process instead.\n\n### The MCP shell\n\n- `theourgia-mcp --store <path>` speaks MCP `2025-11-25` over stdio: one tool per verb routed to the daemon, and `eval`, run as the shell's own child.\n- A core refusal comes back as a successful tool result.\n- Each session gets a writer of its own unless `THEOURGIA_WRITER` or `--writer` fixes one.\n\n### The command line\n\n- `theourgia` is the command; `theourgiad`, the daemon program, and `theourgia-mcp`, the MCP shell, sit beside it.\n- `build.ss` compiles the tree and igropyr into a directory of objects, which start about twelve times faster than source.\n- Every verb takes `--store`, `--actor`, `--socket`, `--wire`, `--req` and `--cursor`.\n\n### Platforms and requirements\n\n- Chez Scheme 10.1.0, 10.3.0, 10.4.0 or 10.4.1, libuv and igropyr. On another Chez version the verbs that print a datum refuse with `unsupported-printer-version`.\n- Platforms with a measured row: macOS on arm64, and Linux on x86_64 and on aarch64 with glibc. On any other platform every program exits 75 with `platform-unmeasured`.\n\n### Published to\n\n- Homebrew: the tap `guenchi/theourgia`, whose formula builds this release with Homebrew's Chez Scheme.\n- npm: `theourgia` 1.0.0.\n- The VS Code extension, at its own version 1.0.0, packaged for macOS on Apple Silicon.\n\n### Known not to work\n\n- macOS on Intel, or under Rosetta: the core has no measured row there and exits 75 with `platform-unmeasured`.\n- FreeBSD 15 on amd64 has a measured row, but the daemon does not start there yet.\n- The machine registry never retires an entry: entries for deleted stores stay in it, marked active.\n- The export verbs take no `--cut`: a historical cut with drafts over it has no export."))
4440ff78 (9 1793400282454 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "d9l36a5r.40" src "# Changelog\n\n## 1.3.1 — 2026-10-30\n\n*31 commits.* Splitting and import with symbols on real TypeScript and JavaScript: the ECMAScript scan no longer falls back to one block at a division, a regular expression, a template or a generic (on a real TypeScript tree of 132 files, 28 were split by symbol before and all 132 after), a file the scan cannot check is imported whole instead of being left out, a symbol-cut block carries the symbol's name where the language derives none, and JSX-looking text in strings, comments and regular expressions no longer makes plain JavaScript fall back. Beside it: the daemon yields during blocking file I/O, `context` lists the users of a unit's names, the review channel keeps every text of a packet section, a failed commit says why, and the store tools refuse a few wrong landings by name.\n\n### Import with symbols\n\n- Splitting and `import --symbols`: the ECMAScript scan keeps every reading the text has not disproved (a `/` read both as a division and as a regular expression, template and interpolation frames, TypeScript's postfix `!` and type-argument `>`) and takes a line as top level only when every surviving reading does, so divisions, regular expressions, templates and generics no longer make a file fall back with `lexically-uncertain`; past 32 readings the file falls back as before.\n- A file the scan cannot check (no language entry, or text the scanner does not follow) is imported whole and listed under `symbols-imported-whole`; a file whose cut was checked and found wrong is still left out, under `symbols-refused`.\n- A symbol-cut block the language does not name is titled by the symbol the editor gave.\n- JSX in plain JavaScript is refused by position, not by text: a `<` where an operand is expected is an `opener`, and a `/` read as a division whose next token is `>` is a `self-close`; markup in strings, comments, templates and regular expressions passes.\n- In the table scan of the other languages, a comment no longer ends the statement it follows.\n\n### Daemon\n\n- Blocking file I/O yields to the scheduler; a probe defers while a publication is in progress.\n- A public name spelled like another endpoint's lock is refused.\n\n### Context and working views\n\n- `context` gains a `users` clause after `background`: the live code blocks outside the unit that use a name it defines, each listed whole or counted under the excluded counter `users`; the clause is reserved at the counters' digits, so which hard blocks are shown still depends on the hard material only.\n- A draft new file's provisional id is answered `(error provisional-id (id ..) (reason not-committed))` by every verb that resolves an id over a view; with `--working`, a refusal of the view still comes first.\n- A fold that fails at a named cut is answered in its own class (a busy lock as `store-busy`), not as an unusable cut; `context`'s description says that under `--working` a drafted block's premises are the working view's versions.\n\n### Store and store tools\n\n- A failed commit's answer carries a `(failed ...)` clause naming its cause, where it said only `commit-barrier-failed`.\n- `publish-landing` refuses a landing whose datum libraries changed and names a library with no path by its id; a freeze stays current when nothing that lands has changed; `landing-freeze --tree` compares the freeze with the tested edit directory.\n- `import-git` follows the domain for a range instead of refusing a binary.\n- `supply` and `init` keep an existing `.gitignore` and refuse one that is not a file; `supply --clear` writes nothing it need not; the edit tools find the edit directory's sidecar by one lexical fold.\n\n### Review channel\n\n- A packet section may carry several `(text ...)` entries: joined in packet order with one empty line; `(text)` and `(text #f)` carry none; a non-string text refuses the packet as `packet-malformed`.\n\n### Tests\n\n- The change-stream guard runs real daemons on a real seed and reads answers whole; the guard names the daemon program by a define the spawn reading sees; two timing rows wait for the event, not a clock; the scan-cost row reads medians over three pairs; the two-opener daemon rows read the held loser by pid; the two context base rows leave the users clause out of both sides; eval-local binds its runs before their probes; signal!'s identity guard has its own rows; the mcp-shell stub appends its log line before it answers.\n\n## 1.3.0 — 2026-10-28\n\n*336 commits.* Manteia, the rule language, completed (kinds, stored derived relations, aggregation and stratified negation, incremental advance); structural drafts; a review channel that carries a frozen candidate; the daemon answering from its publication with one session kept across requests, started by the MCP shell and leaving when idle; a store identity that survives a remount; the reducer's pending queue and gates made linear; and the store's git bridge (import-git, publish-landing, the edit tools).\n\n### Manteia, the rule language\n\n- Column kinds for the rule language, checked statically; kind containment normalises both sides; goals dispatched to one handler per kind in three environments.\n- Stored derived relations, declared by a verb (`derive`) and checked where they are used; a resent rule is its replay; recursive relations kept whole; availability by one search over the reach, the shortest through-chain reported; a relation's through-chain built once a table.\n- `not`, `count`, `sum`, `min` and `max` over a goal; stored relations stratified; a form's table named by its occurrence and group; emptiness read goal by goal.\n- `session-advance`: a session advanced to a later state, its positive strata maintained by delete-and-rederive, the auxiliary session's work charged to the advance; each callee collected once.\n- An `and` nested in an `and` is its goals in place; `supply` from any tool with its version.\n- The README's language chapter, construct by construct, its example answers measured.\n- A text-mode Scheme file's library and imports are read from its header through its file holder (`in-library`, `imports` and the new `file-imports`, all external), by a new bounded reader library, `(theourgia reader)`; a `holder` fact relates a code block to its file holder, and `def-for`'s no-library clause holds only within one holder, so a script's own definitions serve its own pieces and nothing else. `uses-name` for a text block of a language with a reader binds what its complete forms bind, `(syntactic (reading forms))`. `context` folds a name's definitions past the first two into a `folded` clause, takes `--names <n>` (repeatable, reported in `asked`), scopes a file holder or any non-code target to the code blocks under it, and takes `--budget min+<t>`; the session trace counts, per call, definitions-index builds, fact builds by relation, preferred candidates by step, and whether the session was reused or why it was dropped.\n\n### Daemon\n\n- `query` and `context` are published reads of the store process: the publication made current first, no replay while the store has not changed; a query after an outside write sees it (the `theourgia` hooks rely on it).\n- One query session kept by the daemon across requests: reused on the same publication, advanced when the publication moves forward, replaced otherwise; `serve --session-size <entries>` (0 disables); `(name-use syntactic)` reported per answer; the one exception to byte-identity with the local route: a query the local route refuses for its budget may be answered by the daemon.\n- A store whose directory is removed is drained and the daemon exits `store-gone`; an absent store creates nothing.\n- A plain command starts no daemon. With nobody listening, the command line starts `core.sc --frame` as a child, writes it the request frame a daemon would have been sent and reads its answer as a daemon's (the same 32 MiB ceiling, no answer deadline, so the request waits for the store's lock), and nothing is left running; a daemon is started by `serve`, or by the MCP shell for the session it serves. A request answered in a child meets the in-process route's differences: `describe` adds no template clause, the lock is waited for rather than refused `store-busy`, the store is read as it is rather than as last published, `--rev` is refused `no-published-state` and `subscribe` `needs-daemon`. A daemon's connection and the child read, check and dispatch a frame through one library, `(theourgia frame)`. A store without its lock file answers `absent` on a plain command; `serve-start-failed` is only the MCP shell's start relaying `serve`'s answer.\n- `search` answers over the state the dispatcher hands it, as every read verb does, instead of opening and reducing the store on each call, so a resident daemon no longer folds the whole store again for every search. Over a connection it answers the publication as it stands, as `read` does.\n- `serve --idle-exit <seconds>` and `--idle-grace-ms <milliseconds>` (default 200, at most 5000, a larger grace refused `too-large`): a daemon leaves by itself after that long with no connection open and no request running. It unlinks its public link first, takes for the grace the connections the kernel had already completed, and exits 0 with `(idle-exit (after <seconds>) (refused-accepts <n>) (callback-raised <n>))`; a late connection cancels the exit (`idle-resumed`), and a resume that cannot happen drains, exiting 75 with `(idle-exit (reason resume-failed <cause>))`. A daemon started with the options names them in its `serving` line.\n- The MCP shell is the opener: it starts its daemon with `--idle-exit` from `THEOURGIA_IDLE_EXIT` (1800 seconds when unset, 0 never; any other value refused `bad-environment` before a start) and `--idle-grace-ms 200`, gives each request one deadline and at most two attempts (a `serve-busy` start retried after 100 ms, a daemon gone before the request's own connect started once more), and then answers `connect-failed` with `(carried-out no)`; a written request is never sent again.\n- The socket path is a symbolic link to the daemon's private socket, `<socket>.d/<pid>`, in a directory of its own whose `.endpoint` marker reserves it for the endpoint. A start reclaims what an earlier daemon of the same endpoint left; it is refused `serve-path-reserved` inside another endpoint's marked directory, `socket-dir-missing` where it would make such a directory, and `socket-path-too-long`, exit 2, when the path leaves no room for the private name. A bind or publish that fails after the store is ready exits `listener-down`.\n- Every drain waits for its listener to hand over the connections it accepted, so a connection accepted just before a drain is answered `draining` or served, never closed with nothing.\n\n### Reducer and admission\n\n- Gated arrivals: an arrival's work no longer grows with the number of standing gates (the square of the standing gates per arrival before, the whole fold the cube).\n- The pending queue indexed by what each record waits for: arrivals wake only their waiters; a seq or dep outside the log's envelope refused at `reduce-apply!`; two copies of one event leave the heap in arrival order.\n- An arrival's work independent of the standing gates (the gates as a table in admission; the notes exact); the declaration tables indexed by name and replayed in linear time; the blocks and the pasts found by id; the block structure maintained, not recomputed; the outline's order total.\n- Admission judges only what an arrival can change; a single record naming a plan is judged again when its plan's binding changes.\n\n### Structural drafts\n\n- A draft can create a file or delete one: `working-new` and `working-delete` beside the block drafts, each with its own version and cut; `commit --working-new <provisional>=<version>` and `--working-delete <holder>=<version>`; the commit makes the holder and its pieces by the construction import-git shares, in one batch with the block drafts, and answers `(created ...)` and `(pieces ...)`, also on a replay. edit-sync turns an added file into a new-file draft and a removed one into a delete draft, refusing paths outside the store route's domain (dot-named, not text, a mode other than 100644, a case collision in the resulting tree) and any live structural draft the export does not account for (`draft-changed`, remedy: export again). A new file is shown by the export only (not by outline, context or eval --working).\n\n### The review channel\n\n- `scope --freeze <dir>` reviews a frozen candidate (the copies hold the drafts' bytes, a `before` section the committed text) and writes no main letter; `--packet` adds the questions; `--roots all` copies the whole tree; `--home <h>` gives the scoped store its own home so the reviewer writes its verdict there. review-results answers `pending` until a verdict exists and refuses an incomplete one by name; sources must include a candidate copy. `collect-into --letter new --map <LANDED.sexp> --to-writer <W>` brings the verdict into the main store after the landing, resolving provisional ids and pieces, and turns the reviewer's proposals into drafts for writer W. Cuts are compared as sets. The mail handler is now contrib/mail-hook.sh (codex-mail.sh still runs it), and contrib/claude-hooks.json runs it for Claude Code with `THEOURGIA_ACTOR=claude`; it exits 1 with a line when its query fails, since a failed query is not an empty mailbox.\n\n### Store tools and the git bridge\n\n- `import-git`: a git range absorbed into the store as committed blocks and a tag; one comparison-domain judgement for publication and import.\n- `publish-landing`: the files a landing changed published from the store as one git commit; `landing-commit` tags the start of a landing; `edit-export` and `edit-sync` turn an agent's file edits into a writer's drafts; dump and restore a writer's drafts; the freeze as one script checking the view its tree came from.\n- `changed-files`: the files whose exported bytes or presence differ between two cuts; `locate`: a file's byte or line range, or one diff hunk, to the blocks holding it; `position`: the local writer's last sequence; a named tag's answer carries the cut it bound.\n- Exports at a past cut (`export-code`, `export-md --cut`), judged against the daemon's publication as a read at a cut is.\n- A draft whose base fell behind says so: `drafts --writer W` names each after its items, `(behind <block> (based-on <v>) (committed <v>))`. `landing-freeze` brings every draft onto the applied cut before it dumps (a draft whose block did not change is moved, one whose block changed is merged by a line-based three-way merge, or the freeze stops with `drafts-behind-conflict` naming every conflicting block), so a record committed after the drafts, a review letter included, no longer leaves every draft to be rebased by hand; `landing-freeze --check <dir>` validates a frozen directory and answers current, refreeze or the conflict, so a landing script can stop before its start tag.\n- `landing-freeze` moves a structural draft behind an unrelated commit onto the applied cut: a new file whose path no live file holds there, or a delete whose file's baseline there is still the draft's `based-on`, is rewritten in place at the applied cut with the same bytes, path and language and a version recomputed for that cut, under one hold of the writer's draft lock and only while it is still the version the freeze judged (a draft written again since is refused `structural-changed`, not overwritten). Nothing is removed first: a refusal or a failure before the rename leaves the entry where it was, a failed directory flush after it leaves the whole new entry at the applied cut, and either way the freeze is refused `structural-move-refused`. `REBASED.sexp` records the move as `(moved <key> (from-cut <cut>) (to-cut <cut>))`. A new file whose path a live file holds at the applied cut, or a delete whose file is gone or has changed there, still stops the freeze with `drafts-behind-conflict`.\n\n### Reading and rendering\n\n- A text-mode code block's src and doc given as text; a src held as bytes renders and is measured as its text; a draft beginning with a UTF-8 byte-order mark commits as its bytes; `whereis` nearest finds a name in another case.\n- `search` over a long query, and the relevant step of `context --for`, cost what the hits cost: a scan drops a block at the first token none of its fields takes and compares characters where they stand, without a copy per position, and the relevant step asks with each token of the text once. On a development store of 17,000 blocks, the context of a 30 KB block went from 420 s to under 4 s, with the same hits, scores and snippets for every query.\n- `context --working` assembles the material over the request writer's working view, the committed state at the drafts' cut with that writer's drafts laid over it, in a fresh query session, never the daemon's kept one. The answer carries `(working \"<writer>\")` after `for`, counted in the budget; a refusal of the view (`writer-required`, `invalid-working-writer`, `working-unavailable`, `working-draft-unreadable`) is the answer, and an id the view does not hold is answered as `read` answers it over that view. Drafts are not changes, so validity and the other hard queries answer the committed rows; the premises of drafted blocks are the overlay's and are not handed to a commit.\n\n### Store identity\n\n- A remount is not a move: the device is compared last, and a store whose directory changed only its device (a reboot can do that) is written as the same instance, `instance.sexp` rewritten with the live device under the store lock and the change kept in a history of the last eight remounts; `adopt` on such a store answers `(ok (reason remount) (device <old> <new>))` without deriving or retiring a writer. A copy elsewhere has another inode and is refused for it as before. The limit: a block-level clone of a whole volume keeps its inode numbers and is taken for a remount; `cp -R` makes new files, and adopting that copy gives a store of its own.\n- `check` reports the store's identity as `(instance (machine <m>) (device <d>) (inode <i>) (nonce <n>) [(remounts ...)])`, and a writer whose highest segment is empty after its last record's segment as `(empty-segment <n>)`, the state an interrupted rotation leaves; neither changes the verdict.\n\n### Log, ffi, platform\n\n- An append keeps its own writer's prefix; the tail check lists segments without a stat per entry; the kept prefix seeds past an older empty segment.\n- `path-stat` and `fd-stat`; a child receives this process's environment on every platform, FreeBSD included; reading the environment fails as a spawn failure.\n- A test-only timing seam on the write path (the wrappers a library of their own, decided at expansion).\n\n### Transport and CLI\n\n- `--help` and `-h` answer the verb's usage and touch nothing; the verb libraries entered on first use; a registered verb handed the dispatcher's helpers; a transport option on the stream's route refused by name; a trace line written whole.\n- Programs compiled as program objects at optimize-level 2.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- `split-suggest` reads Scheme character literals, so `#\\(` opens nothing and a file with one is no longer a single block; a Scheme library's body is cut at its definitions.\n- A `define-values` definition names its formals, so `whereis` finds it as a definition.\n- A marked export whose last body row would be a marker line but for a trailing carriage return, followed by a pad, now escapes that row; such a file decoded as `invalid-padding` before.\n- `context` finds the blocks relevant to a committed text held as bytes.\n- `adopt` on a store with no local writer is refused `no-local-writer`, before any step; it answered `internal`.\n- `write` given only half a baseline names the missing half: `--based-on` alone is refused `cut-missing`, `--working-cut` alone `based-on-missing`, a working parent's likewise.\n- An error raised out of a verb is answered with the dispatch's clause on every route; the command line ended on an uncaught exception.\n- `diff` checks that each replay covers its cut, and is refused `cut-moved` when one does not.\n- An endpoint selector whose field clause held `_` matched nothing, so every link under its relation was refused `relation-endpoint`. `_` now holds for any value of a field the block carries (a contested field holds none), and a new declaration that writes a variable there is refused `selector-value-not-literal`. An earlier declaration with `_` now holds for any value; nothing stored is reinterpreted.\n- The MCP shell refused `tools/list` whenever it carried params, answering \"Pagination is not available\", so a client that sends params with no cursor (Codex does) could not start; it now serves the whole list and refuses only a cursor with a value. `contrib/doorbell.sh` sends the Enter as its own keystroke after the line, which a combined send left unsubmitted in some terminals.\n- A batch of a few hundred inserts under a rule naming `field+` was refused `rule-unevaluable` (`query-budget`, `field+`) before a selector was asked: a write's facts (`kind+`, `field+`, `edge+`, `edge-kind+`) were read by scanning the whole relation and charged its full size on every dispatch. They are now read by their bound arguments and charged the rows matched each time the goal is dispatched; an over-budget join is still refused.\n- A writer directory copied in from another checkout that sorted first was taken as the local writer, so this copy's writes went into another instance's log. The local writer is now chosen by ownership: a live writer whose `owner.sexp` names the `(machine nonce)` of this copy's `instance.sexp` is preferred by every write, `position`, a commit's cursor and `adopt`, and name order decides only when none does; a request whose writer comes to be passed over while it runs is refused `writer-superseded` (remedy `reopen`). The verbs that change drafts refuse a `--writer` owned by another instance (`foreign-writer`, remedy `draft-writer`), and `check` reports `writer-owner-foreign`, `writer-owner-unverified`, `instance-absent` and `instance-unavailable` without changing its verdict.\n- A write refused for the store's identity left an empty next segment behind, and the `adopt` that followed named a prefix in no segment, which `check` then read as damage for ever. A write is now admitted before it cuts a torn tail or rotates, and `adopt`'s prefix names the segment of the last applied record.\n- A working view asked for a writer with no drafts, through `context`, `eval`, an export or the drafts listing, no longer creates the writer's area and lock file: the view looks first, without the lock, for the writer's `working/` or `structural/` directory, and with neither answers the committed state and creates nothing (`read --working` never created it).\n\n### Tests\n\n- The test runner gives fixtures a home of their own: `HOME` is the run's scratch home and `THEOURGIA_HOME` is unset, so no fixture writes the user's machine registry; the test home links the `.rustup` and `.cargo` directories of the runner's own home when they exist, so toolchains that resolve from `HOME` still run.\n- The fixture launcher reads the anchor's process group back instead of trusting an errno: a failed `setpgid` with the group set goes on with a note, another group stops the launch as `ANCHOR GROUP NOT SET`, and only `ESRCH` is `ANCHOR DIED`. Its test-only `--fault-setpgid` gains the `anchor-skips` and `anchor-late` modes.\n- The interleave row of `unreadable-acknowledge-routes` waits for the search's answer on the monotonic clock instead of a count of shell sleeps, which a slow runner stretched past the daemon's hold.\n- Two `context-verb` rows pin a working answer's used tokens against what it rendered, and that its hard queries are asked at the drafts' cut.\n- The change-stream guard masks the device and inode in `check`'s instance clause, which differ between any two store directories, and nothing else.\n\n### Known at this release\n\n- The median start-up, 668 ms over ten runs, is above its 637 ms budget; recorded, with a profiling item open.\n\n## 1.2.0 — 2026-10-11\n\n*156 commits.* Mail and review between sessions of any vendor through a store, rules and relation effects declared as data and judged before every write, a first import cut at an editor's symbols, stale verifications named by `context`, and a run of fixes found by the first two measurements on the real tree.\n\n### New verbs\n\n- `scope <dir> --cut <cut> --roots <id> --for <actor>` makes a store for one reader holding exactly the blocks under the roots as they were at the cut, with a letter copy (`to`, `status`, `cut`, `roots`, `scope`, `baseline`) and one letter in this store; `review-results` reads the reader's verdict and findings back as one datum; `collect-into <letter> --results <datum>` and `collect <dir>` bring them under the letter here, each finding's `about` edge on the original block. A message is a block of kind `doc` with `to` and `status`; reading one's mail is a query. `contrib/` holds the Codex hooks, the doorbell and the reader's AGENTS.md. `THEOURGIA_SOCKET` names the main store's daemon for `scope` and `collect`.\n- `relation <name> --as <kind> [--from <selector>] [--to <selector>]` declares what a relation name does in this store: one of the six relations with an effect, or `nothing`; the lifecycle reads the declarations, and a reason names the relation.\n- `rule <name> --on <kind> [--where <goal>] --must|--must-not <goal>` declares a rule of this store in the query language; every committed write is judged against the rules before it is written, by a rehearsal of the write on a clone of the state, and refused with the rule's name. `--builtin citation-coverage` enables the built-in rule. `template apply` installs a template's rules with its roots; the `project` template declares `cover`.\n- `import-code --symbols <symbols-file>` splits the FIRST import of a file with no marker lines at the symbols an editor collected, into code blocks under its file block; a file that carries marker lines follows its markers.\n\n### New options and clauses\n\n- `context` names a stale verification: for a block in scope whose `verifies` edge's target has moved past it, the notes carry `(stale-verification <m> verifies <c>)` and the receipt holds the target, read through the new query relation `verified-moved`; the human output prints the receipt on one line, as `--wire` writes it.\n- `commit` answers `behind-records`, how far each writer that moved since the writer's baseline is, in records.\n- `check` answers `(undecodable-text ((<id> <field> ...) ...))` naming the blocks whose stored text cannot be decoded; a text block beginning with a UTF-8 byte-order mark is text, and `search` and `grep` read it.\n- `eval` refuses an output limit by naming the option, cuts what a limit carries to the limit, and takes `--under` a library id; `eval --working` refuses a draft on a datum block by name.\n- `set <id> mode datum` from the command line stores the symbol, as `batch` does.\n- A datum block takes no `src` by any intent's spelling or order; a batch is read as a set for the datum rule.\n\n### Changed behaviour you may notice\n\n- A delete's targets include the live far ends of the deleted block's edges, so a rule over a review that cites a result judges the deletion of that result.\n- A field set to the symbol `omit` reads as itself; it was read as absent.\n- The history's order is delivery order, and the comments and the two readers of it say so and agree on every route.\n- A plan another writer made is read as its members are declared: five readers unwrap an `expect` wrapper through one procedure; a revoked plan's wrapped member is restored from its text.\n- The daemon's own repair of a torn tail replaces what it knows of the segment, and records the snapshot only when it is at least the cut's length; what it knew of the segment is kept only when the segment shrank or its new snapshot cannot be read.\n- The transport refusal of the MCP shell names its option, and the tools say they are bound to a store.\n- `def` refuses a name the library already defines.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- `supply` refuses a datum library's file by name instead of answering stale.\n- `state-revoked` compares versions as the strings they are.\n- A write declaration too short for its verb is asked its shape first, instead of being read as a different request.\n- An insert refused for both datum and `src` carries no block clause; `eval` states its busy pool before usage.\n- `check`'s reserved-relation records are sorted by event, the same on every route.\n\n### Test-only hooks\n\n- `THEOURGIA_PROBE=off` starts a test daemon without its timer probe; read only by an injection build, any other value refused at load.\n\n### Documentation\n\n- The README gains \"Mail and review between sessions\" (the mailbox, `scope`, `review-results`, `collect-into`, `collect`, a review round, Codex as a reader, what this does not protect against, where this runs), the `relation` and `rule` sections, the rules' place in `template`, and the environment variable table's new rows.\n\n## VS Code extension 1.1.1 — 2026-10-09\n\n*The extension's own version, `theourgia.theourgos`, published on the Marketplace for macOS on arm64 and x86_64 and Linux on x86_64 and arm64.* Import with Symbols, on a core pinned at theourgia be42914.\n\n### Added\n\n- Import with Symbols: a folder or a source file is imported into the store with the core's `import-code --symbols`, and the first import of each file is split at the editor's top-level symbols into a file block and its code blocks, with no review copy first. The report in the output channel places each new block at its lines, and gives a file the core refused to split with the line and column of the byte it names. A file with unsaved edits stops the command before anything is sent.\n\n### The core it runs\n\n- The core is pinned at theourgia be42914, ten commits after 1.1.0, which adds `import-code --symbols`.\n- The core checks every cut of a first import split at symbols: a cut must fall at the top level of the file, outside its protected prefix (a shebang, a coding line, a byte-order mark), so a symbol that starts inside a body, a string, a block comment or an open bracket refuses that file instead of cutting it. The report names the refusal: `symbols-not-top-level`, `symbols-unscanned`, `symbols-in-prefix`, `symbols-unchecked` or `symbols-no-file`.\n- The extension's unit suite passed against that core on darwin-arm64, darwin-x64, linux-x64 and linux-arm64 (CI run 37816045917).\n\n## VS Code extension 1.1.0 — 2026-10-06\n\n*The extension's own version, `theourgia.theourgos`, on the Marketplace for macOS on arm64 and x86_64 and Linux on x86_64 and arm64.* The core pinned at theourgia 1.1.0.\n\n### Changed\n\n- The extension is Theourgos: package `theourgos` under the publisher `theourgia`, id `theourgia.theourgos`, packages named `theourgos-<target>.vsix`. Its commands and settings keep their `theourgia.` names. A 1.0.0 installed by hand from a `.vsix` built before this change has the id `theourgia.theourgia`, which VS Code treats as another extension: let its pending saves go through, or discard them, then uninstall it before installing 1.1.0, since both register the same commands.\n- The core is pinned at theourgia 1.1.0 (3a4ac93).\n- The setting `theourgia.writer` is removed: each window has written its drafts under a name of its own since before 1.0.0, so it decided nothing. A `settings.json` that still sets it shows an unknown setting, and nothing changes.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- The search view answered \"the store did not answer the search\" for every query that found anything, since 1.0.0. A search now lists its hits.\n- A datum-mode block opened as an empty buffer, and an edit saved through it was answered as saved and never reached the code. It now opens read-only, as the datum export writes its library's file, and a file opened under 1.0.0 on a datum block is refused on save instead of appearing to save.\n- A save the core refuses because its block is a datum block, or because a log segment was replaced under the store's daemon, now says which, and for the second says to restart the store's daemon and save again.\n- Search and the hover's mentions ask the core with `--wire`. When every hit is in a superseded or refuted block, the search view says the words are found only in blocks that are not in force, rather than that nothing matches.\n- The package for macOS on x86_64 passes its unit suite against the core it is pinned to, and 1.1.0 is on the Marketplace for all four targets; 1.0.0 was packaged for Apple Silicon only.\n\n## 1.1.0 — 2026-10-05\n\n*26 commits.* Queries and context for agents, read receipts and write premises, class and validity, a change stream, runners for five more languages, and a measured row for macOS on x86_64.\n\n### New verbs\n\n- `query <goal>` answers every binding of a goal's variables over the committed state at one cut. Its facts are twenty relations the store already answers, combined by nineteen rules kept as data; `query --relations` lists both. A query reads no log, runs no code and writes nothing; one whose new work passes a million tuples is refused, never answered in part.\n- `context --for <id> --budget <tokens>` answers what to read before working on a block, within a budget counted as four bytes a token of the answer as the wire prints it, its cut left out, with a receipt that a commit can be given back as its premises.\n- `subscribe changes <rev> [<token>]` keeps a connection to the daemon and prints one line for each publication: blocks added and removed, fields, positions and parents changed, conflicts entered and resolved, edges added and removed. A commit made outside the daemon reaches subscribers within about a second. The MCP shell does not offer it, and without a daemon it answers `needs-daemon`.\n- `commitments` lists the decisions still owed: a decision is open until a live block implements it (a task only once it is done) or its status is done or dropped. `--drifted` names implementations whose content has changed since the link, unless a later link or edit of the decision has seen the change; status, batch, keywords, class and slug do not count.\n- `init --template` or `--template-file`, `template apply` and `template export` make a store from a template of document roots and relations, or give one to a store; the built-in templates are `project` and `memory`. `tasks` lists blocks of kind task.\n- `names <id>` lists the names a code block uses and the libraries a library block imports; `uses <name>` lists the live code blocks that use a name.\n\n### New options and clauses\n\n- `read <id> --cut <cut>` reads a block at a causal cut, written out or as a tag; every `log` entry gives the event's `cut` and `past`.\n- `read --rev` adds the daemon publication the answer was read from, `(rev <n> (daemon \"<token>\"))`, so a subscriber can apply frames by revision.\n- Read receipts: `(cut ...)`, the state a read was answered from, and `(versions ...)`, the version of each block it shows or lists, at the end of an answer. `read`, `refs`, `commitments`, `tasks`, `names` and `uses` carry both; `search`, `grep` and `whereis` add the versions; `outline` and `reach` carry the cut.\n- `--premises <datum>` on every committed write -- insert, set, move, del, link, unlink, tag, batch, commit, import-code, import-md, def and template apply -- with a list of block versions and query digests, or a `receipt` as `context` prints it. When they no longer hold, nothing is written and the write is refused by name.\n- A block's `class`: observation, inference, ruling, verification or external. The relations supersedes, refutes, depends-on, implements, verifies and conflicts-with decide whether a block is valid, superseded, refuted or needs review. A plain `read` of a block that is not valid adds `(validity ...)`; `search`, `grep` and `whereis` leave out superseded and refuted blocks, and `--all-validity` keeps them.\n- `eval --lang` has default runners for `typescript` (node), `go` (go run), `rust` and `c` (compiled, then run) and `java` (single-file launch). `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_TYPESCRIPT`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_GO`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_RUST`, `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_C` and `THEOURGIA_RUNNER_JAVA` each replace one with a whole runner. Runners still need `THEOURGIA_RUNNERS=on`.\n\n### Platforms\n\n- macOS on x86_64 has its own measured row, read under Rosetta, and is no longer refused; there `stat` and `lstat` bind their `$INODE64` symbols.\n\n### Changed behaviour you may notice\n\n- When validity leaves out every hit, the human output of `search`, `grep` and `whereis` now prints the `(excluded ...)` line instead of nothing. A script that took empty output to mean no match should read `--wire`.\n- `write` on a block whose mode is datum is refused `draft-on-datum-unsupported`, and `commit` refuses a draft that would put text on one. Both used to answer ok, and the edit never took effect. Change a datum block with `def`; `check` reports a datum block that already carries text.\n- A daemon refuses every later write to a writer's log once the segment it was appending to is replaced by a shorter one -- a git checkout of another branch under a running daemon -- until it is restarted. Restart the daemon after switching the store's branch.\n- The answers of `read`, `refs`, `commitments`, `tasks`, `names`, `uses`, `search`, `grep`, `whereis`, `outline` and `reach` end with receipt clauses. The human output is unchanged except for plain `read` and `reach`, which print whole; a script that compares whole `--wire` answers will see the new clauses at the end.\n- Superseded and refuted blocks are left out of `search`, `grep` and `whereis` by default in a store that links those relations. Ask with `--all-validity` to see them.\n- Starting from source takes about 1% longer: the code the premises check adds is compiled at every start (1.1%, the median of three runs of 40 interleaved pairs); the commitments registry added 0.5% before it.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- The daemon sampled a publication's snapshot after its fold had released the store's lock, so a commit by another process in between could go unseen until something else changed.\n- A retry that completes an interrupted plan -- a commit, an import, a def -- wrote its missing members over what other requests had written to the same blocks since, and answered ok. It now checks first and answers `stale-baseline` when a block has moved. An interrupted import that created a file can now be completed; it used to answer `malformed-intent`.\n- `import-md` replaced bytes that were not UTF-8 with U+FFFD and imported the file changed. A file that is not valid UTF-8, or holds a NUL byte, is now skipped and listed under `(skipped ...)`, as `import-code` does.\n- `read --cut` could answer ok from a smaller cut when a record was quarantined between the cut check and the replay; it now refuses `cut-moved`.\n- A code block whose derived fields raised when shown was answered `(error internal ...)` with no block named; the answer now names it.\n- A value written once that merely had a conflict's shape was read as a conflict by `search`, `grep`, references, `commitments` and `tasks`; a field is contested now only when two or more of its candidates survive.\n- A record definition with a malformed field spec could raise when the store read a block's names; it now binds only its type's names. The name walk's shape rules follow what Chez accepts.\n\n### Documentation\n\n- README corrected against the code, with a new opening line.\n- The agent-memory guide, `docs/claude-code-memory.md`, checked against the code.\n\n## 1.0.0 — 2026-09-30\n\n*225 commits.* The first release: a block-graph store where agents and people write together, with a command line, a daemon, an MCP shell and a VS Code extension.\n\n### The store\n\n- A store is a directory of plain files. Each writer appends to its own segments, and the store can live in a git repository; `init` writes its `.gitignore`.\n- 38 verbs, each with a usage line and a one-sentence description that `describe` answers: init, insert, set, move, del, link, unlink, def, outline, read, refs, reach, search, grep, whereis, log, tag, diff, describe, conflicts, write, restore, commit, drafts, diagnostics, discard, batch, import-md, export-md, import-code, export-code, split-suggest, supply, adopt, check, snapshot, publish and eval.\n- Writing goes through drafts: `write` keeps a draft in the writer's own space, and `commit` installs it; a draft whose baseline the block has moved on from is refused `stale-baseline`.\n- The block-editing verbs, `batch` and `tag <name>` accept a request identity, so a client that missed the answer can send the request again and be told what happened.\n- Code and prose sit in one graph: `import-md` and `import-code` read a directory into blocks (Scheme as data with `--datum`), and `export-md` and `export-code` write them back out.\n- `eval` evaluates one expression against the store in a child process with no filesystem and no network; another language runs with `--lang` where the operator has set `THEOURGIA_RUNNERS=on`.\n- `supply` keeps what an editor computed (signatures, calls, diagnostics) in `<store>/derived/`, outside the log.\n- `publish` receives another store's history one segment at a time.\n\n### The daemon\n\n- `theourgia serve` holds a store open and answers over a unix socket; the command line starts one when none is running, and finds its socket by the same rule the daemon used to create it.\n- It answers through the same dispatcher as the command line. `THEOURGIA_LOCAL=1` answers in process instead.\n\n### The MCP shell\n\n- `theourgia-mcp --store <path>` speaks MCP `2025-11-25` over stdio: one tool per verb routed to the daemon, and `eval`, run as the shell's own child.\n- A core refusal comes back as a successful tool result.\n- Each session gets a writer of its own unless `THEOURGIA_WRITER` or `--writer` fixes one.\n\n### The command line\n\n- `theourgia` is the command; `theourgiad`, the daemon program, and `theourgia-mcp`, the MCP shell, sit beside it.\n- `build.ss` compiles the tree and igropyr into a directory of objects, which start about twelve times faster than source.\n- Every verb takes `--store`, `--actor`, `--socket`, `--wire`, `--req` and `--cursor`.\n\n### Platforms and requirements\n\n- Chez Scheme 10.1.0, 10.3.0, 10.4.0 or 10.4.1, libuv and igropyr. On another Chez version the verbs that print a datum refuse with `unsupported-printer-version`.\n- Platforms with a measured row: macOS on arm64, and Linux on x86_64 and on aarch64 with glibc. On any other platform every program exits 75 with `platform-unmeasured`.\n\n### Published to\n\n- Homebrew: the tap `guenchi/theourgia`, whose formula builds this release with Homebrew's Chez Scheme.\n- npm: `theourgia` 1.0.0.\n- The VS Code extension, at its own version 1.0.0, packaged for macOS on Apple Silicon.\n\n### Known not to work\n\n- macOS on Intel, or under Rosetta: the core has no measured row there and exits 75 with `platform-unmeasured`.\n- FreeBSD 15 on amd64 has a measured row, but the daemon does not start there yet.\n- The machine registry never retires an entry: entries for deleted stores stay in it, marked active.\n- The export verbs take no `--cut`: a historical cut with drafts over it has no export."))
b3494798 (10 1793413338487 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "d9l36a5r.58" src "`query <goal>` answers every binding of a goal's variables over the committed state at one cut. Its facts are 26 relations the store already answers -- a block's kind, class, validity, version, title and fields, edges, parents and text references, libraries, a code block's file holder and a text file's header imports, definitions, name use, the lifecycle's reasons and states, and search scores -- with 4 tests, and 21 relations defined by the library's 43 rules combine them; `query --relations` lists them, then the store's declared and stored relations. The language is Manteia (below). A query reads no log, runs no code and writes nothing, and one whose new work passes its budget is refused, never answered in part. An answer carries a digest of its rows, which a write can give back as a premise. Through the daemon, `query` and `context` are answered from the daemon's publication, made current first when the disk has moved past it, so a query after a write made outside the daemon sees that write.\n\nA Scheme file stored in text mode takes its library and imports from its header, read by the store's own bounded reader from its pieces joined in order: a code block in no library block is `in-library` its file's library, and a script's pieces see the libraries it imports and no others. A definition in no library serves only the pieces of its own file, the code blocks with the same `holder`. Name use reads such a block as forms where its text reads, `(name-use syntactic (reading forms))`, and falls back to its tokens, `(lexing whole-text)` with a reason, where it does not.\n\n`context --for <id> --budget <tokens>` answers what to read before working on a block, within a budget counted as four bytes a token of the answer as the wire prints it, its cut left out: the blocks it must show, with their roles and validity, the decisions still unsettled for the block, implementations in review and contradictions in its scope, then definitions, evidence, dependents, the users of the block's unit and ancestors as the budget allows. The users are a clause of their own after `background`, `(users (listed <k>) (of <n>) ...)`: the live code blocks outside the unit that use a name the unit defines, by the name-use table `uses` reads, each listed whole or counted under the excluded counter `users`. The clause is reserved at the counters' digits, so it never changes which of the blocks that must be shown are shown, or how. A count above 999999 is shown as 999999 and its clause ends with `(capped)`. Its receipt holds a premise for every block it had to show and the digests of the five queries that chose them, so a commit given that receipt is refused if any of that has changed; what it adds as the budget allows is not in the receipt. A verification whose target has moved past it is named in the notes as `(stale-verification <m> verifies <c>)`, and its target is held by the receipt. The human output prints the receipt on one line, exactly as `--wire` writes it, ready for `commit --premises`.\n\n`--working` answers over the request writer's working view instead, the one `read --working` reads: the committed state at the drafts' cut with that writer's drafts laid over it, in a fresh query session, never the one the daemon keeps. The answer carries `(working \"<writer>\")` after `for`, counted in the budget. A refusal of the view (`writer-required`, `invalid-working-writer`, `working-unavailable`, `working-draft-unreadable`) is the answer, and an id the view does not hold is answered as `read` answers it over that view. A working view changes block text and the names it uses, not validity: drafts are not changes, so the five queries answer the committed rows. Its premises for drafted blocks are the overlay's versions and are not handed to a commit; a committed answer's are. Without `--working` context writes nothing; with it, it reads the writer's drafts under the writer's draft lock, and for a writer with no draft area it creates nothing.\n\nWhen a name the block uses has more than two definitions left, context shows the first two and counts the rest in `(folded ...)`. `--names <name>`, repeated, enters the definitions of each given name in the block's scope before anything else that is optional, and `(asked ...)` says which are defined only in a block that must be shown or nowhere; for a file block, or any block that is not code, the scope is that of every code block under it. `--budget min+<t>` asks for the smallest budget the request needs plus t tokens, and the budget clause shows the number it came to."))
2c88f76d (11 1793413340551 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "8pniyrtk.1a" src "write puts a proposed next version of a block into a writer's own slot. It is not in the log, and a read sees it only when it asks for that writer's drafts. commit, in describe's own words, installs a writer's drafts into the store as one change; the process that owns the store carries commits out one at a time, so the outcome of a race is decided rather than interleaved. A draft records the version it was made against; if the block has moved on since, the commit is refused.\n\nA draft stands still while the store moves on. Every committed record moves the store's applied cut, whoever wrote it and whatever block it is on -- a review letter as much as a code change; a draft never moves it. When a commit changed the drafted block itself, the draft is behind: `drafts --writer W` reads it `(fresh #f)` and names it again after its items, `(behind <block> (based-on <v>) (committed <v>))`. A commit on other blocks only moves the cut, and leaves no draft behind.\n\nA draft can also create a file or delete one: a new file is a path and its bytes, named by a provisional id, `new-` and the first 56 hex digits of the sha256 of the path; a deleted file is its holder. `commit` takes them only when named, `--working-new <provisional>=<version>` and `--working-delete <holder>=<version>`, in the same write as the block drafts, and its answer maps each provisional id to the block it made, `(created ...)`, and each piece to its id, `(pieces ...)`. Until then a provisional id names no block: asked as one by any verb that resolves an id over a view, it is answered `(error provisional-id (id \"<id>\") (reason not-committed))`, with or without `--working` (with it, a refusal of the view comes first)."))
fc824e97 (12 1793413342400 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "d9l36a5r.2o" src "instance.sexp is this copy's identity. request-index.sexp and snap/ are rebuilt from the log, and derived/ by the next supply. The working areas and draft locks are one machine's drafts. The `*.tmp-*` files are what an atomic write keeps after a failure -- except under a writer's incoming/, which the last line brings back, because a candidate kept there is evidence a replay is answered from.\n\nA `.gitignore` already in the directory keeps every byte it has: init appends only the lines it lacks, each whole and on its own line, and writes nothing when it holds them all; a line ending in one carriage return holds the line, as git reads it. supply adds its line by the same rule. A `.gitignore` that is not a regular file -- a link, a directory -- is refused before anything is written, `gitignore-not-a-file`.\n\nTrack the lock. It is zero bytes and only its existence is used, but a clone without it answers store-busy to every verb, reads included -- and init will not create it in a store that exists.\n\nAdd the store as a whole, never named files -- `git add <store>` -- because a segment rolls over after 1 MiB, and a list of named files misses the one that rolled since."))
ef17ac69 (13 1793413344244 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "8pniyrtk.8p" src "Every verb can run standalone: open the store, answer, exit. For a session that sends many verbs, serve holds the store open and answers over a unix socket. The client finds the socket by the same rule the daemon used to create it, so nothing needs to be told where it is. The daemon's answer is the CLI's answer, byte for byte; both call the same dispatcher, and no verb or answer shape exists in one and not the other, except eval, scope and collect: the daemon does not run them -- sent over the socket, they are answered unknown-verb -- and the CLI runs them in its own program (eval in core.sc as its own child). A plain command never starts a daemon: when none is listening, the CLI starts `core.sc --frame` beside itself as a child, writes it the request frame a daemon would have been sent, and reads the child's answer as it reads a daemon's (the same 32 MiB ceiling, and no answer deadline, so the request waits for the store's lock); nothing is left running after it. A daemon is started by `serve`, or by the MCP shell for the session it serves. Answered in a child, a request meets the in-process route's differences: `describe` adds no template clause, the store's lock is waited for rather than refused `store-busy` after five seconds, the store is read as it is rather than as last published, `--rev` is refused `no-published-state` and `subscribe` `needs-daemon`. `THEOURGIA_LOCAL=1` skips the socket and answers in process even when a daemon is there, for debugging. The daemon answers reads from the state it last folded, and can be one commit behind an outside change: a read that notices a record appended by another process, or a segment a git pull brought in, is still answered from that state and asks for a fresh fold, and the reads after the fold see the change. `query` and `context` are the exception: they make the publication current first. A write is never answered from that state: it is decided on the store as it stands under the lock. While a daemon serves a store, write through it.\n\nThe daemon keeps one query session across `query` and `context` requests: a request over the same publication reuses what earlier requests derived, the session is advanced when the publication moves forward, and a fresh one replaces it otherwise. Every answer is the one a fresh session would give, with one exception: a query whose budget a fresh session would exhaust may be answered, since the kept session has done some of the work already. `serve --session-size <entries>` bounds the kept session (0 keeps none). A store whose directory disappears is drained, and the daemon exits with `(exiting (reason store-gone))`.\n\nThe socket path is a symbolic link to the daemon's private socket, `<socket>.d/<pid>`, in a directory of the daemon's own whose `.endpoint` marker reserves it for that endpoint; a client connects through the link as it always has. A start reclaims what an earlier daemon of the same endpoint left; it is refused `serve-path-occupied` when the socket path or `<socket>.d` is something it does not own, `serve-path-reserved` when its socket's directory is inside another endpoint's marked directory, `serve-name-derived` (exit 1, before anything is made or locked) when its name is spelled as another endpoint's lock, `.<name>.lock` or `<name>.d.reserve`, under the case rule of its directory, and `socket-path-too-long` (exit 2, before anything is created) when the path leaves no room for the private name. README's `serve` section gives the namespace's rules, the `<socket>.d.reserve` file that serialises a reservation, and the windows it accepts.\n\n`serve --idle-exit <seconds>` makes a daemon leave by itself after that long with no connection open and no request running; an open subscription is an open connection, and 0 or no option means never. When the window runs out the daemon unlinks its public link first, so from then on a client finds no daemon and sends nothing; for the grace, `--idle-grace-ms` (default 200, at most 5000), it still takes the connections the kernel had already completed, then exits 0 with `(idle-exit (after <seconds>) (refused-accepts <n>) (callback-raised <n>))` if none came. A late connection cancels the exit: the daemon binds and publishes again, logs `(idle-resumed (socket <path>))`, and answers it once the resume is done. A resume that cannot happen drains the daemon: the late requests are answered `draining` and it exits 75 with `(idle-exit (reason resume-failed <cause>))`. Every drain waits for the listener to hand over each connection it accepted, so a connection accepted just before a drain is answered `draining`, or served when its request was already running, never closed with nothing."))
a9caf959 (14 1793413346087 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "d9l36a5r.6c" src "A change made in the store reaches a git repository, and commits made in git reach the store, through scripts rather than verbs, in `tools/`:\n\n- `edit-export` and `edit-sync` let an agent that edits files work on an export of its writer's working view: the sync turns each edited, added or removed file back into the writer's drafts, and refuses, writing nothing, when a hunk does not fall in exactly one block or a block it touches has moved since the export. The directory is folded to one name lexically, in `edit-export`, `edit-sync` and `landing-freeze --tree` alike, so `.`, `dir/.` and `dir/x/..` name the same directory and find the same sidecar beside it.\n- `drafts-dump` and `drafts-restore` keep a writer's drafts, which do not travel with a store's git; `landing-freeze` first brings every draft onto the store's applied cut -- one that is not behind is written again at the new cut, one that is behind is merged with the committed change line by line, and a new-file or delete draft at another cut is moved there when it still applies unchanged (a new file whose path no live file holds at the applied cut, a delete whose file's baseline there is still the draft's base), rewritten in place under one hold of the writer's draft lock and only while it is the version the freeze judged, so that a draft written again since, or a write that fails, leaves the entry where it was and refuses the freeze, `structural-move-refused`; when the two changes touch the same or adjacent lines, or a new file's path is taken or a deleted file is gone or changed at the applied cut, nothing is written and the freeze stops, `drafts-behind-conflict`, naming the blocks and the structural keys -- then freezes them with the working view for a landing's review; with `--tree <edit-dir>` the frozen tree must equal the tested edit directory byte for byte, dot-named names skipped, or the freeze is refused `tree-differs`. `landing-freeze --check <dir>` judges a freeze already made against the store as it is now (current, refreeze, or the conflict), and `landing-commit` commits them only if nothing the files are made of has changed since the freeze's cut, optionally tagging the landing's start. A cut moved only by records on other blocks -- a review letter, a landing's decision block, a tag -- is not a move; after a change to the files, a freeze made again rebases them.\n- `publish-landing` publishes the files a landing changed as one git commit, its message held in a landing block, after checking that the worktree's base equals the store's export at the start tag. It publishes the text projection only, what `export-code --raw` writes: a landing in which a datum library differs between the cuts is refused, `datum-not-published`, rather than published without it.\n- `import-git` absorbs a git range into the store as committed blocks and tags the result, the next landing's start; a file whose bytes are text at no end it exists at is skipped and named in the answer, and one that starts or stops being text is added or removed.\n\nREADME's chapters \"Publishing a landing\", \"Editing a working view as files\", \"Keeping drafts, and landing them at their cut\" and \"Importing a git range\" give each script's checks and refusals."))
4a142a41 (15 1793413347940 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (set "d9l36a5r.5q" src "Sessions on one machine -- of any vendor, through the command line or the MCP shell -- talk to each other through a store. There is no message verb: a message is a block of kind `doc` with `to`, the reader's actor name, and `status`, `\"unread\"` when sent and `\"read\"` once the reader has read it, and reading one's mail is a query:\n\n```\nquery '(and (field ?m \"to\" \"codex\") (field ?m \"status\" \"unread\"))'\n```\n\nA review goes through a store made for the reader. `scope <dir> --cut <cut> --roots <id> --for <actor>` makes a new store holding exactly the blocks under the roots as they were at the cut, a letter copy that names the reader, and nothing else -- the authors' notes, ledgers and earlier rounds are not in it -- and writes one letter in this store. The reader, in a fresh session pointed at `<dir>`, writes a verdict under the letter copy, with `model`, `approval`, `sandbox` and `effort`, and a finding under the verdict for each finding, each with an `about` edge to the copy it concerns. `collect <dir>` brings them back under the letter here, each `about` edge on the original block; `review-results`, run in the scoped store, and `collect-into` are the two halves it is made of. `scope` and `collect` run in the command line's own program or as the MCP shell's child, never in a daemon.\n\nA change can be reviewed before it lands. `scope --freeze <dir>` takes a landing freeze: the copies hold the drafts' bytes, a `before` section under a changed copy holds the committed text, `--packet` adds sections such as the questions, each with any number of texts, joined in packet order with one empty line between two, `--roots all` copies every top-level block as the store is now (refused `roots-all-at-another-cut` when the scope's cut is another), and no main letter is written until the landing. A candidate's results are strict: `review-results` answers `pending` until a verdict exists, and a verdict must name its sources, at least one of them a copy. After the landing, `collect --map <file>` reads the landing's mapping of provisional ids, writes the letter then (`--letter new`, once), and with `--to-writer <W>` turns each proposal -- a candidate copy whose `src` the reader changed -- into a draft for `W`, which `W` keeps, rewrites or discards; nothing is committed by it. `--home <h>` makes the scoped store under its own machine home, so a reader confined to a review directory holding both can write in it; `collect --home <h>` reads it.\n\nCodex reaches a store through the MCP shell (`codex mcp add theourgia -- <the command that starts the shell>`, with `THEOURGIA_ACTOR=codex`). `contrib/` holds what tells it about mail: `codex-hooks.json` runs `mail-hook.sh` on `PostToolUse` and `UserPromptSubmit`, which prints the reader's unread mail as the hook's answer, and exits 1 with a line when its query fails, since a failed query is not an empty mailbox; `doorbell.sh` types one line into a tmux pane when mail arrives for a reader idle at its prompt; `AGENTS.md` is the reader's side of the discipline. Claude Code reads its mail the same way: `contrib/claude-hooks.json` is the `hooks` section of its `settings.json`, running the same handler with `THEOURGIA_ACTOR=claude`.\n\nThe scoped store keeps a cooperating reader from seeing more than its roots. A reader's edits to the copies are not collected, except as the proposals of a frozen candidate's review. It does not keep out a reader that opens the main store's directory or states another actor's name: on one machine the user owns every session, and an actor is a stated name. This program speaks over a local socket and the local filesystem; a store kept in git is read on any clone, and writing one store from two machines is not built."))
a6539efa (16 1793413348420 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (del "d9l36a5r.81"))
0a988974 (17 1793413348759 "guenchi" (("8pniyrtk" . 328) ("d9l36a5r" . 289)) (put ((kind . code) (mode . datum) (lang . chez) (body define verb-table (quote ((init (usage (init ("--template" <name>) ("--template-file" <template-file>))) (description "Create a store in this directory; with --template (a built-in such as project) or --template-file, then apply that template to it.") (protocol #f) (route local)) (eval (usage (eval ("--lang" <language>) ("--cut" <cut>) ("--under" <library-id>) ("--working") ("--latest") ("--writer" <name>) ("--timeout-ms" <n>) ("--memory-bytes" <n>) ("--output-bytes" <n>) <source>)) (description "Evaluate source against the store, or against a writer's working view with --working: Scheme by default, another language with --lang, whose runner runs only where the operator has set THEOURGIA_RUNNERS=on. It runs as a child process of the caller, never in the store's server.") (protocol #f) (route child)) (insert (usage (insert ("--under" <id>) ("--after" <id>) "--title" <text> ("--text" <text>) ("--keywords" <text>) ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Add a block under a parent, with a title and optional text.") (protocol #t) (route daemon)) (set (usage (set <id> <field> <value> ("--if-unchanged" <version>) ("--based-on" <version>) ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Replace one field of one block.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (move (usage (move <id> <parent> ("--after" <id>) ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Move a block to another parent, optionally after a sibling.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (del (usage (del <id> ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Retire a block. Its history stays.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (link (usage (link <from> <rel> <to> ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Record a named relation between two blocks.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (unlink (usage (unlink <from> <rel> <to> ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Remove a named relation between two blocks.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (relation (usage (relation <name> ("--as" <kind>) ("--from" <selector>) ("--to" <selector>) ("--retire") ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Declare what a relation name does: --as one of the six relations with an effect (supersedes, refutes, depends-on, implements, verifies, conflicts-with), whose rules its edges then follow, or nothing, a listed edge with no effect. --from and --to name the blocks each end is for: a write that adds an edge whose end its selector does not match, or retypes such an end, is refused relation-endpoint. With --retire the name is a plain edge again. The same declaration again answers (ok (unchanged)) and writes nothing.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (derive (usage (derive <name> (<value>) ("--retire") ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Declare a stored relation of the store: <value> is ((head (<name> <kind> ...)) (clause (<name> <term> ...) <goal> ...) ...), the head giving every column's kind, each clause a pattern and goals in the query language (facts, tests, library relations, other stored relations, itself). A query, a rule and another stored relation may then name it. Its clauses are checked where they are used: a goal naming one contested, retired or failing its own check there is refused relation-unavailable. --retire ends it. The answer carries (affects (rule ...) (derive ...)) when the write changes what settled rules and stored relations can answer. The same value again answers (ok (unchanged)) and writes nothing.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (rule (usage (rule <name> ("--on" <kind>) ("--where" <goal>) ("--must" <goal>) ("--must-not" <goal>) ("--builtin" <name>) ("--retire") ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Declare a rule of the store: on writes to blocks of the --on kinds (repeated for several), selected by --where when given, the --must goal must have a row for the block, or the --must-not goal none; ?w stands for the block. --builtin names a built-in rule (citation-coverage); --retire ends a rule. A goal is a query goal over the stored facts; one that reads outside the log is refused. Every committed write after it is judged before anything is written; a write that fails is refused rule-violation, naming every failing pair. The same rule again answers (ok (unchanged)) and writes nothing.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (write (usage (write <block> <bytes> ("--writer" <name>) ("--based-on" <version>) ("--working-cut" <cut>) ("--working-parent-writer" <name>) ("--working-parent" <version>) ("--rebase"))) (description "Save a draft of a block in a writer's own space, without committing it.") (protocol #t) (route daemon)) (restore (usage (restore <version> ("--writer" <name>))) (description "Take an earlier version of a draft back into a writer's space.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (commit (usage (commit (<block> ...) ("--writer" <name>) ("--working-version" <block>=<version>) ("--working-new" <provisional>=<version>) ("--working-delete" <holder>=<version>) ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Install a writer's drafts into the store as one change.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (drafts (usage (drafts ("--writer" <name>))) (description "List the drafts a writer is holding.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (diagnostics (usage (diagnostics ("--writer" <name>))) (description "List the diagnostics an editor supplied for a writer's working view, by block and then by start, each at a byte range of its block's own src; one whose range cannot be mapped onto a single block's src is listed with (at unmappable).") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (discard (usage (discard <block> ("--writer" <name>))) (description "Throw away a writer's draft of a block, or a structural draft by its key (a new file's provisional id, or delete- and the holder).") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (batch (usage (batch <intents> ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Carry out several changes as one request.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (split-suggest (usage (split-suggest <file> ("--output" <review-file>) ("--symbols" <symbols-file>))) (description "Propose where a long file could be divided into blocks. With --symbols, the cuts come from an editor's list of the file's top-level symbols instead of the language's definition patterns; the answer's cuts-from says which.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (import-code (usage (import-code <dir> ("--allow-delete") ("--datum") ("--symbols" <symbols-file>) ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Read a directory of source into the store. With --datum, the whole-line ; comments directly above a form become its doc; a ; comment inside a form is dropped, and the answer warns with its line and column. A #| |# block comment, and any comment inside a datum discarded with #;, is dropped with neither. With --datum, only Scheme files are read: those the language table gives to Scheme by extension (ss, sc, scm, sls, matched exactly); every other file the directory walk returns (it does not enter a name that starts with a dot) is listed, in the order it was walked, in the answer's skipped clause, which is there only when something was skipped. A file the reader refuses is named in the refusal's path clause. Text mode, without --datum, skips a file that is not UTF-8 text or that holds a NUL byte, and lists it in the same skipped clause; it is decided by the bytes, not the name, so a source file in a legacy 8-bit encoding or in UTF-16 is skipped and listed, not imported, unless its bytes happen to be valid UTF-8 with no NUL. With --symbols, a file an editor's symbols file names is split on its first import at those symbols' ranges, every start and every end a cut, as a marked import of the same cuts would split it; a file that already carries markers follows them, and the answer lists it as symbols-ignored. A file whose cuts the scanner does not see at the top level is not imported, and the answer lists it with its refusal as symbols-refused; a file whose cuts could not be checked at all (symbols-unchecked: no language entry or suggest profile; symbols-unscanned: the scanner cannot follow it) is imported whole, as one block, and listed with its refusal as symbols-imported-whole.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (export-code (usage (export-code <dir> ("--raw") ("--datum") ("--working") ("--writer" <name>) ("--cut" <cut>))) (description "Write the store's source back out to a directory.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (def (usage (def <name> ("--under" <library>) <source> ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Define one named definition in a datum library, after its last child. A name one of the library's children already defines is refused (name-exists); def does not replace a definition.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (import-md (usage (import-md <dir> ("--allow-delete") ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Read a directory of markdown into the store.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (export-md (usage (export-md <dir> ("--with-ids") ("--working") ("--writer" <name>) ("--cut" <cut>))) (description "Write the store out as markdown.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (supply (usage (supply <kind> <file> ("--for" <writer>) ("--clear"))) (description "Keep facts an editor computed from an export-code projection -- signatures, calls, diagnostics -- in the store's directory under derived/, outside the event log. The header carries the digests of the projected files it was computed from; every file it lists is checked against the store's own re-projection, of the committed state or, with --for, of that writer's working view. A fact is used only while the blocks it depends on still project as they did. With --clear, the table the header names is removed.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (adopt (usage (adopt)) (description "Take in records that are on disk but not yet in the log.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (check (usage (check)) (description "Read the whole store and report whether it is sound.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (snapshot (usage (snapshot)) (description "Record the current state as a reduction that can be reopened quickly.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (publish (usage (publish <writer> <segment> <file> (<sha256>))) (description "Publish a segment of a writer's log.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (outline (usage (outline ("--depth" <n>) ("--with-keywords") ("--with-signatures"))) (description "List the blocks as a tree of titles.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (read (usage (read <id> ("--md") ("--recursive") ("--writer" <name>) ("--working") ("--working-info") ("--signature") ("--cut" <cut>) ("--rev"))) (description "Read one block: its fields, or its text. With --signature, the signature an editor supplied for it, of the committed store or, with --working, of the writer's working view. With --cut, the block as it was at a causal cut (a literal or a tag name): the same answer over that cut's state, or unknown-id where the block did not exist yet.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (refs (usage (refs <id>)) (description "List the relations a block takes part in.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (reach (usage (reach <id> ("--rel" <rel>) ("--depth" <n>))) (description "List the blocks a block reaches over the edges an editor supplied (calls, by default), outward, up to --depth hops (1 by default); the block itself is at depth 0.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (search (usage (search <query> ("--all") ("--all-validity"))) (description "Find blocks whose title, keywords or text match every word given.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (grep (usage (grep <pattern> ("--under" <id>) ("--all") ("--all-validity"))) (description "List the lines that contain a pattern, literally.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (whereis (usage (whereis <name> ("--all-validity"))) (description "Say where a name is defined, and which libraries carry it.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (log (usage (log (<id>))) (description "Show the changes recorded, for the store or for one block.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (tag (usage (tag (<name>) ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Name the current cut, or list the names already given.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (diff (usage (diff <cut> <cut>)) (description "Report what changed between two cuts.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (position (usage (position)) (description "Say the local log writer's last sequence, the cursor a tracked request may name: (position (<writer> . <n>)), 0 before its first record. The writer is the store's log writer, not a draft writer.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (locate (usage (locate <path> ("--writer" <name>) ("--cut" <cut>) ("--bytes" <s:e>) ("--lines" <a:b>) ("--hunk" <hunk>))) (description "Name the blocks holding a byte range, a line range or one unified-diff hunk's old side of a text file's raw bytes (what export-code --raw writes), each whole with its span: (items (block <id> <start> <end>) ...). Give one of --writer (that writer's working view) or --cut; a hunk is meaningful at the cut its diff was taken against. --hunk - reads the hunk from standard input.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (changed-files (usage (changed-files <cut> <cut>)) (description "List the files whose exported bytes or presence differ between two cuts, each as content, added or removed; a title, a level, a link or a language changes no file. Paths a case- or normalisation-insensitive volume would merge are listed as case-ambiguous or normalisation-ambiguous groups.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (conflicts (usage (conflicts)) (description "List blocks whose writers disagree.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (subscribe (usage (subscribe <stream> <rev> (<token>))) (description "Follow the store's publications: <stream> is changes, and every publication after <rev> arrives as a line naming what changed, until the stream ends. 0 starts from now; a resume names the daemon token its acceptance gave.") (protocol #f) (route stream)) (scope (usage (scope <dir> "--roots" <id> "--for" <actor> ("--cut" <cut>) ("--freeze" <dir>) ("--packet" <packet-file>) ("--home" <dir>))) (description "Make a new store at <dir> holding a letter to <actor> and a copy of each block under the roots as it was at the cut, each copy carrying origin (the block's id here) and origin-cut; the edges between copies are copied, an edge leaving the roots is dropped and listed, and a contested field is listed and not copied. The letter copy's baseline is the new store's cut after the copies. --roots is given once per root, or as all, every top-level block. One record is written here: the letter, at the top level, with to, status, cut, roots and scope. With --freeze, a landing freeze, the cut is its pinned cut, each copy the freeze has a draft of holds the draft's bytes with the committed src in a before section under it, and nothing is written here: collect-into --letter new writes the letter after the landing. --packet writes a file's sections under the letter copy. --home makes the new store under that machine home, beside it, so a reader whose sandbox holds both can write there. An existing <dir> is refused before anything is written, and so is a block or edge the write would refuse. It runs in the caller's own program -- the command line's, or the MCP shell's child -- never in a store's server.") (protocol #f) (route child)) (collect (usage (collect <dir> ("--map" <map-file>) ("--to-writer" <writer>) ("--home" <dir>))) (description "Bring a scoped store's review results back under its letter here: the blocks the letter's reviewer created in <dir>, with their settled fields, under the reviewer's name, and their about edges, to the origin of a copy or to the collected block. A candidate's results are collected with --letter new, and --map and --to-writer are passed on; --home reads <dir> under the home it was made under. A second collect writes nothing new. It runs in the caller's own program -- the command line's, or the MCP shell's child -- never in a store's server.") (protocol #f) (route child)) (review-results (usage (review-results)) (description "In a scoped store, list the review results as one datum: each live block the letter's reviewer created that is not a copy, with its parent, settled fields and the actor of each, and its about targets; a finding with no about and a verdict missing a condition field are listed. For a scope of a freeze it answers pending while there is no verdict and results-incomplete for the first field missing (a source must be a copy, the scope's own blocks listed beside them not counting), and the results carry the letter's fields and the proposals, the copies whose src the reviewer changed.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (collect-into (usage (collect-into (<letter-id>) "--results" <datum> ("--letter" <new>) ("--map" <map-file>) ("--to-writer" <writer>))) (description "Write a review-results datum under a letter: the blocks not collected before, then the about edges not present, as the reviewer's records. Answers how many were inserted, linked and already linked, and every field another actor set. A candidate's datum is refused unless complete; --letter new makes its letter, --map reads a landing's answer for the ids it gave provisional origins, and --to-writer makes each proposal a draft for that writer.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (describe (usage (describe)) (description "List the verbs, what each is for, and the writing protocol.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (commitments (usage (commitments ("--open") ("--all") ("--drifted") ("--since" <cut>) ("--under" <id>))) (description "List the decisions still owed: by default those neither implemented (an incoming implements edge) nor marked done or dropped; with --all every decision. --drifted keeps those with an implementation whose latest change was seen neither by its implements link nor by the decision's latest edit.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (tasks (usage (tasks ("--status" <status>) ("--batch" <batch>) ("--under" <id>))) (description "List the tasks: by default those under the root the store's template names for tasks, else every task; each with its status (todo, doing, done or dropped), its batch, what it implements, and (unlinked) when it implements no live decision.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (template (usage (template <action> (<name>) ("--file" <template-file>) ("--premises" <datum>))) (description "Apply a template to this store (apply <name>, or apply --file <template-file>): create the template block and each document root the store lacks, changing nothing that exists. Or print the store's template (export).") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (names (usage (names <id>)) (description "Which names a code block uses, as its stored code shows them, and which libraries a library block imports. A datum block's body is walked as data: a symbol is a use unless something in the form binds it, and an unknown macro's operands count as uses. A text block of a language with a reader (Scheme's) is read as forms first, each walked as a datum block's body is, so comments and strings are not names; when any part of it does not read, the whole block falls back to the tokens, as every other text block's uses are: every token its language's identifier pattern matches, comments and strings included. No name is resolved.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (uses (usage (uses <name> ("--under" <id>))) (description "The live code blocks that use a name, compared whole and exactly, one row per block with its library and mode; with --under, those inside that block. It is not find-references: a use is listed whether or not anything defines the name, and a text block that defines the name also lists it.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (query (usage (query (<goal>) ("--relations"))) (description "Answer one goal of the query language over the committed state: every binding of its variables, one row each, sorted by their bytes, with a digest of the rows. A goal is a fact relation, a rule of the library, a test, or (and <goal> ...). --relations lists the fact relations and the rules.") (protocol #f) (route daemon)) (context (usage (context "--for" <id> "--budget" <tokens> ("--names" <name>) ... ("--all-validity") ("--working"))) (description "The material to read before working on a block, within a budget of tokens. What must be shown is the answer of five queries, each block placed once: for, constraints, evidence or to-verify by its validity and class; what else fits follows in a stated order (counterexamples, background). The receipt covers every block that must be shown, whether it fitted or not, and commit --premises takes it back; under --working, the premises of a drafted block are the overlay's versions and are not handed to a commit. A name with more than two definitions left shows two and says so in folded. --names <name>, repeated, enters the definitions of each given name in the block's scope first and says in asked which are defined only in hard blocks or nowhere; --budget min+<t> is the minimum the request needs plus t. --working answers over the request writer's working view (its drafts over the committed state at the drafts' cut) and says so in (working <writer>) after for.") (protocol #f) (route daemon))))) (doc . "") (parent . "8pniyrtk.2") (ord . 121))))
